\par\par \b \uc2 Marketmind: Core beliefs\b0\par\par\uc2 634 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2022\par 12:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A look at the day ahead in U.S. and global markets from \uc2 Mike Dolan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 World markets are unlikely to catch a break until central banks see some convincing progress on reining in inflation - and Thursday is a huge moment in that fight despite myriad tensions elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 Headline U.S. consumer price inflation is expected to have slipped back two tenths of a percentage point to a still-bruising 8.1% in September as the Labor Department gets set to publish its latest report.\par \par \uc2 But it was creeping annual growth in August of underlying "core" prices that exclude energy and food that sent markets into tailspin last month - fearing, correctly, that it would push \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  hawkishness into overdrive.\par \par \uc2 And that core inflation rate is forecast to have higher again in September to 6.5% from 6.3%.\par \par \uc2 The impact of the last CPI report on Sept. 13 has been pretty clear - as futures markets have pushed the implied peak or "terminal" rate for the Fed's interest rate cycle up almost 75 basis points to about 4.7% since then. Ten-year Treasury yields have risen about 60bp to just under 4%, the S&P500 has lost more than 10% and the dollar has boomed almost 5%.\par \par \uc2 The readout of last month's Fed policy meeting showed officials pushing for interest rates at more restrictive levels and maintaining them there for some time to defuse "broad-based and unacceptably high" inflation.\par \par \uc2 There was an eerie market calm of sorts before Thursday's release, although a VIX index of implied U.S. equity volatility that's in excess of 33 points reveals the level of simmering tension. However, even battered British gilts facing a blizzard of domestic problems managed to steady up on Thursday too - nervously awaiting the end of direct \uc2 Bank of England\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday.\par \par \uc2 With Germany confirming its annual September inflation rate at a whopping 10.9% on Thursday, European central bankers in Washington for the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  meeting were just as hawkish as the Fed\par \par \uc2 And that's left Japan's ongoing monetary easing as an outlier and the yen back on the slide to levels that prompted solo \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support it over the past month. Worryingly for Tokyo, the latest G7 finance ministers statement mentioned nothing about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market ructions.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2  said he told his G7 and G20 counterparts that Japan was "deeply worried about sharply rising volatility" in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and that Japan stands ready to take "decisive" action against rapid moves.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the \uc2 International Energy Agency\uc2  said on Thursday that OPEC+'s decision to cut in output has driven up prices and could push the global economy into recession - while Saudi Arabia claimed the decision was not political.\par \par \uc2 With the start of the U.S. earnings season underway later with releases from \uc2 BlackRock\uc2  and others, Taiwanese chipmaker \uc2 TSMC\uc2  said cut its annual investment budget by at least 10% for 2022 and struck a more cautious note than usual on upcoming demand.\par \par \uc2 Key developments that should provide more direction to U.S. markets later on Thursday:\par \par \uc2 * U.S. Sept Consumer Price Index, weekly jobless claims\par \par \uc2 * G20 finance ministers and central bankers meet at annual \uc2 IMF\uc2 /\uc2 World Bank\uc2  meeting in Washington\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Atlanta\uc2  President \uc2 Raphael Bostic\uc2  speaks in Atlanta\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Vice President \uc2 Luis de Guindos\uc2  speaks in Frankfurt\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Bank of England Financial Policy Committee\uc2  director Sarah Breedon speaks in London. \uc2 BoE\uc2  monetary policymaker \uc2 Catherine Mann\uc2  speak in Washington\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  auctions 30-year bonds\par \par \uc2 * U.S. corporate earnings: \uc2 BlackRock\uc2 , Domino's Pizza, \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2 , Walgreen's Boots Alliance, \uc2 Fastenal\par \par \uc2 (By \uc2 Mike Dolan\uc2 , editing by David Evans mike.dolan@thomsonreuters.com. Twitter: @reutersMikeD) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-13T10:03:38.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221013eiad01jeh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
