light22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8226\'20\'22 Dollar typically climbs against the yen in October \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/JPY's log correlation with USD/JPY is high, 60-day measure above +0.50 GBP/USD up to threaten 1.11 as volatility \u8226\'20\'22 Cable met headwind just shy of 1.11 after extending north from 1.0955 \u8226\'20\'22 1.0955 was London morning low. 1.0925-1.1060 was Asia range \u8226\'20\'22 Tuesday's high was 1.1180 -- before GBP fell on Bailey comments \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 BoE\uc2  gilt purchases will end on Oct 14-spokesperson \u8226\'20\'22 British pension funds press \uc2 BoE\uc2  to extend gilt buys amid cash scramble \u8226\'20\'22 UK economy on brink of recession AUD/USD holds below 0.63 after lift on China stocks rebound \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD met headwind pre-0.63 after eliciting boost from China stock rebound \u8226\'20\'22 The AUD is often treated as a China proxy \u8226\'20\'22 0.6240 was new 30-month low in Asia (before China stocks rebounded sharply) \u8226\'20\'22 Drop to 0.6240 influenced by iron ore fall to two-week low \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance levels beyond 0.6300 include 0.6346 (Tuesday's high) and 0.6379 \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/NZD hit one-month low of 1.1185 at 0759 GMT (1.1496 was high last week) \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders should expect a bigger EUR/USD slump \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders should expect a bigger EUR/USD slump in coming sessions, as the euro's downside remains vulnerable due to fundamental and technical factors. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are fixated on Thursday's U.S. consumer price report. The U.S. dollar will remain underpinned as red-hot inflation is expected. A rising greenback would continue to exert downward pressure on the EUR/USD. EUR/USD's has registered two daily closes under the 0.9708 Fibo, a 61.8% retrace of the 0.9528 to 0.9999 (September to October) rise, on Monday and Tuesday. That increases the odds for bigger losses well below the 0.9639 Fibo, 76.4% retrace of the same rise. EUR/USD's "bull trap" last week above the 0.9942 Fibo, a 61.8% retrace of the 1.0198 to 0.9528 (EBS) drop, weighs heavily on the market. A bull trap is set when a market breaks above a key technical level but subsequently reverses. That is usually a bearish sign. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3et2reD"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  Today's inflation data may be as important as tomorrow's U.S. PPI data, which will give traders some indication of future trends, will be released at 1230 GMT on Wednesday ahead of Thursday's CPI report, which will confirm the level of inflation in September. Although PPI final demand is seen dropping to 8.4% year-on-year - the lowest since August 2021 - prices are seen rising 0.2% mm in September. Unlike CPI, which has barely dropped from 2022's 9.1% year-on-year peak, PPI has dropped more substantially from 11.3% in June. Core CPI is seen rising to 6.5% year-on-year, while the headline measure of inflation is expected to dip to 8.1% year-on-year, which is more than four times greater than the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's target. If forecasts are met traders should expect policymakers to beat their hawkish drum hard, underpinning the dollar and undermining stocks. Unfortunately, falling producer prices stem from the growing risk of recession. Light at the end of the tunnel for inflation probably means that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  deemed safe will perform more strongly when economies slow. The dollar, which has soared this year, should rise further. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3Mt6K6y"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3Eva1QK"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-15T11:26:01.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221012eiac02531\par }\page {\par\