\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar at 24-year peak to yen after U.S. yields jump; sterling choppy\b0\par\par\uc2 803 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2022\par 06:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 12 (Reuters) - The dollar scaled fresh 24-year heights on the yen on Wednesday, breaching levels that prompted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese officials last month, as traders braced for U.S. inflation data and its implications for further Federal Reserve rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 Sterling slipped to a new two-week trough after \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  Governor Andrew Bailey reiterated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will end its emergency bond-buying program on Friday and told pension fund managers to finish rebalancing their positions within that time frame.\par \par \uc2 However, the pound rebounded slightly after a report in the \uc2 Financial Times\uc2  said the \uc2 BoE\uc2  has siganlled privately to lenders that it's prepared to prolong its bond purchases.\par \par \uc2 The risk-sensitive Australian dollar sank to a 2 1/2-year low.\par \par \uc2 The dollar strengthened 0.22% to 146.18 yen in Asian trading, after pushing as high as 146.39 for the first time since August 1998.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is particularly sensitive to the gap between U.S. and Japanese long-term bond yields. The benchmark 10-year Treasury yield jumped to the cusp of a 14-year high overnight at 4.006%, while the equivalent Japanese government bond yield is pinned near zero by the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities staged their first yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 1998 on Sept. 22, when the yen tumbled to as low as 145.90 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Chief Cabinet Secretary \uc2 Hirokazu Matsuno\uc2  said at a regular media briefing on Wednesday that officials remain ready to take appropriate steps to counter excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves.\par \par \uc2 "Given the overriding strong dollar trend in place, it's possible that instead of defending the yen at a particular level, the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  would try to slow down the pace of the dollar-yen's rise by defending at a higher level" than previously, said Alvin Tan, head of Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tan, who expects Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken to 150 per dollar by the turn of the year, added: "However, the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  is also mindful of the rising volatility at the global macro level, (which) is now a bigger driver for potential further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  than the volatility in any individual \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BOJ\uc2  is a major outlier among developed-nation central banks, committing to maintaining its massive bond-buying stimulus even as global policymakers have embarked on a wave of tightening to curb inflation.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar index - which measures the greenback against a basket of six major peers, including the yen, sterling and the euro - edged 0.08% higher to 113.43, after earlier touching the highest since Sept. 29 at 113.59.\par \par \uc2 The euro slumped to its weakest since Sept. 29 overnight at $0.9670 and remained not far from that level, trading 0.08% lower than Tuesday's close at $0.96975.\par \par \uc2 Worries that continued aggressive policy tightening by the Fed and most of its peers will lead the global economy into recession have been a major driver of risk sentiment over recent months.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  warned on Tuesday that countries representing a third of world output could be in recession next year, even as it urged central banks to keep up their fight against inflation.\par \par \uc2 Recent strong U.S. labour market reports have scuppered hopes among some market participants that Fed policymakers may slow the pace of rate hikes into year-end.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. consumer price report, due on Thursday, could be a flashpoint for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility, and a sharp move higher in dollar-yen could become a trigger for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Joseph Capurso\uc2 , a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 "But we maintain any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 -induced moves in USD/JPY will be unwound within a few weeks," Capurso wrote in a note to clients.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, sterling which earlier touched $1.0925, marking a fresh low since Sept. 29, bounced 0.4% to $1.1008 after the FT report.\par \par \uc2 Gilt yields had soared earlier on Tuesday following the \uc2 BoE\uc2  governor's comments, lifting yields in the U.S. and elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 "GBP remains at risk of sudden drops because of uncertainty about government debt sustainability and the dislocation in UK pension (superannuation) funds that has spilled over into \uc2 UK government\uc2  bond market," Capurso said.\par \par \uc2 The euro slumped to its weakest since Sept. 29 overnight at $0.9670 and remained not far from that level, trading 0.08% lower than Tuesday's close at $0.96975.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie sank as low as $0.62395, a level last seen in April 2020, and last traded 0.28% weaker at $0.62555.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Kevin Buckland and Georgina Lee; Additional reporting by Vidya Ranganathan Editing by Shri Navaratnam) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-12T05:18:52.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221012eiac00ho5\par }\page {\