\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar at 24-year peak to yen as U.S. yields jump; sterling on the ropes\b0\par\par\uc2 665 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Oktober 2022\par 03:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 12 (Reuters) - The dollar rose to a fresh 24-year high against the yen on Wednesday, moving above levels that prompted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese officials last month, as traders braced for U.S. inflation data and its impact on further Federal Reserve rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 Sterling slipped to a new two-week trough after \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  Governor Andrew Bailey reiterated that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will end its emergency bond-buying program on Friday and told pension fund managers to finish rebalancing their positions within that time frame.\par \par \uc2 The risk-sensitive Australian dollar sank to a 2 1/2-year low.\par \par \uc2 The dollar strengthened 0.3% to 146.30 yen in Asian trading, after pushing as high as 146.35, a level not seen since August 1998.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is particularly sensitive to the gap between U.S. and Japanese long-term bond yields. The benchmark 10-year Treasury yield jumped to the cusp of a 14-year high overnight at 4.006%, while the equivalent Japanese government bond yield is pinned near zero by the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Japanese officials staged their first yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 1998 on Sept. 22, when the yen tumbled to as low as 145.90 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2  said on Wednesday that authorities will take necessary steps in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if needed, adding that what was important was the speed of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements, Jiji Press news agency reported.\par \par \uc2 "It is the speed of change rather than the level that will trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , (meaning) USD/JPY may rise past its pre-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  of 145.9 without triggering the BoJ to step in if the rise occurs gradually," \uc2 Joseph Capurso\uc2 , a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2 , wrote in a client note.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the U.S. consumer price report on Thursday could trigger the kind of sharp move that would prompt another \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Capurso added, "but we maintain any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 -induced moves in USD/JPY will be unwound within a few weeks."\par \par \uc2 The Fed has signalled it will continue its aggressive tightening campaign to rein in inflation, and recent strong U.S. labour market reports have scuppered hopes among some market participants that policymakers may slow the pace of rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 Cleveland Fed\uc2  President \uc2 Loretta Mester\uc2  backed that view on Tuesday, saying the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has yet to get surging inflation under control and will need to press forward with rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar index - which measures the greenback against a basket of six major peers, including the yen, sterling and the euro - edged 0.16% higher to 113.52, after touching the highest since Sept. 29 at 113.54.\par \par \uc2 Sterling slipped 0.13% to $1.0947, and earlier touched $1.09385, marking a fresh low since Sept. 29, following the comments by the \uc2 BoE\uc2  governor.\par \par \uc2 Gilt yields soared on Tuesday, lifting yields in the U.S. and elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 The euro slumped to its weakest since Sept. 29 overnight at $0.9670 and remained not far from that level, trading 0.17% lower than Tuesday's close at $0.96885.\par \par \uc2 Worries that continued aggressive policy tightening by the Fed and most of its peers will lead the global economy into recession continues to weigh on risk sentiment.\par \par \uc2 Investors will be seeking for further clues when inflation data for September is released on Wednesday for prices that sellers get for their products, and on Thursday for prices consumers pay for purchases.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie sank as low as $0.62395, a level last seen in April 2020, and last traded 0.5% weaker at $0.62415. The New Zealand dollar was 0.21% lower at $0.5570, approaching the previous day's low of $0.5536, a level not visited since March 2020.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Kevin Buckland Editing by Shri Navaratnam) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-12T02:49:48.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221012eiac007k9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
