ger-term technicals highlighting bearish potential. Monthly RSI is oversold but is falling and not diverging, which implies downside momentum remains and October's monthly inverted hammer candle implies bears remain in charge. A break of key support near 0.9500 should lead to a test of the 0.9000 area. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3fXMduG"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  Sterling bounces by 1.10 on \uc2 BoE\uc2  props, but remains at risk Sterling rose on Tuesday, pausing its recent slide after the \uc2 BoE\uc2  added more support to the gilts market, but the day's low of 1.0999 may not hold as the UK's combination of inflation and rate-hike vulnerability will challenge even the most astute policymakers. Sterling rebounded to a 0.65% gain after \uc2 BoE\uc2  aid pulled most gilts yields lower, signaling less financial distress, though 30-year gilts yields were still up 8bp. The \uc2 BoE\uc2  is priced to hike rates an additional 360bp or so by May for a 5.8% peak, kicked off by very aggressive 100bp hikes in November and December. It's also scheduled to end its bond-buying on Friday and begin selling its QE holdings on Oct. 31, which could result in further financial market disruptions and sterling losses. Some are hoping the \uc2 British government\uc2 's Oct. 31 budget plans will reduce fiscal concerns that helped trigger the breakdown in gilts and sterling's drop to record lows. Before then, staying above 1.1000 may depend on the \uc2 BoE\uc2  extending its market supports and U.S. economic data dimming dollar demand. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3emXbJz"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  Chart of the Day (USD/JPY) USD/JPY appears headed for new 24-year highs and possibly more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  unless U.S. CPI and retail sales data this week dim Fed tightening expectations.It's high of 145.86 high Tuesday sits just below September's 24-year peak at 145.90 that triggered Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a one-day dive to 140.31. With 2-year Treasury-JGB yield spreads near their highest since August 2006, the dollar should remain in demand while the BoJ remains accommodative and disinclined to allow the recently untraded 10-year JGBs surpass its 25bp cap. That leaves further \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk and long-term technical resistance to slow USD/JPY's uptrend, unless U.S. economic data softens. If Thursday's U.S. core CPI rises to 6.5% from 6.3% and Friday's retail sales are near the 0.2% monthly increase forecast, USD/JPY should clear 145.90-146.00 and drive toward 1998's 147.64 EBS peak. Given 1998's peak was the highest since 1990, another round of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend it looks likely, along with spec long profit-taking due to long-term technical hurdles in the 147.08-9.38 range. This week's largest options expiries are Thursday's $1.0bln at 147.25 for an interim target. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3CO6zPI"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-14T19:23:07.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221011eiab03vi9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
