\par\par \b \uc2 Japanese Yen Stabilizes For Now But Faces Further Losses -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,482 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2022\par 12:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1054 GMT - The recent stability of the Japanese yen against the dollar after the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  is less favorable than it seems, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. The dollar has weakened against most \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of industrialised nations since last week, which isn't reflected in USD/JPY, \uc2 Commerzbank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst Thu Lan Nguyen says in a note. "That also means: as soon as the dollar is able to appreciate again-- which we expect--upside pressure on USD/JPY is also likely to rise again." USD/JPY last trades up 0.1% at 144.755. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1039 GMT - German factory orders contracted by 2.4% on month in August, but the fall was driven by big-ticket orders, with core orders up 0.8% over the month, Oxford Economics' senior economist Katharina Koenz says in a note. Core orders remained resilient thanks to solid capital goods, which benefited from easing supply bottlenecks and robust foreign demand, the economist explains. Although the resilience in German core factory orders over the summer is encouraging, surveys suggest that the resilience in orders is unlikely to last, Koenz warns. But sky-high backlogs should soften the hit to production once orders start to weaken, Koenz says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1039 GMT - Spain's GDP is expected to grow by 4.4% in 2022, according to \uc2 BBVA Research\uc2  . The Spanish bank has raised its forecast for this year from 4.1%, thanks to the strong consumption of non-residents, which accelerated economic growth in the second quarter, economists at \uc2 BBVA\uc2  say in a note. Goods exports also showed strength despite higher production costs and supply-chain problems, \uc2 BBVA\uc2  says. However, the Spanish bank has noted a deterioration of the economic recovery in the last months, with the economy struggling to grow in the third quarter. Economists say even moderate drops in GDP could be registered in the coming quarter as uncertainties rise. \uc2 BBVA\uc2  has significantly cut its growth forecast for 2023 to 1.0%, from 1.8% previously. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1032 GMT - The \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  's latest survey on U.K. business inflation expectations confirm the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will need to raise interest rates aggressively at its next policy meeting on November 3, Monex Europe says. "This view is similarly shared by short-term interest rate markets, with overnight index swaps for the Bank's upcoming two meetings rising by 10 basis points on the day to an implied year-end rate of 4.33%," Monex \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist \uc2 Simon Harvey\uc2  says in a note. U.K. business expectations for consumer price inflation in one year's time rose to 9.5% in September from 8.4% in August, the BOE survey showed Thursday. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1015 GMT - Emissions of carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gases are the main issue impacting enterprise value across sectors, say \uc2 Citi\uc2  analysts in a research note. The analysts mapped 3,000 stocks against the sustainability issues considered most financially relevant as per the \uc2 Sustainability Accounting Standards Board\uc2  's standards. Overall, emissions are the ESG issue that stand out as being most important for consideration by investors, \uc2 Citi\uc2  says. However, there remains nuance across sectors, it notes, noting that social issues are generally deemed more important in the retail space compared with environmental issues. (maitane.sardon@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0958 GMT - The Polish zloty faces further declines after the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left is benchmark interest rate unchanged at 6.75% Wednesday, defying market expectations for a 25 basis-point rise, ING says. "The interest rate differential fell by 20bp during yesterday's session alone and we expect today's press conference by [\uc2 Poland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  ] governor \uc2 Adam Glapinski\uc2  to confirm the dovish tone and increase pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," ING analyst Frantisek Taborsky says in a note. Moreover, the weak global outlook and rising gas prices are negative for the Central and Eastern European region, he says. EUR/PLN rises 0.6% to 4.8465.(renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0943 GMT - Rising yields in the gilts market has made hedging interest rate risk "prohibitively expensive" say \uc2 ABN AMRO\uc2  fixed income strategists Shanawaz Bhimji and Larissa de Barros Fritz in a note. Yields on the ten-year gilts have risen by 100 basis points to 4.073% in the last month after the government announced a large and unfunded fiscal plan. U.K. swap rates, which normally trade close to gilt yields with corresponding maturities, have gone up as yields on \uc2 U.K. government\uc2  bonds rise. "The general idea we are getting from talking to investors is that hedging has become prohibitively expensive following the surge in the swap rates," the strategists say in a note. (miriam.mukuru@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0932 GMT - The euro may struggle to recover materially given the prospect of a challenging winter in Europe amid an energy crisis, ING says. A lasting recovery in EUR/USD to levels above parity might only be driven by markets buying more aggressively into expectations for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to pivot on its tight policy stance and/or other factors offering sustained support to risk assets, ING analyst \uc2 Francesco Pesole\uc2  says in a note. EUR/USD could stay pressured into a 0.90-0.95 range for the rest of 2022, he says. "The new pack of sanctions by the EU likely suggest a prolonged stand-off with Russia, while markets await more details on the proposed oil price cap." EUR/USD rises 0.1% to 0.9893.(renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0917 GMT - German industrial orders resumed their downward trend due to a lack of bulk tickets in August after a temporary respite in July, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  's chief economist for Germany \uc2 Stefan Schilbe\uc2  says in a note. "When taking into account both the substantial revision of the July data and the fact that the weak August number mainly reflected a lack of big ticket orders, the industrial orders report for August wasn't that bad," Schilbe says. However, the weakness of domestic demand particularly for capital goods is worrying and hints at an abrupt decline of business fixed-investment spending, the economist says. Furthermore, the perspectives for future demand have deteriorated further, according to the latest sentiment indicators, Schilbe says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0859 GMT - Sterling should resume its weakening trend following its recent rebound after U.K. Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  dampened hopes for further changes to the government's fiscal policy, \uc2 MUFG Bank\uc2  says. Truss on Wednesday confirmed a U-turn on plans to scrap the highest rate of income tax but said the government would press ahead with other planned tax cuts. Without further fiscal tightening, the U.K. faces even higher government bond yields which undermines the already weak economic growth outlook, \uc2 MUFG\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst \uc2 Lee Hardman\uc2  says in a note. "Now the dust is beginning to settle after the pound's recent volatile price action, we expect the bearish trend to resume." GBP/USD is steady at 1.1305, while EUR/GBP gains 0.3% to 0.8759. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0857 GMT - Manufacturing in Spain recovered somewhat midway through the third quarter, from a poor start to the quarter, Pantheon Macroeconomics' chief eurozone economist \uc2 Claus Vistesen\uc2  says in a note. Industrial production in Spain increased by 5.5% year-on-year and by 0.4% month-on-month in August. The headline was lifted almost exclusively by a 5.3% month-to-month increase in production of capital goods, due to a 30.2% leap in output of motor vehicles, the economist says. Vistesen says the surveys suggest the upturn in Spanish industry won't continue. The quarter-on-quarter change if production was unchanged in September--the carry-over for the quarter--is a 0.9% drop, according to Pantheon. Survey data point to downside risks for month-on-month output in the coming months, Vistesen warns. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0818 GMT - The decline in German manufacturing orders is attributable to a significant drop in large-scale orders and if this is excluded, order intake actually increased month, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  senior economist \uc2 Ralph Solveen\uc2  says in a note. "This means that the slight upward trend observed since the spring has continued for this core indicator," he says. However, a look at the individual sectors shows that this is due solely to a recovery in orders for cars and auto parts, while in all other sectors, orders have fallen since April, the economist says. "However, the decisive factors for the development of production aren't the current order intake anyway, but the availability of intermediate products and the development of energy prices," Solveen says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 06, 2022 06:54 ET (10:54 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020221006eia60018l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
