\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-World central banks caught in the Fed's slipstream\b0\par\par\uc2 914 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 Oktober 2022\par 13:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Central banks forced to follow Fed or face backlash\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 UK, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , emerging countries \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem rout\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Prospects of coordination are slim\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Investors hope for 'Fed pivot'\par \par \uc2 By Francesco Canepa\par \par \uc2 FRANKFURT, Oct 6 (Reuters) - The world's central bankers are caught up in a race to curb inflation that only the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  can stop.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has embarked on its most aggressive policy tightening cycle for four decades, raising interest rates by 3 percentage points since January to slow runaway inflation.\par \par \uc2 That has left policymakers elsewhere with a tough choice: keep up with the Fed at the risk of hurting your own economy or watch your \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bonds collapse as investors switch to dollars.\par \par \uc2 "There is a growing risk that central banks will err on the side of caution by overtightening," Capital Economics economist \uc2 Jennifer McKeown\uc2  said. "The risk is that rate hikes beyond our expectations prompt an even deeper downturn."\par \par \uc2 Central bankers and finance chiefs, who will meet in Washington next week, are mostly fighting inflation driven by factors including energy prices and trade supply snags.\par \par \uc2 But few economies can stomach the diet of rate hikes the Fed has adopted to cool overheated domestic demand - largely the result of massive pandemic-era U.S. stimulus that the rest of the world couldn't match.\par \par \uc2 Responses have varied, with South Korea pledging to follow the Fed, belated but robust rate hikes in the euro zone despite a looming recession, and market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Britain to stem bleeding in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bonds.\par \par \uc2 But they all face the same problem: there is less money to go around since the Fed turned off the taps, making investors impatient with profligate governments, stubborn central banks or lacklustre growth.\par \par \uc2 Data from the United States, the euro zone, China and Japan shows the amount of money in circulation has fallen.\par \par \uc2 That has long been a harbinger of trouble for poorer economies that rely on foreign capital, and central bankers in the Philippines and Mexico have been clear about the impact of the Fed's actions on their own stances.\par \par \uc2 But it is an unwelcome throwback for central bankers in richer countries, who had thought the resilience of their economies and their own reputations as inflation fighters would cushion the effects of U.S. monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 What's worse, worldwide rate hikes reinforce each other by depressing trade and markets, raising the risk of a global recession - as the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  has warned.\par \par \uc2 The damage has already become visible in financial markets, where shares and bonds have fallen sharply, leaving investors hoping the Fed will change course.\par \par \uc2 "Only the Fed can print the dollars necessary to fix the problem quickly," \uc2 Mike Wilson\uc2 , chief investment officer at \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 , said in a podcast.\par \par \uc2 "A Fed pivot is likely at some point given the trajectory of global U.S. dollar money supply. However, the timing is uncertain."\par \par \uc2 Fed policymakers have this week restated their focus on taming inflation.\par \par \uc2 Rather than competing with each other, economist Maurice Obstfeld has suggested central bankers should team up to pursue a "gentler tightening path".\par \par \uc2 This happened during the financial crisis, when central banks acted together to stabilise markets, and with 1985's Plaza Accord, agreed by the top five developed economies to depreciate the dollar.\par \par \uc2 But with the Fed happy for a strong dollar to bring down import prices and few signs of a political backlash against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation, the chances of a repeat are low.\par \par \uc2 "I think it's unlikely at the current juncture to a large extent because it's not in the U.S.'s interest to participate in such a move," said Kamakshya Trivedi, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rates and emerging market strategy at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Fed chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  said recently there was no "coordination" among central banks but that he and his colleagues were "very aware of what's going on in other economies".\par \par \uc2 MARKET \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Instead, governments and central banks must bear alone the cost of market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and shield their financial systems from instability.\par \par \uc2 Droves of emerging economies, including Chile, the Czech Republic and India, have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, where volatility soared around 50% in two months, according to a widely watched \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  index.\par \par \uc2 But richer countries are stepping in too.\par \par \uc2 Japan has started buying the yen for the first time since 1998 after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was pummelled by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to keep rates at zero.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  last week bought gilts to help shield pension schemes from market ire at government tax-cutting plans.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ECB\uc2  has meanwhile unveiled an emergency scheme to cap any bond yields of the euro zone's 19 member countries it feels are rising too fast.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said none of these measures was likely to work unless the Fed stops raising rates, however - and for some, such actions are a sign of looming capitulation to market pressures.\par \par \uc2 "If central banks are not yet waving the white flag, it has been (unfurled)," CrossBorderCapital, a market consultancy, said in a note. "The list of policymakers using some form of yield curve control is getting longer by the day."\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Sriring Orathai, Cynthia Kim, Swati Shetye, Karen Lema, Gayatri Suroyo, Stella Qiu and Tommy Wilkes; Graphics by Vincent Flasseur; Editing by Mark John and Catherine Evans)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-9T11:20:57.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221006eia601xwp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
