\par\par \b \uc2 BUZZ-U.S.  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Markets Open - October 4\b0\par\par\uc2 1,065 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Oktober 2022\par 12:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dollar relaxes its grip as U.S. yields fall\par \par \uc2 \f2               \par               \par               \par               \par Market briefs\par \par               \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD 0.6%, USD/JPY 0.13%, GBP/USD 0.39%, EUR/GBP 0.22% \u8226\'20\'22 DXY -0.48%, DAX 2.93%, FTSE 1.72%, Brent 0.89%, Gold 0.5% \u8226\'20\'22 Euro zone Aug producer prices rise slightly more than expected \u8226\'20\'22 EZ Aug Producer Prices MM 5.0%, 4.9% f'cast, 4.0% prev; YY 43.3%, 43.1% f'cast, 37.9% prev, 38.0% r'vsd \u8226\'20\'22 UK parliament committee says fiscal plan will come this month \u8226\'20\'22 Spain's September jobless rises 0.6% from August to 2.94 Mln \u8226\'20\'22 North Korea conducts longest-range missile test yet over Japan \u8226\'20\'22 Oil prices edge up ahead of OPEC+ meeting to discuss supply cuts Looking Ahead - Economic Data (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 12:55 US 1 Oct, w/e Redbook YY 11.0% prev \u8226\'20\'22 14:00 US Aug Factory Orders MM 0.0% f'cast, -1.0% prev \u8226\'20\'22 14:00 Aug JOLTS Job Openings 10.775 mln f\u8217\'20\'19cast, 11.239 mln prev Looking Ahead - Events, Other Releases (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 12:00 \uc2 ECB\uc2 's Enria speaks at the FMA Supervisory Conference 2022 \u8226\'20\'22 13:00 Fed's Logan gives welcome remarks before the 2022 Technology-Enabled Disruption (TED) conference \u8226\'20\'22 13:00 Fed's Williams gives opening and closing remarks in a webinar hosted by the \uc2 NY Fed\uc2  \u8226\'20\'22 13:15 Fed's Mester speaks on "Payments" before the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago\uc2  Payments Symposium \u8226\'20\'22 15:00 \uc2 ECB\uc2 's Lagarde speaks with students at an event organised by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Cyprus \u8226\'20\'22 15:45 Fed's Jefferson speaks before the 2022 Technology-Enabled Disruption (TED) conference \u8226\'20\'22 17:00 Fed's Daly participates in fireside chat before hybrid event at \uc2 the Council on Foreign Relations\uc2  Commentary and Analysis EUR/USD rises towards more challenging levels \u8226\'20\'22 Oct 4 sees EUR/USD rise 0.9807-0.9896 EBS, last 0.9870 \u8226\'20\'22 Pair approaching Sep 22 peak at 0.9908 \u8226\'20\'22 Mid-point of 20-Day Bollinger bands is 0.9887 \u8226\'20\'22 The 50% retracement of Sep 12-26 drop from 1.0198 to 0.9528 is 0.9863 \u8226\'20\'22 Traders sold ten billion dollars to establish bet EUR/USD rises \u8226\'20\'22 Series of rising daily lows may encourage more bullish betting USD/JPY likely to soar due to a number of factors \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY technical outlook remains very bullish \u8226\'20\'22 Dollar typically climbs against the yen in October \u8226\'20\'22 Strong rebound in stocks are underpinning USD/JPY \u8226\'20\'22 In times of risk appetite, funds usually flow out of the safe-haven yen \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY sees a 144.42-144.91 range so far, on Tuesday, according to EBS data \u8226\'20\'22 It's far from certain recent \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will succeed \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/JPY's log correlation with USD/JPY is high, 60-day measure above +0.50 GBP/USD supported by 1.1333 after retreat from 2-week peak \u8226\'20\'22 Cable elicited support around 1.1333 after retreat from 1.1428 (2-week peak) \u8226\'20\'22 1.1333 was Monday's high (after \uc2 UK government\uc2 's tax U-turn boosted GBP) \u8226\'20\'22 Ascent to 1.1428 spurred by rise in risk appetite and UK fiscal plan news \u8226\'20\'22 TSC confirms UK fiscal plan will come this month \u8226\'20\'22 Fiscal plan details may impact size of \uc2 BoE\uc2  rate hike on Nov 3 \u8226\'20\'22 Truss refuses to rule out welfare cuts to fund economic plan AUD/USD drops back towards 25 bps \uc2 RBA\uc2  hike-spurred low \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD has fallen over half-a-cent since hitting 0.6547 at 0649 GMT \u8226\'20\'22 0.6547 was highest level since Sept 23 (0.6537 was last week's high) \u8226\'20\'22 Ascent to 0.6547 fuelled by rise in risk appetite (AUD is risk-sensitive) \u8226\'20\'22 0.6451 was Asian session low, following \uc2 RBA\uc2 's 25 bps rate hike \u8226\'20\'22 Most economists expected the \uc2 RBA\uc2  to raise rates by 50 bps \u8226\'20\'22 Nikkei closed up 3%, its biggest daily gain since March Will the pound's pride precede another fall? Sterling's rapid rise from the historic low it hit at the start of last week is raising questions about how high it can go, or whether its rebound will precede another material fall. GBP/USD took the cumulative size of its ascent from last week's low of 1.0327 to 11 cents on Tuesday. The pound has benefitted from news UK finance minister \uc2 Kwasi Kwarteng\uc2  will bring forward his fiscal plan from Nov. 23 to a date expected before the next scheduled \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  rate decision on Nov. 3. That news came hot on the heels of Monday's U-turn on tax policy from Kwarteng, which also boosted GBP. GBP/EUR has also climbed sharply from a two-year low of 1.08 at the start of last week to 1.1564, its highest level since mid-September. With Britain's weakened Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  facing a fight for credibility, it leaves the pound at the mercy of events - which may or may not prove to be good for it. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3SOeZvC"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3EczfmP"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  Bad gambling may drive EUR/USD to fresh year lows Speculators have flipped their positions to bet on EUR/USD rallies, with little obvious justification. They did this at the start of this year, and there is a high risk that the massive drop that resulted is repeated. As traders flipped from shorts to longs in January, EUR/USD dropped from near 1.15 to 1.0349. Traders strongly increased their bullish bets before Federal Reserve and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meetings in June, lifting the pair to 1.0787, with EUR/USD then collapsing towards the year low before they sold short. They had only established a modest short position when EUR/USD dropped below parity and rushed to reverse these bets, selling a massive ten billion dollars around the time of September's \uc2 ECB\uc2  meeting and lifting the pair to 1.0198. EUR/USD subsequently dropped to 0.9528 where it was technically oversold, resulting in the current rally which has reached 0.9875. It is no longer oversold. Traders are betting it goes up, yet the trend is clearly down and the interest rate divide that is weighing is not seen changing. EUR/USD may soon meet the next targets for the trend at 0.9250 and 0.8850. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3UWKHJ8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3RsCrxC"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-7T10:47:23.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221004eia401nkh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
