\par\par \b \uc2 BUZZ-Europe  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Markets Open - October 3\b0\par\par\uc2 1,489 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2022\par 06:41\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 USD stays bid, AUD perky ahead of \uc2 RBA\uc2 , risk soft\par \par \uc2 \f2               \par               \par               \par               \par Market Briefs\par \par               \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 Fed united on inflation front as Brainard rejects early rate cuts \u8226\'20\'22 Britain's Kwarteng doubles down on tax cuts, promises fiscal discipline \u8226\'20\'22 S&P cuts UK rating outlook after tax cut plan \u8226\'20\'22 Swiss franc not highly valued - SNB's Jordan \u8226\'20\'22 Japan ready to take 'decisive' steps on yen - finance minister \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 BOJ\uc2  board debated risk of overshoot in inflation - Sept mtg summary \u8226\'20\'22 Japan's business mood worsens as cost hikes bite - BOJ tankan \u8226\'20\'22 JP Tankan Big Mf Idx Q3 8, 11 f'cast, 9 prev; Big Mf Outlook DI Q3 9, 11 f'cast, 10 prev \u8226\'20\'22 JP Tankan Big Non-Mf Idx Q3 14, 13 f'cast, 13 prev; big non-mf outlook DI Q3 11, 15 f'cast, 13 prev \u8226\'20\'22 JP Tankan All Big Capex Est Q3 21.5, 18.8% f'cast, 18.6% prev; All Sm Capex Est Q3 1.3%, 1.6% f'cast, -1.4% prev \u8226\'20\'22 JP Tankan Small Mf Idx Q3 -4, -3 f'cast, -4 prev; Sm Mf Outlook DI Q3 -5, -5 f'cast, -5 prev \u8226\'20\'22 JP Tankan Small Non-Mf Idx Q3 2, -2 f'cast, -1 prev; Sm Non-Mf Outlook DI Q3 -3, -3 f'cast, -5 prev \u8226\'20\'22 JP Sep JibunBK Mfg PMI Final SA 50.8, 51.0 prev \u8226\'20\'22 China's Sept new home prices fall for third straight month - private survey \u8226\'20\'22 Australia house prices fall again in Sept, slide slows somewhat \u8226\'20\'22 Asia's factory activity weakens on global slowdown, cost pressures \u8226\'20\'22 Brazil election goes to runoff as Bolsonaro outperforms polls \u8226\'20\'22 Oil rebounds 3% as OPEC+ weighs biggest output cut since 2020 Looking Ahead - Economic Data (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 07:50 FR Sep S&P Global Mfg PMI 47.8 f'cast, 47.8 prev \u8226\'20\'22 07:55 DE Sep S&P Global/BME Mfg PMI 48.3 f'cast, 48.3 prev \u8226\'20\'22 08:00 EZ Sep S&P Global Mfg Final PMI 48.5 f'cast, 48.5 prev \u8226\'20\'22 08:30 GB Sep S&P GLBL/CIPS Mfg PMI FNL 48.5 f'cast, 48.5 prev Looking Ahead - Events, Auctions, Other Releases (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 N/A \uc2 ECB\uc2 's Lagarde and Panetta participate in Eurogroup meeting in Luxembourg \u8226\'20\'22 N/A British finance minister Kwarteng speaks at annual Conservative party conference \u8226\'20\'22 09:00 Bank of Spain's De Cos speaks at financial conference in Barcelona \u8226\'20\'22 10:00 \uc2 Riksbank\uc2 's Flod\u233\'00\'E9n participates in seminar, to discuss current monetary policy \u8226\'20\'22 18:00 \uc2 BoE\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Mann panellist at CD Howe Institute's 'The path back to 2 percent' Commentary and Analysis USD/JPY Holds below 145.00 on Japan "decisive steps" warning \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY consolidates in a well supported 144.50-144.89 range Monday \u8226\'20\'22 Rally capped as Japan's finmin says ready to take 'decisive' steps on yen \u8226\'20\'22 \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent record of nearly $20 bln on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support yen \u8226\'20\'22 Can use sizable 135.5 bln held in deposits with foreign c.banks \u8226\'20\'22 Japan's business mood worsens as cost hikes bite - BOJ tankan \u8226\'20\'22 Buoyant US yields, Fed's determined inflation fight to limit downside \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance 144.90-145.00, 145.50, support 144.50, 143.90-144.00 EUR/USD climbs shakily as US stocks futures dribble lower \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD climbs as high as 0.9829 from 0.9774, last 0.9807 \u8226\'20\'22 Friday's close above 38.2% Fibo 0.9777 looks supportive \u8226\'20\'22 50% retracement at 0.9855 from September' tumble may cap rally \u8226\'20\'22 Wobbly start to the week as US stock futures slip a tad \u8226\'20\'22 Focus on US NFP this week but EU data may have \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  impact too \u8226\'20\'22 Sept producer prices, retail sales will be watched AUD/USD- Better bid on pre-\uc2 RBA\uc2  bargain hunting \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD rallies 0.6% on light short-covering as focus turns to \uc2 RBA\uc2  Tuesday \u8226\'20\'22 \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expected to hike 50 bps, but may surprise with 25 \u8226\'20\'22 Higher-than-expected global inflation ups fears of jumbo c.bank rate hikes \u8226\'20\'22 Risk aversion, falling commodities, recession fears cap AUD rallies \u8226\'20\'22 ASX 200 flat; Sydney holiday Monday limits \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading interest \u8226\'20\'22 Asia range 0.64025-0.6451; support 0.6400-05,0.6360-65, resistance 0.6480-85 GBP/USD struggles amid lingering risk-off; Fibo floor eyed \u8226\'20\'22 GBP/USD fades to 1.1131 from Mon peak 1.1195 vs close 1.1165 \u8226\'20\'22 Could probe Aug-Sep 38.2% Fibo support at 1.1070 \u8226\'20\'22 If that fails, Bollinger downtrend channel 1.0995 attracts \u8226\'20\'22 Lingering risk-off as S&P futures struggle to stay up \u8226\'20\'22 UK finance minister to speak at Conservative conference \u8226\'20\'22 More GBP stress expected as market looks to US NFP this week \uc2 RBA\uc2  expected to hike 50 bps, but may surprise with 25 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  is widely expected to lift the cash rate on Tuesday, but board members are likely to have a lively debate on the size of the hike. The latest Reuters poll clearly favours a half-point rise to 2.85%, with 21 of 29 economists polled expecting that outcome. They cite the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 's aggressive action and note that the Australian dollar's 6.5% fall against the greenback since the last meeting is adding to inflationary pressures. Some economists, including those at \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2 , believe the \uc2 RBA\uc2  will opt for a smaller 25 basis-point hike. They highlight new monthly inflation data showing a slight easing of price pressures and the fact the \uc2 RBA\uc2  may want to assess the impact of four straight 50 bps hikes. \uc2 RBA\uc2  Governor \uc2 Philip Lowe\uc2  said on Sept 16 that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was getting closer to the point for a smaller rate rise and would discuss whether to hike 25 or 50 bps at the October meeting. The falling AUD may tip the balance towards a 50 bps hike, but it will be a close call. A half-point hike accompanied by a statement indicating the cash rate is in neutral territory would likely see limited AUD/USD upside. A surprise 25 bps hike would probably send AUD/USD below the 2022 trend low at 0.6363. Week Ahead-UK budget fallout, \uc2 RBA\uc2 , \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  hikes in focus British Finance Minister \uc2 Kwasi Kwarteng\uc2 's comments at the Conservative Party's annual conference will be closely watched after his mini-budget sent markets into turmoil. The Reserve Banks of Australia and New Zealand are expected to hike interest rates this week. \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  was forced to buy huge amounts of bonds to avoid a financial crisis after the new \uc2 British government\uc2 's tax cut plan. While the \uc2 BoE\b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  calmed markets for now, analysts believe the elements that caused the upheaval are still in place. The \uc2 RBA\uc2  meets on Tuesday and is expected to raise the cash rate by 50 basis points to 2.85%, according to the latest Reuters poll. The \uc2 RBA\uc2  will likely debate between 25 or 50 bps and Governor \uc2 Philip Lowe\uc2  has noted the case for tightening at a slower pace. But the rise in global inflation since his comments, aggressive tightening by other major central banks and the steep fall in the AUD tip the balance in favour of a 50 bps hike. The \uc2 RBNZ\uc2  meets on Wednesday and the latest Reuters poll shows 26 of 29 economists expect a 50 bps rise to 3.50%, with the majority seeing the cash rate at 4.0% by the end of 2022. This follows hawkish guidance at the \uc2 RBNZ\uc2 's August meeting and a 12% drop in the NZD/USD since then, which has added to inflationary pressures. Week Ahead-U.S. jobs report leads busy global data calendar The U.S. jobs report for September leads a busy global data calendar this week, with the market expecting another strong rise in non-farm payrolls. The Reuters poll predicts 250,000 jobs were created in September, while the unemployment rate is expected to remain at 3.7% and average hourly earnings to rise 0.3% month-on-month, the same as August. A stronger-than-expected jobs report will strengthen the case for more aggressive Federal Reserve rate hikes. Other key U.S. data includes ISM manufacturing and non-manufacturing, factory orders, international trade and ADP employment. Euro zone final September PMIs lead Europe's calendar, with producer prices, retail sales and German industrial production also due. UK PMI will be released this week, along with Halifax house prices. Chinese markets are closed for the week-long National Day break but \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and Caixin services \uc2 PMI\uc2  are expected. Japan's quarterly Tankan survey, out early Monday, dominates the week's calendar. Tokyo CPI and final PMIs are also scheduled. Australia's data includes building approvals and trade. New Zealand has quarterly NZIER business confidence, while trade, \uc2 PMI\uc2  and employment data are due in Canada.\par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-6T04:41:10.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221003eia300hld\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
