\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Asian Shares Drift Lower In Thin Holiday Trade\b0\par\par\uc2 484 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Asian stocks retreated on Monday as inflation and interest-rate worries lingered, and oil prices spiked on speculation over OPEC+ cutting production substantially this week.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices jumped more than 3 percent ahead of the Oct. 5 OPEC+ meeting, while the dollar/yen rate held steady below the 145-level following an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to shore up the fragile \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Trading volumes were thin as Chinese markets kicked off the Golden Week holiday. South Korean markets were also shut.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's Hang Seng index ended 0.83 percent lower at 17,0879.51, after having hit a low of 16,096.96 earlier in the day on fears of tightening U.S. monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Japanese stocks bucked the weak trend, with chip-related firms and energy companies leading the surge. Sentiment was buoyed by the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's "tankan" survey results showing that corporate capital expenditure plans for the current fiscal year remains strong.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei average climbed 1.07 percent to 26,215.79, while the broader Topix index closed 0.63 percent higher at 1,847.58, reversing initial losses.\par \par \uc2 Japan Petroleum gained 2.9 percent and \uc2 Inpex Corp\uc2  added 3 percent as oil prices rallied amid talk of OPEC+ production cuts. In the tech sector, Screen Holdings, \uc2 Tokyo Electron\uc2  and \uc2 Advantest\uc2  surged 4-5 percent.\par \par \uc2 Mimasu Semiconductor Industry soared 12.5 percent after a strong forecast for earnings this fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 Australian markets ended slightly lower ahead of a widely expected interest rate hike by the Reserve Bank on Tuesday, with economists expecting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise rates by 50 basis points to 2.85 percent -- a level not seen since April 2013 to stem inflation which is trending at 20-year highs.\par \par \uc2 The benchmark S&P/ASX 200 dropped 0.27 percent to 6,456.90 while the broader All Ordinaries index slipped 0.33 percent to close at 6,656.40. Tech stocks led losses, with \uc2 WiseTech Global\uc2 , Appen and \uc2 Zip Co\uc2  falling 1-4 percent.\par \par \uc2 Across the Tasman, New Zealand's benchmark S&P/NZX-50 index fell 0.96 percent to 10,959.45.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks fell in choppy trading on Friday to notch their longest streak of quarterly losses since the market collapse of 2008, as sportswear maker \uc2 Nike\uc2  warned of a margin squeeze and new data closely watched by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  showed that prices continued to rise at a rapid pace in August.\par \par \uc2 In remarks prepared for a speech in New York, Fed Vice Chair \uc2 Lael Brainard\uc2  warned that the risk of additional inflationary shocks cannot be ruled out and that monetary policy will need to be restrictive for some time.\par \par \uc2 The Dow lost 1.7 percent while the S&P 500 and the tech-heavy Nasdaq Composite both shed around 1.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020221003eia300819\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
