\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen rises above key level and sterling trims gains\b0\par\par\uc2 582 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 By Rae Wee\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Oct 3 (Reuters) - The yen rose above the key 145 per dollar level on Monday for the first time in more than a week since \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while sterling gave back some of its gains after a modest recovery late last week.\par \par \uc2 The yen bottomed at 145.4 per dollar, and at the time of writing was down 0.1% at 144.9, in an Asian session of limited volume on a public holiday in China, South Korea and some Australian states.\par \par \uc2 Monday's drop followed comments by Finance Minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2  that Japan was prepared to take "decisive" action in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if excessive yen movements persisted.\par \par \uc2 September 22 was the last time the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened below 145 per dollar, after the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  kept interest rates ultra-low, prompting record spending of 2.8 trillion yen ($19.7 billion) by the authorities to prop up the yen.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, sterling was losing 0.69% to $1.1088 after a sharp rally late last week when the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  said it would buy as much government debt as necessary to restore order after financial chaos unleashed by new Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 's tax-cut plans.\par \par \uc2 Truss reiterated on Sunday that the government was sticking to its economic policy and added that Finance Minister \uc2 Kwasi Kwarteng\uc2  had not previously informed his cabinet of the decision.\par \par \uc2 The Australian and New Zealand dollars were gaining ground in Asia ahead of expected rate hikes by their central banks during the week.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar was up 0.25% to 0.64270 dollars, while the "kiwi" (New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) was up 0.41% to 0.56265 dollars.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  and the \uc2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\uc2  meet on Tuesday and Wednesday, respectively, and markets expect both to raise their interest rates by 50 basis points, although attention will also focus on the tone of the monetary leaders.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  could be quite influential if it gives a more nuanced approach and signals that it could drop to 25 basis points at the November meeting. That could be taken, globally, quite well," said Chris Weston, head of analysis at Pepperstone.\par \par \uc2 The euro was down 0.1% at $0.9790 as expectations of another big rate hike from the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  this month, following red-hot inflation data, raised fears that the economy could slip into recession.\par \par \uc2 Data on Friday showed euro zone inflation beat forecasts and hit a record 10.0% in September, beating expectations of 9.7%.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 ECB\uc2  is still going to have to go hard. (...) For me, Europe and the U.K., it's less about the dynamics of relative interest rates and more about the dynamics of growth," Weston said.\par \par \uc2 "I think what we're starting to try to do now is look for markets where we can see the inflation ceiling or start to feel a little bit more confident about the trajectory around inflation. I think the U.S. falls into that category."\par \par \uc2 The U.S. job creation report is due later in the week, while manufacturing \uc2 PMI\uc2  data due on Monday will also give some insight into the outlook for the global economy.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index was up 0.06% at 112.30.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Rae Wee; editing by Clarence Fernandez; translated by Tomas Cobos)\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020221003eia3007cn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
