\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen weakens past key level, sterling pares gains\b0\par\par\uc2 568 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 2022\par 07:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Oct 3 (Reuters) - The yen breached the key level of 145 a dollar on Monday, for the first time in more than a week since \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while sterling gave up some of its gains after a modest recovery at the end of last week.\par \par \uc2 The yen bottomed at 145.4 to the dollar, and last traded down 0.1% at 144.9, in thin Asian trade on a holiday in China, South Korea and some Australian states.\par \par \uc2 Monday's fall came after Finance Minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2 's comments that Japan stood ready for "decisive" steps in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if excessive yen moves persisted.\par \par \uc2 Sept. 22 was the last time the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had weakened below 145 to the dollar, after the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  stuck with ultra-low interest rates, which prompted a record expenditure of 2.8 trillion yen ($19.7 billion) by authorities to prop up the yen.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, sterling lost 0.69% to $1.1088, following a strong rebound at the end of last week when the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  said it would buy as much government debt as needed to restore order after financial chaos unleashed by new Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 's plans to cut taxes.\par \par \uc2 The government was sticking with the policy, Truss reiterated on Sunday, adding that her cabinet had not been told in advance of the decision by finance minister \uc2 Kwasi Kwarteng\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Australian and New Zealand dollars gained ground in Asia ahead of expected rate hikes by their central banks during the week.\par \par \uc2 The Aussie was up 0.25% to $0.64270, while the kiwi was 0.41% higher at $0.56265.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  and the \uc2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\uc2  meet on Tuesday and Wednesday, respectively, with markets expecting both to lift their cash rate by 50 basis points, though focus will also be on the tone of policymakers.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 RBA\uc2  could be quite influential if they give a more nuanced approach and signal that they may come down to 25 basis points in the November meeting. That might be taken, globally, quite well," said Chris Weston, head of research at Pepperstone.\par \par \uc2 The euro fell 0.1% to $0.9790, as expectations for another jumbo \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate hike this month, following a red-hot inflation print, heightened worries that the economy would be tipped into a recession.\par \par \uc2 Data on Friday showed that euro zone inflation zoomed past forecasts to a record high of 10.0% in September, beating expectations of 9.7%.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 ECB\uc2  is still going to have to go hard... for me, Europe and the UK, it's less about relative interest rate dynamics, and more about growth dynamics," said Weston.\par \par \uc2 "I think what we're starting to try and do now is look at markets where we can price inflation or start feeling a bit more confident about the trajectory around inflation. I think the U.S. falls into that category."\par \par \uc2 U.S. non-farm payrolls are due at the end of the week, while a flood of manufacturing \uc2 PMI\uc2  data out later on Monday will also give insight into the outlook for the global economy.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar index rose 0.06% to 112.30.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Rae Wee; Editing by Clarence Fernandez) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-3T06:20:09.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020221003eia300lol\par }\page {