\par\par \b \uc2 REFILE-UPDATE 2- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  spent record of nearly $20.0 bln on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  to support the yen\b0\par\par\uc2 545 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 Oktober 2022\par 05:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Corrects to add dropped word 'to' in first paragraph)\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  drains nearly 15% of readily available funds\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  can avoid selling \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  bills for now - analysts\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Impact of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may diminish - analysts\par \par \uc2 By Leika Kihara and Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 30 (Reuters) - Japan spent up to a record 2.8 trillion yen ($19.7 billion) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last week to prop up the yen, \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on Friday, draining nearly 15% of funds it has readily available for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The figure was less than the 3.6 trillion yen estimated by Tokyo money market brokers for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first dollar-selling, yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 24 years to stem the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's sharp weakening.\par \par \uc2 The ministry's figure, indicating total spending on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Aug. 30 to Sept. 28, is widely believed to have been used entirely for the Sept. 22 \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It would surpass the previous record for dollar-selling, yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1998 of 2.62 trillion yen. Confirmation on the dates of the spending will be released in November.\par \par \uc2 "This was a big burst of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if it had happened on a single day, underscoring Japanese authorities' determination to defend the yen," said Daisaku Ueno, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "But the impact of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will diminish as long as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  solo," he said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , conducted after the yen slumped to a 24-year low of nearly 146 to the dollar, triggered a sharp bounce of more than 5 yen per dollar from that low, although the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has since drifted down again to around 144.25.\par \par \uc2 "Recent sharp, one-sided yen declines heighten uncertainty by making it difficult for companies to set business plans. It's therefore undesirable and bad for the economy," \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  was quoted as saying at a meeting with cabinet ministers on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Japan held roughly $1.3 trillion in reserves, the second biggest after China, of which $135.5 billion was held as deposits parked with foreign central banks and the \uc2 Bank for International Settlements\uc2  (BIS), according to foreign reserves data released on Sept. 7. Those deposits can easily be tapped to finance further dollar-selling, yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Even if it were to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  likely won't have to sell \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  bills and instead tap this deposit for the time being," said Izuru Kato, chief economist at Totan Research, a think-tank arm of a major money market brokerage firm in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 If the deposits dry up, Japan would need to dip into its securities holdings sized around $1.04 trillion.\par \par \uc2 Of the main types of foreign assets Japan holds, deposits and securities are the most liquid and can be converted into cash immediately.\par \par \uc2 Other holdings include gold, reserves at the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ) and \uc2 IMF\uc2  special drawing rights (SDRs), although procuring dollar funds from these assets would take time, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 ($1 = 144.4000 yen) (Reporting by Leika Kihara and Tetsushi Kajimoto; Editing by Sam Holmes, Edmund Klamann & Shri Navaratnam) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-1T04:24:51.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220930ei9u01t4j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
