\par\par \b \uc2 BUZZ-Asia  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Markets Open - October 3\b0\par\par\uc2 2,454 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2022\par 22:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 USD higher after hot core PCE lifts Fed higher-for-longer rate view\par \par \uc2 Market briefs\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 U.S. consumer spending rebounds, but high inflation cooling demand \u8226\'20\'22 US Aug Consumption, Adjusted MM 0.4%, 0.2% f'cast, 0.1% prev, -0.2% r'vsd \u8226\'20\'22 US Aug Personal Income MM 0.3%, 0.3% f'cast, 0.2% prev, 0.3% r'vsd \u8226\'20\'22 US Aug Personal Consump Real MM 0.1%, 0.2% prev, -0.1% r'vsd \u8226\'20\'22 US Aug Core PCE Price Index MM 0.6%, 0.5% f'cast, 0.1% prev, 0.0% r'vsd; YY 4.9%, 4.7% f'cast, 4.6% prev, 4.7% r'vsd \u8226\'20\'22 US Aug PCE Price Index MM 0.3%, -0.1% prev; YY 6.2%, 6.3% prev, 6.4% r'vsd \u8226\'20\'22 US Sep U Mich Sentiment Final 58.6, 59.5 f'cast, 59.5 prev \u8226\'20\'22 Fed united on inflation front as Brainard rejects early rate cuts \u8226\'20\'22 Fed's Barkin: At this point risk of inflation festering feels bigger than that of Fed doing too much \u8226\'20\'22 Fed's Daly: Additional rate hikes are the right thing to do, but how high we will go depends on data, hoping can get to 2% inflation by end of 2023 or close to 2024, but will chip away at it earlier than that \u8226\'20\'22 Fed's Brainard: Will take time for tighter financial conditions to fully impact different sectors and bring inflation down \u8226\'20\'22 Brainard: Will take time for tighter financial conditions to fully impact different sectors and bring inflation down, risk of additional inflationary shocks cannot be ruled out \u8226\'20\'22 Brainard warns against premature rate cuts in face of high inflation \u8226\'20\'22 Barkin: Will need positive real interest rates to restrain demand, just beginning to reach that level, hard decision for Fed is sorting out what level is sufficient to stop \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  won't make emergency rate hike but will go big on Nov 3-Reuters poll \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 ECB\uc2 's Schnabel says further increases in our key policy rates will be needed, risks of a wage-price spiral are contained, provided inflation expectations remain anchored Looking Ahead - Economic Data (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 2 Oct 22:00 AU Sep Mfg PMI Final 53.9 prev \u8226\'20\'22 2 Oct 23:50 JP Q3 Tankan Big Mf Idx 11 f'cast, 9 prev; Tankan Big Non-Mf Idx 13 f'cast, 13 prev \u8226\'20\'22 2 Oct 23:50 JP Q3 Tankan Big Mf Outlook DI 11 f'cast, 10 prev; Tankan big non-mf outlook DI 15 f'cast, 13 prev \u8226\'20\'22 2 Oct 23:50 JP Q3 Tankan All Big Capex Est 18.8% f'cast, 18.6% prev; Tankan All Sm Capex Est 1.6% f'cast, -1.4% prev \u8226\'20\'22 2 Oct 23:50 JP Q3 Tankan Small Mf Idx -3 f'cast, -4 prev; Tankan Small Non-Mf Idx -2 f'cast, -1 prev \u8226\'20\'22 2 Oct 23:50 JP Q3 Tankan Sm Mf Outlook DI -5 f'cast, -5 prev; Tankan Sm Non-Mf Outlook DI -3 f'cast, -5 prev \u8226\'20\'22 3 Oct 00:30 JP Sep JibunBK Mfg PMI Final SA 51.0 prev \u8226\'20\'22 3 Oct 21:00 NZ Q3 NZIER Confidence -65 prev \u8226\'20\'22 3 Oct 21:30 AU Q3 AIG Manufacturing Index 49.3 prev \u8226\'20\'22 3 Oct 23:30 JP Sep CPI, Overall Tokyo 2.9% prev \u8226\'20\'22 3 Oct 23:30 JP Sep CPI Tokyo Ex fresh food YY 2.8% f'cast, 2.6% prev \u8226\'20\'22 3 Oct 23:60 JP Sep Monetary Base YY 0.32% prev\par \par \uc2 Looking Ahead - Events, Other Releases (GMT)\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 Oct 1 07:00 The 2022 La Toja Forum ends with the participation of the Bank of Spain\u180\'00\'B4s De Cos and the Euorpean Commission's Borrell \u8226\'20\'22 Oct 2 23:50 BOJ releases summary of opinions from board members at its Sept. 21-22 policy meeting \u8226\'20\'22 Oct 3 N/A British Finance Minister Kwarteng speaks at the annual Conservative party conference in the aftermath of the pound plummeting following his mini-budget \u8226\'20\'22 Oct 3 10:00 \uc2 Riksbank\uc2 's Flod\u233\'00\'E9n participates in a seminar and discuss current monetary policy at the \uc2 Stockholm Chamber of Commerce\uc2  \u8226\'20\'22 Oct 3 13:05 \uc2 Atlanta Fed\uc2 's Bostic speaks at the 2022 Technology-Enabled Disruption conference, hosted by the Federal Reserve Banks of Atlanta, Richmond, and Dallas \u8226\'20\'22 Oct 3 15:45 \uc2 Richmond Fed\uc2 's Barkin speaks in Atlanta at "Techology-Enabled Disruption" conference organized by Federal Reserve Banks of Atlanta, Dallas and Richmond \u8226\'20\'22 Oct 3 18:00 BoE's Mann participates at the CD Howe Institute's Monetary Policy Initiative 'The path back to 2 percent' \u8226\'20\'22 Oct 3 18:15 \uc2 Kansas City Fed\uc2 's George speaks on "Payments" before the \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago\uc2  Payments Symposium \u8226\'20\'22 Oct 3 22:45 \uc2 Atlanta Fed\uc2 's Bostic moderates a discussion before virtual 2022 Technology-Enabled Disruption conference\par \par \uc2 Macro Themes In Play\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 The dollar index rose on Friday as the corrective pullback it suffered after \uc2 BoE\uc2  bond \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and heading into quarter-end ran its course, with high-beta \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling off in particular as focus shifted to global recession risk. \u8226\'20\'22 Reflecting this risk, euro zone yields weakened even after inflation in the bloc hit an above-forecast 10% in August, with the core rate rising to 6.1% from 5.5%]. Sizeable pre-existing \uc2 ECB\uc2  rate hike pricing at the next two meetings cushioned the impact, as did falling energy. \u8226\'20\'22 Euro zone inflation pricing continued to drift lower and the period-end scramble for dollar liquidity via swaps showed signs of peaking. \u8226\'20\'22 Though U.S. core PCE and spending were above forecast, the dollar-supportive bounce in Treasury yields was slowed by a big dive in September Chicago PMI and downward revision to Michigan consumer sentiment, that is until a late surge in yields. \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD fell 0.1%, after its rise to a high of 0.9854 on EBS halted near the 50% Fibo of September's 1.0198-0.9528 tumble at 0.9863. It could close bullishly above the tenkan and 10-day moving average by 0.9790. \u8226\'20\'22 The broader dollar index bounced 0.27% after erasing all of its post-Fed meeting gains. Past dollar uptrend corrections hint there could be more downside if peak Fed rate hike pricing has passed. \u8226\'20\'22 Sterling gained 0.4% as its correction from the 1.0327 record lows and UK financial instability the \uc2 BoE\uc2  addressed on Monday reached 1.1235 by last Wednesday's lows and the 38.2% Fibo of the May-September slide. \u8226\'20\'22 The pound's recent collapse had also sent EUR/GBP soaring almost to the 2020 risk-off peak, with those gains now basically erased, as the euro zone joins the UK in the double-digit inflation realm and governments roll out costly plans to blunt the inflation trauma. \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY managed a 0.2% gain and maintained its risk/reward vigil just below resistance at 145 and ahead of the 24-year peak at 145.90 \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's MoF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  against last Thursday for a flash dive to 140.31. \u8226\'20\'22 With the MoF Thursday reaffirming its readiness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again and having plenty of ready reserves to sell, bulls staring at a great long USD/JPY carry trade have to weigh whether to risk a year's worth of carry due to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with USD/JPY only about 2% away from 1998's peak. \u8226\'20\'22 Buying dips remains the mantra, while keeping plenty of risk-reversal downside protection deployed. \u8226\'20\'22 The Australian and Canadian dollars fell 1.35% and 1.0%, respectively. \u8226\'20\'22 USD/CNH gained 0.5% on the assumption the \uc2 PBoC\uc2 's only trying to slow the dollar's advance, not reverse the uptrend to record highs in offshore yuan. \u8226\'20\'22 Energy prices fell sharply, led by European natural gas regardless of the war in Ukraine as global \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  tightening threatens demand. \u8226\'20\'22 The focus next week is on Friday's U.S. jobs report, with a glance at ISM along the way.\par \par \uc2 Commentary and Analysis\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD-Upward momentum stalls, indecision emerges\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD hit a 6-session high of 0.9854 then fell, NY opened near 0.9765 \u8226\'20\'22 0.9734 hit on EBS after US PCE data buoyed US rates & US$ \u8226\'20\'22 Pair rallied above 0.9800 with help from EUR/JPY lift above 141.70 \u8226\'20\'22 Equity commodity losses helped stall the intra-day rally \u8226\'20\'22 USD/CNH lift above 7.1325 helped keep EUR/USD near the 10-DMA late \u8226\'20\'22 Daily doji formed, implies indecision but daily RSI diverged on the high \u8226\'20\'22 Falling monthly RSI implies longer-term downward momentum remains \u8226\'20\'22 Record 10% euro zone inflation fails to lift EUR/USD outlook\par \par \uc2 USD/JPY-Late Treasury yield rise keeps 145 pivot point nearby, MoF in sight\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY was stuck below resistance at 145 all week and is ending bid \u8226\'20\'22 Dips bought as juicy Tsy-JGB carry trade draws in buyers \u8226\'20\'22 Bulls weigh risk of getting run over by MoF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  again \u8226\'20\'22 But Tsy yields rising before the close, forcing fresh bids \u8226\'20\'22 Prices now less than 2% from 1998's 147.64 peak \u8226\'20\'22 Last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  sent prices down 3.8%, albeit briefly \u8226\'20\'22 145 was high prior to Thur's peak and is 100% Fibo off Aug's base \u8226\'20\'22 Close above it could open up 161.8% Fibo at 149.52 in time \u8226\'20\'22 But might need strong NFP report to expend Fed hike ceiling, USD rise\par \par \uc2 Sterling runs into offers above 1.12, holds lion's share of weekly gain\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 GBP$ ending NorAm +0.05% at 1.1125, Friday's range 1.1235-1.1025 \u8226\'20\'22 Month/quarter-end USD selling abates, GBP offered abv 1.1220 in Asia, Europe \u8226\'20\'22 GBP$ dips from high above 1.12 as month-end USD selling ebbs \u8226\'20\'22 US core PCE running hot keeps Fed higher-for-longer view in focus, lifts USD \u8226\'20\'22 GBP$ support 1.1077's 10-DMA, 1.1021, 50% of 1.0327-1.1235; 200-HMA at 1.10 \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance 1.1176 NorAm pullback high off 1.1025 low, 1.1235 Fri's 1-wk high \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/GBP dips 0.48% to 0.8797, Fri range 0.8846-0.8753; UK rates diverge from EZ\par \par \uc2 AUD/USD-Longs put on defense into the weekend\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 Weaker US rates, US$ helped AUD/USD hit 0.6523 overnight but gains faded \u8226\'20\'22 Intense sterling sales brought on a bout of risk-off trading, buoyed US$ \u8226\'20\'22 USD/CNH rallied above 7.1300, AUD/JPY fell towards 92.90 \u8226\'20\'22 Equity gains eroded and copper turned negative on the day \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD fell below the 61.8% Fib of 0.5510-0.8007 then struck 0.6418 \u8226\'20\'22 Techs lean bearish; RSIs falling, daily inverted hammer candle formed \u8226\'20\'22 Break of Sept. 28 low will negate bull signal, likely resume down trend\par \par \uc2 Commentary & Analysis\par \par \uc2 Record 10% euro zone inflation fails to lift EUR/USD outlook\par \par \uc2 EUR/USD fell on Friday, retreating from a six-session high as sterling selling -- and accompanying financial stability fears -- boosted the dollar while rising U.S. and European inflation was seen as more supportive for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  than the euro. Euro zone inflation hit a record high of 10% in September, bolstering the case for a 75bp \uc2 ECB\uc2  hike in October, but bund yields and implied Euribor interest rates fell, showing investors expect aggressive tightening to heighten recession risks, thus undermining the euro. In contrast, with the U.S. economy in a strong position, the dollar firmed after above-forecast U.S. August core PCE boosted U.S. rates since it is seen bolstering the Fed's hawkish stance. The dollar's yield advantage over the euro, reflected in two-year Treasury-bund spreads, hit its widest since August 26. Technicals highlight downside risks. Daily RSI is falling again after diverging on Friday's high, EUR/USD is trading below the 10-day moving average and monthly RSI implies downside momentum despite being in oversold territory. Another test of support near 0.9500 remains possible, a break of which would lead shorts to target the 0.9000 area. Charts: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3BZQwg8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  & {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3SISZlP"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\par \par \uc2 Greed or fear is the dilemma as dollar bulls consider the yen\par \par \uc2 Dollar bulls face a classic fear-or-greed dilemma as they consider whether two-year Treasury-JGB yield spreads above 4% and the potential for a 2% rally to 1998's Asian debt crisis highs versus the yen are worth risking the wrath of \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . After the MOF spent $20bln last Thursday to slam USD/JPY 3.8% lower, the answer is an enthusiastic maybe. USD/JPY recovered from that 145.90-140.31 plunge but stalled for five sessions below resistance at 145, with traders wary of inciting more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which could wipe out a year's worth of interest rate carry in a couple of hours. Adding to the dilemma is that peak Fed hike pricing has stalled just above 4.5% since last week and the broader dollar index is working through a correction from overbought extremes. Friday's PCE , Chicago PMI and Michigan sentiment failed to lift Fed rate hike expectations. The \uc2 BoJ\uc2  has dismissed the rise in Japanese inflation above its 2% target, and is likely to maintain its -0.1% policy rate, 0.25% cap on 10-year JGB yields and QQE, at least until Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2 's term ends in April. Chart: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3SDyTK4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\par \par \uc2 Chart of the day (GBP/USD)\par \par \uc2 GBP/USD weakened on Friday, falling 130 pips from early session highs by 1.1235 as quarter-end and post-BoE \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar selling ebbed, and it is likely to slide further as traders question the merits of the UK's fiscal stimulus amid more hawkish monetary policy. Technically, Wednesday's close above 1.1033, the 50% Fib of 1.1738-1.0327 signals GBP/USD bears are retreating and bulls focus is turning to a series of daily moving averages from the 21-DMA at 1.1311 to the 55-DMA at 1.1728. However, reduced quarter-end dollar selling and Friday's hot euro zone and U.S. inflation data should keep the Fed, and \uc2 ECB\uc2 , on a higher-for-longer hike path, usurping recent sterling gains. Despite Friday's significant fall from one-week highs by 1.1235, the break of 1.1033 should be respected and bulls are in control while GBP/USD is above 1.0781, the 50% Fib of 1.0327-1.1235. The key roadblock for sterling remains conflicting fiscal and monetary policies. While the \uc2 BoE\uc2 's gilt \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  calmed markets in the near-term, sterling traders may be less optimistic as they await finance minister \uc2 Kwasi Kwarteng\uc2 's medium-term fiscal plan on Nov. 23. Chart: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3SsTsJp"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-10-3T20:00:45.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220930ei9u044ot\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
