\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Spent $19.4 Billion This Month Trying to Shore Up the Yen Against the Dollar -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Megumi Fujikawa \par \uc2 328 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2022\par 13:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's first yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 24 years didn't come cheap.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Friday it spent 2.8 trillion yen, equivalent to $19.4 billion, defending the yen in September as the strong dollar reverberated worldwide ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/for-wall-street-a-strong-dollar-is-front-and-center-11662849218"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.wsj.com/articles/for-wall-street-a-strong-dollar-is-front-and-center-11662849218}}}\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 The ministry said last week it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/japan-intervenes-in-currency-market-to-support-yen-11663836942"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.wsj.com/articles/\b  \highlight22japan\b0 \highlight22 -\b  \highlight22intervenes\b0 \highlight22 -in-\b  \highlight22currency\b0 \highlight22 -market-to-support-yen-11663836942}}}\uc2 ) after the yen fell to a 24-year low against the dollar. It hasn't said whether it acted on other days as well, but analysts said Friday's figure suggested that the Sept. 22 action was probably the only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far.\par \par \uc2 On that day, the dollar rose to 145.90 yen, then fell sharply after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But the effects weren't long-lasting. The dollar rose again this week to more than 144 yen.\par \par \uc2 The government's most recent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  before this year was in 2011, the year of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's devastating earthquake and tsunami, when it had the opposite objective. It bought dollars then to push down what it saw as an overly strong yen.\par \par \uc2 With more than $1 trillion in foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, Japan still has firepower--and history suggests there's a chance of more to come in 2022. Still, the market is huge too. More than $1.1 trillion ({\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bis.org/statistics/rpfx19.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://www.bis.org/statistics/rpfx19.htm}}}\uc2 ) of yen trading takes place each day, according to the \uc2 Bank for International Settlements\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NOTE: In-line links reference additional content of interest chosen by the WSJ news team.\par \par \uc2 This item is part of a Wall Street Journal live coverage event. The full stream can be found by searching P/WSJL (WSJ Live Coverage).\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 30, 2022 07:51 ET (11:51 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220930ei9u0029b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
