\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Asian shares head for worst month since pandemic started\b0\par\par\uc2 728 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2022\par 04:10\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY, Sept 30 (Reuters) - Asian shares on Friday were headed for the worst month since the onset of the COVID-19 pandemic, while jitters in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond markets persisted over hawkish talk from central banks, worries about global recession and rising geopolitical risk.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific shares outside Japan was largely flat on Friday, as a bounce in Hong Kong and among mainland Chinese bluechips offset declines elsewhere. Japan's Nikkei fell 1.6%.\par \par \uc2 Relief came from Chinese factory activity data that beat market expectations, with the manufacturing sector returning to growth in September after contracting for two months.\par \par \uc2 Still, the Asian index was set to record a staggering 12.5% drop for the month, the largest since March 2020 when the COVID-19 pandemic threw financial markets into chaos.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong shares were likely heading for their worst quarter since 2001 and Chinese bluechips might also finish September by recording their biggest quarterly loss since a stock market meltdown in 2015.\par \par \uc2 "The 'troubling triad' of rising rates, slowing growth and strong dollar have all intensified," said Timothy Moe, chief Asia-Pacific equity strategist at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We reduce our forecasts further and expect largely flat regional performance over the next two quarters with better returns on a 12-month view."\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, traders remained edgy amid risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  from central banks.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar was little changed against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 111.88 on Friday, after retreating 0.9% in the previous day.\par \par \uc2 However, it is up 2.9% for the month, the best since April. The relentless rise of the dollar has pushed the Japanese yen, Chinese yuan and many emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to record lows.\par \par \uc2 Traders are also wary of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from China and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Reuters reported that \uc2 China's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has asked major state-owned banks to be prepared to sell dollars for the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in offshore markets.\par \par \uc2 In Europe, Britain's gilt market has been roiled along with the pound by government plans for heavy borrowing to finance spending.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  said on Thursday she will stick to her plan to reignite economic growth, breaking her silence after nearly a week of financial market chaos.\par \par \uc2 German Chancellor \uc2 Olaf Scholz\uc2  also set out a 200 billion euro ($196 billion) "defensive shield", including a gas price brake and a cut in sales tax for the fuel, to protect companies and households from the impact of soaring energy prices.\par \par \uc2 That came as Europe braces for a double-digit inflation reading later in the day, as the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  voiced support for another big interest rate hike. German inflation accelerated to 10.9% this month, far beyond market expectations.\par \par \uc2 "Increased uncertainty and risks \u8211\'20\'13 and higher interest rates \u8211\'20\'13 logically see higher volatility in financial markets. Even G7 countries are now trading like emerging markets," said Jan Lambregts, head of global economics and markets research at \uc2 Rabobank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Indeed, markets now also see a far wider range of possible outcomes when it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rate movements."\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasuries stabilised somewhat after a renewed bout of selling on hawkish talks from \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials, with the yield on 10-year bonds up by 4 basis points in early Asia to 3.7815%.\par \par \uc2 The two-year Treasury yield also rose a similar amount to 4.2048%.\par \par \uc2 A strong U.S. jobs market with weekly jobless claims hitting a five-month low adds to the case of more aggressive tightening from the Fed. Overnight hawkish comments from Fed officials offered no indication that recent \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond market drama will lead the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to back off from its rate hike course.\par \par \uc2 Further weighing on market sentiment, Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  will begin annexing four Ukrainian regions to his country on Friday, a move the United Nations said would mark a "dangerous escalation" and jeopardise prospects for peace.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices were little changed in early trade on Friday. U.S. crude ticked up 0.11% to $81.32 a barrel while Brent crude rose to $88.51 per barrel.\par \par \uc2 Gold was slightly higher. Spot gold was traded at $1663.29 per ounce. ($1 = 1.0195 euros)\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Stella Qiu; Editing by Christopher Cushing) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-30T03:10:11.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220930ei9u009vl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
