\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 The euro will continue to be worth less than the dollar for most of 2023\b0\par\par\uc2 798 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's race to raise interest rates has driven a wedge between the price of money in the United States and the rest of the major Western economies, and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are reflecting this emphatically. The Fed is ahead of the curve, with rates in the 3%-3.25% range and expected to raise them to over 4.5% next year.\par \par \uc2 The aggressiveness of its monetary policy to combat inflation is cementing the strength of the dollar, which is unrivaled in its advance among Western \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The euro has depreciated by 15.3% over the year and the pound has plummeted by 20% over the year. Its plunge following the announcement of the UK tax cut has sharply exacerbated its weakness and has also shown that the fight against inflation and fiscal policies are having a powerful ripple effect on the volatile \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday was a calm day for the pound, which halted its slide. The euro also appreciated slightly against the dollar. In the short term, however, no change in trend is in sight and experts are betting that the dollar will continue to strengthen in the coming months. "We don't expect a turnaround in the euro's value against the dollar until the Fed has done its job, which doesn't look likely to happen until the first quarter of 2023 at the earliest," says \uc2 Chris Turner\uc2 , an analyst at ING. His forecast is for the euro to settle in the $0.97 area, with a tightening of \uc2 ECB\uc2  policy unlikely to make a difference, he says.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 , they also expect the dollar to remain strong in the near term and only depreciate very gradually as early as next year, when the Fed may start to be more concerned about growth than inflation. "In the meantime, new risks may drive the dollar to new highs," notes the US bank. Thus, the greenback not only has in its favor the rate gap that separates the\par \par \uc2 The greenback not only has the rate gap between the US and the euro zone in its favor, but also the energy crisis in Europe, which makes a recession in the Old Continent much more likely, and the safe-haven status that the US \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  enjoys in times of maximum uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 "Even after hitting new records, the dollar's strength has room to extend somewhat further, driven particularly by the global slowdown and the European energy crisis," says Thomas Hempell, head of macro and market analysis at Generali Investments Partners. \uc2 Citi\uc2  sees the euro below parity, at 0.98 dollars, until the end of the year and in a horizon of 6 to 12 months its value would appreciate to 1.05 dollars. The bank rules out that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could fall to 0.85 or 0.90 dollars and has 0.95 as the level for a bearish scenario. The end of the dollar rally would only come when global growth expectations have bottomed out, something that will be left for 2023.\par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  does not see the euro at $1.05 for at least a year. In the meantime, their estimate is $0.97 for the near term. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader Ebury has just revised its forecast for the dollar and although its estimate for this year is for the euro at parity with the dollar and its advance to $1.1 until the end of 2023, it acknowledges that the dollar will remain strong in the coming months. "Rates will be higher for longer than we thought, which will support the US \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," explains Itsaso Apezteguia, analyst at Ebury.\par \par \uc2 Japan pulls out all the stops with record daily yen buying. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's authorities have stepped up their artillery in defense of the yen, at a time when fears of economic recession are accentuating the dollar's strength to the detriment of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Japan bought more yen in Tuesday's session than during the entire time it was supporting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the 1998 crisis. In a single day, it bought 3.6 trillion yen (24.9 billion dollars), according to data published by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The amount thus exceeds the 3 trillion yen bought between April and June 1998.\par \par \uc2 Bonds. The Bank of Japan is reluctant to raise interest rates and is still holding them at -0.1%, an exception among developed economies. But it has decided to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in favor of the yen for the first time since 1998. Without rate hikes, Japanese debt is also catching the global rise in yields. In Tuesday's session, the Japanese 20-year bond rose above 1% for the first time since 2015.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220928ei9s003gb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
