\par\par \b \uc2 Malaysia's 2022 GDP Growth Likely to Hit 6.4%, \uc2 World Bank\uc2  Says -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,385 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2022\par 06:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0455 GMT - Malaysia's 2022 GDP growth is likely to hit 6.4%, says \uc2 World Bank\uc2  in a note. The economic growth will be driven by strong domestic demand due to continued improvements in labor market conditions. Both domestic and foreign travelers are expected to engage in more tourism-related activities, it says, adding that the continuation of multi-year investment projects will probably also support economic expansion. However, the \uc2 World Bank\uc2  says downside risks prevail, especially from the external front, citing a global growth slowdown, unfavorable financial conditions and continued supply chain disruptions. Besides, speculation over the next general election could raise investors' uncertainty in the near term, it says. \uc2 World Bank\uc2  forecasts Malaysia 2023 GDP growth of 4.2%. (yingxian.wong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0235 GMT - Most Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthen against the U.S. dollar in the Asian morning amid risk appetite, driven by gains in U.S. stock futures and due to a likely technical rebound following their recent weakness. However, a further rise in financial-market volatility could lead to more U.S. dollar strength due to its safe-haven status, Carol Kong, senior associate of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy and international economics at \uc2 CBA\uc2  , says in a research note. In particular, \uc2 Atlanta Fed\uc2  president \uc2 Raphael Bostic\uc2  said the rout in U.K. financial markets will put more stress on the U.S. economy, Kong notes. USD/KRW falls 0.2% to 1,427.45, USD/SGD drops 0.2% to 1.4361 and AUD/USD rises 0.5% to 0.6484. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0137 GMT - \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to refrain from selling U.S. Treasurys while undertaking its dollar-selling, yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Totan Research's President and economist Izuru Kato reckons. Instead, the ministry will likely use its $136 billion reserves deposited overseas and with BIS if the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  arises, he says. He estimates that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Sep. 22, its first in 24 years, totaled about Y3.6 trillion. The finance ministry is scheduled to release the final figure on Sep. 30. The dollar stood at Y144.40 on Tuesday, compared with Y145.90 before last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .(megumi.fujikawa@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0108 GMT - Singapore's FTSE Straits Times Index is 0.2% lower at 3174.97, weighed by bank and property stocks. "The aggressive moves by central banks have weighed on equities, with many investors now seeing increased odds of a recession," Phillip Securities analysts say in a note. Movements in the SGD will also be closely watched, after USD/SGD rose 70 pips Monday to 1.4380, a new high this year so far and the highest since April 2020, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analysts say in a note. DBS falls 0.5%, \uc2 OCBC\uc2  declines 0.3% and \uc2 UOB\uc2  is 0.9% lower. Among property developers, \uc2 City Developments\uc2  slips 0.1% and Hongkong Land falls 0.6%. (justina.lee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0039 GMT - Gold prices are higher in Asian trade, rebounding after settling at the lowest price in almost two-and-a-half years overnight. However, a stronger USD could continue to weigh on the precious metal. "The move in the dollar is not over and that should keep the pressure on bullion," Oanda senior market analyst \uc2 Edward Moya\uc2  says in a note. Meanwhile, ANZ analysts reckon that investors are the most bearish on gold in almost four years as central banks increase interest rates to tame inflation. "Its status as a haven asset in times of economic distress has failed to stem the flow of selling," they say in a note. Spot gold rises 0.4% to $1,629.23/oz. (justina.lee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0015 GMT - Japanese stocks are higher in morning trade, led by gains in auto and electronics stocks following recent selloffs caused by concerns about policy tightening by major central banks. \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  is up 1.7% and \uc2 TDK\uc2  is 1.8% higher. Investors are closely watching the yen and Japanese government officials' comments on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . USD/JPY is at 144.33 compared with 143.84 as of Monday's Tokyo stock-market close. The Nikkei Stock Average is up 0.7% at 26620.10. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 2349 GMT - Japanese stocks may trade rangebound amid continuing uncertainty over policy tightening by major central banks and the economic outlook. Nikkei futures open up 125 points at 26350 on the SGX following Monday's selloffs. Some financial stocks may outperform following gains in \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields overnight. Investors are closely watching the yen and Japanese government officials' comments on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . USD/JPY is at 144.58, compared with 143.84 as of Monday's Tokyo stock market close. The Nikkei Stock Average closed 2.7% lower at 26431.55 on Monday.(kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2126 GMT - Canadian stocks edge lower, with the benchmark S&P/TSX Composite Index down 153.94 points, or 0.8%, to 18327.04 and the blue-chip S&P/TSX 60 Index down 8 points, or 0.7%, to 1114.61. Shares of \uc2 NioCorp Developments\uc2  rise 15% to C$1.21 after the company said it has agreed to merge with special purpose acquisition company \uc2 GX Acquisition Corp. II\uc2  . Shares of \uc2 Bank of Nova Scotia\uc2  fall 2.8% to C$67.30 after the bank said Chief Executive \uc2 Brian Porter\uc2  will retire on Jan. 31 and will be succeeded by \uc2 Scott Thomson\uc2  . Thomson is the CEO of \uc2 Finning International\uc2  . (kathryn.hardison@wsj.com; @knhardison)\par \par \uc2 2102 GMT - The IPC stock index closes down 1.3% at 44808 points and the peso weakens to 20.40 against the US dollar from 20.20 Friday in a broad bout of global risk aversion. Low-cost airline Volaris shares fall 7.8%, and cement maker \uc2 Cemex\uc2  shares close down 2.7%. Statistics institute Inegi reports a 0.4% rise in July economic activity, which picked up after small declines in May and June. "This is a good precedent for 3Q GDP as it lowers significantly the likelihood of a contraction as is happening in other parts of the world," Monex analyst Marcos Daniel Arias says in a note. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2037 GMT - The two-year rises 0.103 percentage point to 4.315%, the highest since August 2007, and the 10-year gains 0.183 pp to 3.878%, the highest since April 2010. Data due Tuesday are expected to show signs that monetary tightening is working. Analysts expect August durable goods orders and new home sales and July home prices all to have slowed down from previous readings. Odds remain high that the Fed will raise rates by 75 basis point in November. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 1942 GMT - A group of Trader Joe's employees in New York say they filed with a federal agency to hold a union election. The workers at the Williamsburg store say they informed management of their intent to unionize and that they are petitioning to do so with Trader Joe's United, an independent labor organization. If the effort is successful, the Williamsburg store would become the third unionized location at the grocery chain after Hadley, Mass. and Minneapolis. The \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  said its Brooklyn office is making sure that it received union authorization cards from at least 30% of the unit before proceeding with an election. The company did not respond to requests for comment. (jaewon.kang@wsj.com; @_jaewonkang)\par \par \uc2 1920 GMT - The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is expected to keep monetary policy tight until the second half of next year, S&P Global Ratings says. S&P says the Fed is now likely to push rates to 400-425 basis points by early next year. Rate cuts are expected late next year "as its soft landing turns into a hard one with prices softening on weak demand," S&P says. \uc2 Beth Ann Bovino\uc2  , S&P's US Chief Economist, said "While our baseline now includes a shallow recession, we can't rule out chances of an even harder landing." (stephen.nakrosis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 27, 2022 00:55 ET (04:55 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220927ei9r0004s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
