\par\par \b \uc2 Japan May Refrain from Selling U.S. Treasurys -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,413 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2022\par 03:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 2137 ET - \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to refrain from selling U.S. Treasurys while undertaking its dollar-selling, yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Totan Research's President and economist Izuru Kato reckons. Instead, the ministry will likely use its $136 billion reserves deposited overseas and with BIS if the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  arises, he says. He estimates that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Sep. 22, its first in 24 years, totaled about Y3.6 trillion. The finance ministry is scheduled to release the final figure on Sep. 30. The dollar stood at Y144.40 on Tuesday, compared with Y145.90 before last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .(megumi.fujikawa@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2108 ET - Singapore's FTSE Straits Times Index is 0.2% lower at 3174.97, weighed by bank and property stocks. "The aggressive moves by central banks have weighed on equities, with many investors now seeing increased odds of a recession," Phillip Securities analysts say in a note. Movements in the SGD will also be closely watched, after USD/SGD rose 70 pips Monday to 1.4380, a new high this year so far and the highest since April 2020, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analysts say in a note. DBS falls 0.5%, \uc2 OCBC\uc2  declines 0.3% and \uc2 UOB\uc2  is 0.9% lower. Among property developers, \uc2 City Developments\uc2  slips 0.1% and Hongkong Land falls 0.6%. (justina.lee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2039 ET - Gold prices are higher in Asian trade, rebounding after settling at the lowest price in almost two-and-a-half years overnight. However, a stronger USD could continue to weigh on the precious metal. "The move in the dollar is not over and that should keep the pressure on bullion," Oanda senior market analyst \uc2 Edward Moya\uc2  says in a note. Meanwhile, ANZ analysts reckon that investors are the most bearish on gold in almost four years as central banks increase interest rates to tame inflation. "Its status as a haven asset in times of economic distress has failed to stem the flow of selling," they say in a note. Spot gold rises 0.4% to $1,629.23/oz. (justina.lee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2015 ET - Japanese stocks are higher in morning trade, led by gains in auto and electronics stocks following recent selloffs caused by concerns about policy tightening by major central banks. \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  is up 1.7% and \uc2 TDK\uc2  is 1.8% higher. Investors are closely watching the yen and Japanese government officials' comments on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . USD/JPY is at 144.33 compared with 143.84 as of Monday's Tokyo stock-market close. The Nikkei Stock Average is up 0.7% at 26620.10. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 1949 ET - Japanese stocks may trade rangebound amid continuing uncertainty over policy tightening by major central banks and the economic outlook. Nikkei futures open up 125 points at 26350 on the SGX following Monday's selloffs. Some financial stocks may outperform following gains in \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields overnight. Investors are closely watching the yen and Japanese government officials' comments on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . USD/JPY is at 144.58, compared with 143.84 as of Monday's Tokyo stock market close. The Nikkei Stock Average closed 2.7% lower at 26431.55 on Monday.(kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1726 ET - Canadian stocks edge lower, with the benchmark S&P/TSX Composite Index down 153.94 points, or 0.8%, to 18327.04 and the blue-chip S&P/TSX 60 Index down 8 points, or 0.7%, to 1114.61. Shares of \uc2 NioCorp Developments\uc2  rise 15% to C$1.21 after the company said it has agreed to merge with special purpose acquisition company \uc2 GX Acquisition Corp. II\uc2  . Shares of \uc2 Bank of Nova Scotia\uc2  fall 2.8% to C$67.30 after the bank said Chief Executive \uc2 Brian Porter\uc2  will retire on Jan. 31 and will be succeeded by \uc2 Scott Thomson\uc2  . Thomson is the CEO of \uc2 Finning International\uc2  . (kathryn.hardison@wsj.com; @knhardison)\par \par \uc2 1702 ET - The IPC stock index closes down 1.3% at 44808 points and the peso weakens to 20.40 against the US dollar from 20.20 Friday in a broad bout of global risk aversion. Low-cost airline Volaris shares fall 7.8%, and cement maker \uc2 Cemex\uc2  shares close down 2.7%. Statistics institute Inegi reports a 0.4% rise in July economic activity, which picked up after small declines in May and June. "This is a good precedent for 3Q GDP as it lowers significantly the likelihood of a contraction as is happening in other parts of the world," Monex analyst Marcos Daniel Arias says in a note. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1637 ET - The two-year rises 0.103 percentage point to 4.315%, the highest since August 2007, and the 10-year gains 0.183 pp to 3.878%, the highest since April 2010. Data due Tuesday are expected to show signs that monetary tightening is working. Analysts expect August durable goods orders and new home sales and July home prices all to have slowed down from previous readings. Odds remain high that the Fed will raise rates by 75 basis point in November. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 1542 ET - A group of Trader Joe's employees in New York say they filed with a federal agency to hold a union election. The workers at the Williamsburg store say they informed management of their intent to unionize and that they are petitioning to do so with Trader Joe's United, an independent labor organization. If the effort is successful, the Williamsburg store would become the third unionized location at the grocery chain after Hadley, Mass. and Minneapolis. The \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  said its Brooklyn office is making sure that it received union authorization cards from at least 30% of the unit before proceeding with an election. The company did not respond to requests for comment. (jaewon.kang@wsj.com; @_jaewonkang)\par \par \uc2 1520 ET - The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is expected to keep monetary policy tight until the second half of next year, S&P Global Ratings says. S&P says the Fed is now likely to push rates to 400-425 basis points by early next year. Rate cuts are expected late next year "as its soft landing turns into a hard one with prices softening on weak demand," S&P says. \uc2 Beth Ann Bovino\uc2  , S&P's US Chief Economist, said "While our baseline now includes a shallow recession, we can't rule out chances of an even harder landing." (stephen.nakrosis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1512 ET - S&P Global Ratings says it expects US GDP to grow 1.6% for 2022 and 0.2% for 2023, "as the economy falls into a shallow recession in the first half of the year." According to S&P, the jobs market is still tight, and it expects the unemployment rate to reach 4.8% by the end of next year and peak at 5.7% by early 2025. The rate will hold above 5% through 2026, according to S&P. The agency also said inflation likely peaked in third quarter of this year, "but will remain high on continued supply-chain disruptions." Core prices, excluding food and fuel, is expected to stay above the Fed's 2.0% target until the first quarter of 2024, according to S&P. (stephen.nakrosis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1504 ET - Local pension funds in the US saw "extraordinary investment returns" in 2021, but several factors will likely lead to a need for higher contributions in the future, S&P Global Ratings said. In a report, S&P said "market declines in 2022 and the threat of a recession will likely lead to the need for increased future contributions in most cases." Stephen Doyle , a credit analyst with S&P, said "Many of the largest local pension plans across the U.S. have lowered their assumed rates of return in recent years, but this decision may lag current average market return rates." Last year, "outsized fiscal investment returns" increased funded ratios for the 20 largest U.S. cities, to a median of 78.5% from a median of 71.5%, S&P said. It added "much of these funding gains will likely be negated in fiscal 2022." (stephen.nakrosis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 26, 2022 21:37 ET (01:37 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220927ei9r00091\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
