\par\par \b \uc2 Japan May Refrain from Selling U.S. Treasurys -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 136 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 September 2022\par 03:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0137 GMT - \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to refrain from selling U.S. Treasurys while undertaking its dollar-selling, yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Totan Research's President and economist Izuru Kato reckons. Instead, the ministry will likely use its $136 billion reserves deposited overseas and with BIS if the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  arises, he says. He estimates that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Sep. 22, its first in 24 years, totaled about Y3.6 trillion. The finance ministry is scheduled to release the final figure on Sep. 30. The dollar stood at Y144.40 on Tuesday, compared with Y145.90 before last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .(megumi.fujikawa@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 26, 2022 21:37 ET (01:37 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220927ei9r0003l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
