\par\par \b \uc2 GRAPHIC-Take Five:  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  watch is here\b0\par\par\uc2 841 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2022\par 07:57\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Updates paragraph 1 and theme five to reflect weekend events)\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Sept 23 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are hogging the market spotlight with sterling at record lows and the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  having \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week to buy yen for the first time since 1998.\par \par \uc2 Markets remain on watch for any signs of a ratcheting up in tensions between Russia and the West. And election results from Italy, euro area inflation numbers and U.S. and Chinese data also give investors plenty to chew over.\par \par \uc2 Here's a look at the week ahead in markets from Kevin Buckland in Tokyo, Tom Westbrook in Sydney, Lewis Krauskopf in New York, Danilo Masoni in Milan, and Dhara Ranasinghe and Karin Strohecker in London. Graphics by Vincent Flasseur, Vineet Sachdev and Pasit Kongkunakornkul.\par \par \uc2 1/ DOLLAR WHEN?\par \par \uc2 Japan's authorities finally had enough of a weak yen and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem a sharp decline against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 But will it work? The runaway greenback is up more than 20% on the yen this year, and some doubt it's got much left in the tank. But as U.S. rates rise, Japan's are stuck just below 0% and unlikely to budge.\par \par \uc2 So the case for a strong dollar remains. Japan, alongside neighbours China and Korea also pushing back on the dollar, may find itself fighting fundamentals, the market and the Fed.\par \par \uc2 Dealers in Seoul suspect authorities have already been selling dollars, but the won keeps sliding. Likewise, China's yuan has forged new lows although the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has pushed back via the trading band. Friday's China PMI readings, if disappointing, could add to the bear case.\par \par \uc2 2/ 'NOT A BLUFF'\par \par \uc2 Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's military mobilisation order, threats to use nuclear weapons and a push to annex swaths of Ukrainian territory mark a new stage in the seven-month old conflict.\par \par \uc2 The announcements - coinciding with the diplomatic highlight of the year that is the \uc2 UN General Assembly\uc2  meeting - were condemned globally and triggered fresh protests in Russia, where draft-age Russians headed abroad to escape Moscow's biggest conscription drive since World War Two. Russia calls its actions in Ukraine "a special military \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 ".\par \par \uc2 The latest escalation has reverberated across markets: oil prices are sharply higher, raising the spectre of more pain on the energy front for Europe. Meanwhile, \uc2 European Union\uc2  foreign ministers are readying another package of sanctions - their eighth one - which could be formalised in mid-October.\par \par \uc2 3/ RED HOT\par \par \uc2 The "flash" estimate of September euro area consumer price data is out on Friday and should show inflation at a fresh record high above 9%.\par \par \uc2 Investors have already ramped up expectations for another 75 bps, \uc2 ECB\uc2  rate hike in October, so the data shouldn't change the near-term rate outlook.\par \par \uc2 Yet any signs that underlying price pressures are broadening out, could further push up expectations for where rates in the bloc end up. The \uc2 ECB\uc2  is increasingly hawkish in its rhetoric and some \uc2 ECB\uc2  watchers say a mega 100 bps rate hike cannot be ruled out in coming months. Indeed, that's what \uc2 Sweden's Riksbank\uc2  just did, as did the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  in July.\par \par \uc2 4/ TESTING TIMES\par \par \uc2 Can the U.S. consumer defy sizzling inflation and rising borrowing costs? Tuesday's consumer confidence measure will indicate how this key pillar of the economy is holding up.\par \par \uc2 Last month, the \uc2 Conference Board\uc2 's overall consumer confidence index rebounded to 103.2, ending three straight monthly declines. This month's index is expected to come in at 104, a Reuters poll suggests.\par \par \uc2 In one positive sign, data earlier this month showed U.S. retail sales unexpectedly rebounded in August as Americans ramped up purchases of motor vehicles and dined out more thanks to lower gasoline prices.\par \par \uc2 But with stock markets faltering and bond yields climbing, whether consumers remain upbeat is yet to be seen -- especially given a Fed intent on bringing inflation down even at the expense of a sharp slowdown in growth.\par \par \uc2 5/ NO DRAMA HERE?\par \par \uc2 Giorgia Meloni\uc2  looks set to become Italy's first female prime minister at the head of its most right-wing government since World War Two after leading a conservative alliance to triumph at Sunday's election.\par \par \uc2 Provisional results showed the rightist bloc should have a strong majority in both houses of parliament, potentially giving Italy a rare chance of political stability after years of upheaval and fragile coalitions.\par \par \uc2 There had been little visible stress in markets ahead of the election since Meloni has embraced an EU-friendly face, reassuring investors.\par \par \uc2 Italy's 10-year bond yield gap over Germany has widened from the post-pandemic lows but is far from levels seen in 2018.\par \par \uc2 However, Meloni and her allies face a daunting list of challenges, including soaring energy prices, the impact of war in Ukraine and renewed slowdown in the euro zone's third-largest economy.\par \par \uc2 (Compiled by Dhara Ranasinghe; Editing by Ana Nicolaci da Costa and Kenneth Maxwell)\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-29T05:57:33.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220926ei9q00ted\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
