\par\par \b \uc2 Heard on the Street:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Buys Some Time -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 513 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 September 2022\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B10\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is probably aimed at slowing its depreciation \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Jacky Wong \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not prop up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for long.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen jumped 1.4% against the dollar Thursday after the government confirmed that it bought the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the first time since the Asian financial crisis in 1998. It has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  many times since to sell yen -- but with oil prices still high and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  now trading at 143 yen to the dollar, near its weakest in decades, the bank apparently felt it needed to send a shot across bearish traders' bows.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  materialized as the yen came under further pressure when the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  declined to tweak its ultralow rates regime Thursday following its two-day monetary policy meeting. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  had increased interest rates by 0.75% Wednesday and signaled its clear intention to keep raising.\par \par \uc2 With $1.3 trillion in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, Tokyo could clearly keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  for quite a while at its discretion. That creates some uncertainty for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders, which could slow the pace of depreciation. But ultimately, the yen's decline is driven by the interest-rate gap between Japan and the U.S. and a shrinking current account surplus. As long as the monetary policies in the two countries remain divergent, government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to fundamentally change the direction of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Moreover Japan's monetary policy is likely to remain a dovish outlier for a while longer yet. Japan's inflation has picked up to levels not seen in decades, but it's still relatively low by global standards. Japanese consumer prices grew 3% year over year in August, but mostly that reflects imported energy and food prices. Excluding energy and fresh food, inflation was only 1.6%. Japan doesn't want to cripple its domestic recovery by raising rates too early, especially when oil prices have started to decline in recent months. The main risk is that a continually weaker yen pushes up import costs for both businesses and consumers even higher.\par \par \uc2 That's probably what the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ultimately aimed at: slowing, rather than halting \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation until the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  feels comfortable raising rates. In other words, buying time for the recovery with targeted rear-guard actions to prevent a further free fall of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 If oil prices keep falling, that will probably end up looking prudent. If they rise again, and pricey food and oil start really crimping the recovery itself, the bank will have some tougher choices to make -- particularly since a high oil price scenario probably would entail a nastier recession in Japan's key export markets too.\par \par \uc2 Write to Jacky Wong at jacky.wong@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (September 26, 2022).\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 26, 2022 02:32 ET (06:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220926ei9q000fk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
