\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Forex Intervention \highlight\uc2  May Be Able to Keep USD/JPY Below 145 -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,547 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2022\par 03:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 2136 ET - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if sustained, may be able to keep USD/JPY from rising above 145, as the headwind for the yen as a result of U.S.-Japan interest rate differentials will likely abate eventually, Capital Economics economists Thomas Mathews and \uc2 Jonas Goltermann\uc2  say in a research note. In the near term, divergent monetary stances seem set to keep piling downward pressure on the yen, it says. Yet, with inflationary pressures starting to ease in the U.S., \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  expects the Fed to be cutting rates by the end of next year, and that U.S. yields will begin falling ahead of that as the Fed turns less hawkish. USD/JPY is at 141.91. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 2133 ET - Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthen against the U.S. dollar in the early Asian session, in a likely technical rebound after their broad weakness overnight and after some central banks in the region raised rates on Thursday. There could be calmer \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets today after all of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves and updates, say \uc2 MUFG Bank\uc2  analysts in a research report. However, USD strength may be difficult to avoid in the near term, they add. USD/SGD edges 0.1% lower to 1.4176 and USD/PHP falls 0.5% to 58.37 while AUD/USD gains 0.1% at 0.6652. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2132 ET - \uc2 Nomura\uc2  cuts its China 2023 GDP forecast to 4.3% from 5.1%, while keeping its forecast for 2022 unchanged at 2.7%. The investment bank says China will likely keep its zero-Covid policy until March 2023, when the government completes its political reshuffles. China's exports might be much weaker next year on a global slowdown and post-Covid return to normalcy. The continued property downturn and the likely pullback of car sales after policies to encourage sales this year may weigh on growth in 2023, it says. \uc2 Nomura\uc2  also adjusts its forecasts for 3Q and 4Q growth this year to 2.8% and 2.8%, respectively, from 2.6% and 3.3% previously. The upward revision of 3Q growth is due to better-than-expected activity data in August. (singaporeeditors@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1708 ET - US non-investment grade finance companies are benefiting from the relatively long maturities of their unsecured debt, according to \uc2 Moody's\uc2  . About half of a total of $55.8B in unsecured debt held by such companies matures in 2027 or later, compared to 29% for their investment-grade peers, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  says. "Many of the US non-investment grade finance companies we rate took advantage of the very accommodative issuance market of the last several years to extend their maturity runways," the credit-rating firm says. That can give those companies some leeway as rising interest rates raise the risk that they may have difficulty refinancing their unsecured debt, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  adds. (luis.garcia@wsj.com; @lhvgarcia)\par \par \uc2 1635 ET - The IPC stock index closes down 1.4% at 46324 points, while the peso strengthens to 19.93 against the US dollar from 20.02 yesterday. The Bank of Mexico placed MXN23.5B in government securities for monetary regulation purposes at an auction for up to MXN30B. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is seen raising its overnight interest rate target by 75bps at its Sept. 29 meeting, matching the Fed's move this week in the federal funds rate. Mexico's mid-September inflation at 8.76%, and core CPI at 8.27%, support views of a third consecutive 75bp increase in the rate to 9.25%. "We also now expect a bit more tightening in this cycle than before and think that the policy rate will reach 10.50% by early next year," Capital Economics senior emerging markets economist \uc2 Jason Tuvey\uc2  says in a note. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1549 ET - Investors keep dumping US Treasurys, sending yields to multiyear highs. The two-year gains 0.131 percentage point to 4.124% and the 10-year rises 0.194 pp to 3.705%. The main drive is still Wednesday's 75-basis point rate increase by the Fed and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unexpectedly hawkish outlook, which shuffled markets estimates for yields. "The most impactful part of [the FOMC meeting] was the hawkish projections of 4.4% and 4.6% median expected Fed Funds rate by year-end 2022 and 2023," \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  's Yuri Seliger says in a research report. Markets expect another 75 bp hike in November, with recession looming in the horizon. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 1326 ET - A day after the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  pushed up its short-term target rate by a large 0.75 percentage point, money market funds and others handed over $2.359 trillion into the \uc2 New York Fed\uc2  's reverse repo facility. The facility saw a record $2.329 trillion in inflows on June 30, which was a quarter end and subject to special factors. Fed officials have long argued that reverse repos would shrink over time as short-term rates rise and private sector money market investments look more attractive, but for now, eligible firms still prefer the safety and easy returns of the reverse-repo tool. (michael.derby@wsj.com) Corrections and Amplifications\par \par \uc2 This was corrected at 1:55 p.m. ET because it incorrectly gave the amount as $2.315 trillion and incorrectly said it was a near record. Money-market funds and others handed over a record $2.359 trillion into the \uc2 New York Fed\uc2  's reverse-repo facility today.\par \par \uc2 1306 ET - Brazilian presidential candidate \uc2 Luiz Inacio Lula da Silva\uc2  , popularly known as Lula, will likely govern as more of a pragmatist than his campaign speeches indicate, says Robert Wood, economist for Latin America at the Economist Intelligence Unit, in a research note. Polls put da Silva, who served two consecutive terms as president from 2003-2010, comfortably ahead of current President \uc2 Jair Bolsonaro\uc2  in the October elections. Da Silva's choice of the center-right former governor of Sao Paulo, Geraldo Alckmin, as his running mate is a strong signal da Silva plans to work with a broad political base, Wood says. (jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1246 ET - The supply of gilts is expected to be much higher than previously thought due to the government's plan on fiscal expansion, \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  says in a webinar following the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  's interest-rate announcement. The result is likely to be higher gilt yields, higher government debt and a weakening pound. "More borrowing comes with more risk in an environment where interest rates and inflation are rising," \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  says, adding that, "a commitment to fiscal discipline in the medium term [in the minibudget] will probably be a good start." The London based consultancy expects gilt yields to end lower in 2023 than they are now. (miriam.mukuru@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1228 ET - Shanghai, the world's busiest port, plans to build a new container terminal to extend the Yangshan Deepwater Port that will cost 50 billion yuan ($7B) and will handle around 12M containers annually. The \uc2 Shanghai International Port Group\uc2  , that runs the port, said the project will help to move more containers from river to sea at the Yangtze River Delta region, but also warned of rising geopolitical uncertainties, protectionism and anti-globalization that could damage ocean trade. Shanghai Port moved more than 47M containers in 2021. (costas.paris@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1223 ET - Chairman Powell saying yesterday rates have to go a lot higher boosted the dollar, \uc2 Scott Colyer\uc2  , CEO of Advisors Asset Management, tells WSJ. "Powell is a hard liner and he only cares about bringing down inflation, he says. "There's a war in Europe driving wealth to safety. The euro isn't a place people want to keep wealth if war breaks out on the continent," he says. "It's almost the stars aligning" for the dollar, Colyer says. Today the dollar is slightly softer against the euro and 1.2% weaker against the yen. The WSJ Dollar Index is down 0.1% after yesterday hitting its highest level on record going back to 2001, according to Dow Jones Market Data. (jonathan.vuocolo@wsj.com; @jonvuocolo)\par \par \uc2 1210 ET - Investor confidence hits a 27-year low as soaring energy prices and the cost-of-living crisis weigh, \uc2 Hargreaves Lansdown\uc2  says. HL's monthly investor-confidence index, based on a survey of 6,000 of the financial-services firm's clients, hit its lowest this month since it began in May 1995, falling by 27 points to 44--lower than the 52 it hit in December 2018. "Despite periods of under-performance often being some of the best opportunities to buy into markets, Investment Association fund flows show 2022 has seen some of the worst outflows on record," HL's Emma Wall writes. "All eyes will be on central banks and governments to provide a measured response to the crisis--particularly the new \uc2 U.K. government\uc2  as it announces a mini-Budget Friday." (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 22, 2022 21:36 ET (01:36 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220923ei9n0006g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
