  Looking Ahead - Economic Data (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 12:30 CA Jul Retail Sales MM -2.0% f'cast, 1.1% prev \u8226\'20\'22 12:30 CA Jul Retail Sales Ex-Autos MM -1.2% f'cast, 0.8% prev \u8226\'20\'22 13:45 US Sep S&P Global Mfg PMI Flash 51.1 f'cast, 51.5 prev \u8226\'20\'22 13:45 US Sep S&P Global Svcs PMI Flash 45.0 f'cast, 43.7 prev \u8226\'20\'22 13:45 US Sep S&P Global Comp Flash PMI 44.6 prev Looking Ahead - Events, Other Releases (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 18:00 Fed's Powell gives opening remarks at the "Fed Listens: Transitioning to the Post-pandemic Economy" event \u8226\'20\'22 18:05 Fed's Brainard and Bowman will each moderate conversations with leaders from different organizations at the Fed event Commentary and Analysis Next EUR/USD target is 0.9500 but drop could be much deeper \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD has exceeded tech targets around 0.9800 reaching 0.9770 EBS \u8226\'20\'22 Next targets are 0.9575 (daily) 0.9485 (weekly) and 0.9499 (monthly) \u8226\'20\'22 While EUR/USD has plummeted due Fed policy traders reluctant to sell \u8226\'20\'22 Short positions have never been large and were slashed after \uc2 ECB\uc2  hiked \u8226\'20\'22 Traders ill-prepared for the decline that has subsequently extended \u8226\'20\'22 Without the restraint of a large short EUR/USD could fall far \u8226\'20\'22 Targets between 0.8500-0.9000 could be met before Fed stops hiking \u8226\'20\'22 Emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  reach decisive point USD/JPY rebound struggles to remain above a key Fibo in London \u8226\'20\'22 \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  saw USD/JPY drop from 145.90 to 140.31, on Thursday \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY has since made a decent recovery as \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders fight back \u8226\'20\'22 Spot has risen from Asia's 141.77, to hit 143.26, before slipping back \u8226\'20\'22 It has struggled to sustain trading back above 143.11 Fibo in the s-term \u8226\'20\'22 143.11 Fibo is a 50% retrace of the 145.90 to 140.31 (EBS) drop \u8226\'20\'22 Dollar is becoming like a runaway train \u8226\'20\'22 A bigger USD/JPY rebound likely, Japan's actions likely futile \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/JPY's correlation with USD/JPY is high, 30/60-day logs are above +0.50 GBP/USD drops below 1.11 as gilt yields soar on Kwarteng \u8226\'20\'22 GBP/USD falls to 1.1075, new 37-year low, as gilt yields surge \u8226\'20\'22 Tax cuts and borrowing define UK's new economic agenda under Truss/Kwarteng \u8226\'20\'22 1.1157 was low before Kwarteng set out his plans to MPs \u8226\'20\'22 'Desperate gamble', opposition Labour Party criticises Tory government plans \u8226\'20\'22 1.1213 (Thursday's low) is now a GBP/USD resistance level \u8226\'20\'22 1.1365 was Thursday's high (just before the \uc2 BoE\uc2  raised rates by 50 bps) AUD/USD falls to new 28-month low as European shares slide \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD drops to 0.6565 (new 28-month low) as European shares extend losses \u8226\'20\'22 AUD is risk sensitive; USD is safe-haven \u8226\'20\'22 0.6574 was Thursday's low, before \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  lifted AUD/USD \u8226\'20\'22 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 -spurred lift topped out at 0.6670 (last week's low, Sept 16) \u8226\'20\'22 0.6655 was Asia high, when USD/JPY fell to 141.73 EBS low Dollar is becoming like a runaway train The U.S. dollar's rise seems unstoppable in weeks ahead due to a combination of fundamental and technical factors, with very little preventing further strength in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  lifted rates by 75 bps on Wednesday, vaulting the USD index, which tracks the dollar versus a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to a two-decade high. The USD index continues to climb further above the 109.14 Fibo, a 76.4% retrace of the 121.02 to 70.698 (2001-2008) drop. That has increased the odds for much bigger gains, which could well test the psychologically key 115.00 level in the weeks ahead. Fourteen-week momentum remains positive, reinforcing the underlying bullish market structure. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up a freefalling yen has \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors speculating about which \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could move next in the face of a soaring dollar. However, any action by other central banks would likely be confined to verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which would not be enough to curtail the greenback's rise. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3fcdb14"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  Time to gamble? GBP/USD and the 1.00 question The chance of cable dropping to 1.00 for the first time will rise if King Dollar continues to reign supreme and confidence in British assets deteriorates under the new government led by Liz Truss. GBP/USD has never traded at parity, but got very close to that level in 1985, with 1.0075 mooted as the record low. Cable slumped to 1.1075 on Friday, its lowest level since 1985, after Truss's finance minister \uc2 Kwasi Kwarteng\uc2  announced he was slashing taxes to try to drive economic growth. Earlier this week, the \uc2 IFS\uc2  think tank said "setting plans underpinned by the idea that tax cuts will deliver a sustained boost to growth is a gamble, at best". UK minister Simon Clarke responded Friday, saying "this isn't a gamble, the weight of history and evidence is with us". Friday also saw September's flash UK PMI readings come in sub-50, marking a contraction in activity, including in the services sector - the dominant segment of the UK economy. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3DNydgX"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc