\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Volcker doctrine prevails worldwide despite recession risk\b0\par\par\uc2 481 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 23 SEP 2022 - 07:53CEST Central banks around the world have adopted, for the better, the Volcker doctrine to manage their monetary policies: first things first, and the first thing is to ensure price stability within reasonable ranges, and to prevent inflation from destroying present savings and future incomes. Paul Volcker headed the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  from 1979 to 1987, first appointed by President Carter and later confirmed by Reagan, and he did not hesitate to raise interest rates as much as necessary to defeat inflation, even if it meant pain for the indebted and paralysis for new investment projects. In the end, he came out on top and restored the economy to one of the longest and most intense cycles of growth, albeit aided by the high levels of openness and globalization of activity in the world in the 1990s.\par \par \uc2 The current Chairman of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , has also made it clear that this Volckerian catechism must be complied with, and has warned that he will continue to raise rates as much as necessary to put inflation back in the cage it should never have left, and to which they had partly contributed to fatten in recent years with decisions of excessive quantitative easing to suffocate the financial crisis of ten years ago. In the view of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , and apparently that of the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and even the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  and Japan, growth and welfare must be sacrificed to curb a greater evil, such as inflation, which, if not defeated, could send economies into a spiral much more destructive than the simple increase in the cost of financing, however severe it may be.\par \par \uc2 The market is already discounting that in this war spiral against the common enemy, the US will raise rates to 4.5% this year and Europe to 3%, while the UK already has them at 2.25%; and they will do so despite the certain risk of recession in each and every one of these economies, which has intensified with Russia's military decisions to prolong the conflict in Ukraine, and whose end no one dares to predict. In fact, the modest rise in rates in Switzerland and the persistence of negative rates in Japan have caused money to abandon the yen and the Swiss franc (\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has had to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in defense of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time this century) because the nominal remuneration of the dollar or the euro area is unbeatable, although in real terms it remains poor in the short term. With these attitudes, one can bet that inflation will return sooner than expected, although its nature as a prolonged phenomenon is not disputed by any \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Firmness against inflation is the best ally of healthy growth, even if the recessionary desert is inevitable.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220923ei9n00b16\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
