\par\par \b \uc2 Central Banks Scramble To Raise Rates -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 1,179 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2022\par 08:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par A1\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  and its counterparts across the globe take steps to fight inflation \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par By Will Horner and Megumi Fujikawa \f28 \par \par \uc2 LONDON -- Central banks around the world moved Thursday to combat the effects of a soaring dollar and rising inflation, joining the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  in risking a recession to rein in climbing prices.\par \par \uc2 In a flurry of \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meetings from Norway to South Africa, many raised rates by larger-than-expected margins in a day that analysts at ING billed as "Super Thursday."\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  raised its key interest rate for the seventh consecutive time on Thursday. Before the news came out, the British pound briefly touched its lowest point in 37 years against the dollar before recovering some of its losses to reach $1.13.\par \par \uc2 Even some countries that didn't move rates -- the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  left its policy rate at its previous low level -- took other action to ease the growing inflation pressure.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Thursday it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to sell dollars and buy yen, the first such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 24 years, to slow the recent fall in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The yen fell to 145.87 to the dollar, its weakest level since 1998, before the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It then surged to hit 141 yen, though still far off the 115 yen mark at which the dollar was trading earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2  of Japan later said the government would act again if needed, without indicating the size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "Although \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates in principle should be determined in the market, we cannot stand by idly when speculative and excessive moves repeatedly occur," he said.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meetings, mostly pre-scheduled, came after the Fed announced its 0.75-point increase the day before and capped a bustling week of global monetary-policy tightening. Many \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials struggling with a crisis of public confidence after initially arguing that inflationary rises would be temporary, are now racing to raise interest rates to catch up with soaring prices, but not so fast that they trigger unnecessary economic pain.\par \par \uc2 Switzerland's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  joined the stampede toward higher rates by announcing an interest-rate increase that will put its benchmark lending rate above 0% for the first time since 2014, bringing an end to Europe's last remaining experiment in setting negative interest rates. \uc2 Sweden's Riksbank\uc2  lifted rates by 1 percentage point earlier this week, its largest increase in almost three decades.\par \par \uc2 The outlier was Turkey, which appeared unconcerned with the spreading inflation threat. Its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its benchmark interest rate to 12% from 13%, despite inflation surpassing 80% in August and prompting a renewed slide in the value of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . President Recep \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  of Turkey has long pressured the bank to keep interest rates low and adhere to his contrarian views that high interest rates encourage rather than prevent inflation. Turkey's lira then fell to another record low.\par \par \uc2 Among the nine members of the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 's monetary-policy committee, five voted for the half-point rate increase to 2.25%, while three voted for a larger three-quarter-point jump, and one other pushed for a smaller quarter-point rate rise. The split views highlight the competing concerns and conflicting economic signals \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials the world over are facing, but which are particularly pronounced in the U.K. as it is wrestling with its worst inflation increase in roughly four decades.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials are particularly worried about how higher interest rates might buffer the nation's economy and exacerbate a cost-of-living crisis.\par \par \uc2 The latest economic data pointed to tentative signs that inflation in the U.K. was slowing, but also presented weaker-than-expected readings on gross domestic product. As in the U.S., a tight labor market and low unemployment have been a source of strength despite broad economic weakness.\par \par \uc2 In coming to their decision Thursday, \uc2 BOE\uc2  officials eschewed the option of a larger rate rise, which some were expecting. The bank has continued to exhibit greater caution in the fight against inflation than central bankers elsewhere who are increasingly following the Fed's strategy of lifting interest rates by 0.75 percentage point or more at a time.\par \par \uc2 "They are walking exactly the same tightrope at the BOE but the calculus is a lot more about how fragile the economy is, even though the U.K. has one of the worst inflation problems of the G-10," said Altaf Kassam, head of investment strategy for Europe, the Middle East and Africa at \uc2 State Street Global Advisors\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 By opting for the half-point increase, BOE officials pointed to recent government measures to cap soaring energy bills that are expected to help alleviate one of the biggest contributors to U.K. inflation.\par \par \uc2 At its last meeting, the bank had warned that inflation would peak above 13%. The bank said Thursday that the recently announced cap would likely mean consumer-price rises will peak at just under 11% in October but inflation could remain in double digits for months before falling. The government assistance would likely mean consumers spend more at a later date, adding to inflation in the medium term.\par \par \uc2 The bank also plowed ahead with plans to begin selling its portfolio of \uc2 U.K. government\uc2  bonds. The sales, totaling GBP80 billion, equivalent to $90.2 billion, over the next 12 months, come just as the \uc2 U.K. government\uc2  is expected to borrow more to fund a yet-to-be-announced bumper spending plan.\par \par \uc2 The bank's half-point rise also means officials opted to disregard recent criticism that they weren't being tough enough on the inflation surge. U.K. Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 , who recently took office, has said she would review the bank's inflation-fighting mandate. Meanwhile, the \uc2 BOE\uc2 's own surveys have shown public confidence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to control inflation has fallen to a record low.\par \par \uc2 The keenly anticipated meeting came a week later than initially planned after it was postponed during a period of national mourning following the death of Queen Elizabeth II.\par \par \uc2 Write to Will Horner at William.Horner@wsj.com and Megumi Fujikawa at megumi.fujikawa@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications Five members of the Bank of England's Monetary Policy Committee voted to raise rates by 0.50 percentage point, while three voted for a 0.75 percentage point increase and one other for a 0.25 percentage point increase. An earlier version of this story incorrectly said seven had voted for the half-point rate increase, one for a three-quarter-point jump and one other for a quarter-percentage point rise in interest rates. (Corrected on Sept. 22)\par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (September 23, 2022).\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 23, 2022 02:33 ET (06:33 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220923ei9n000fi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
