\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Trading: Yen Rallies After  \highlight22\uc2 Japan Intervenes \highlight\uc2  -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Megumi Fujikawa and Chelsey Dulaney \par \uc2 1,217 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2022\par 08:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par B11\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 TOKYO -- \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by buying yen for the first time in 24 years, shortly after the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  accelerated a fall in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by confirming it would maintain ultralow interest rates.\par \par \uc2 The rare \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the latest example of global concern triggered by the strong dollar, which has gained ground on the back of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's interest-rate increases.\par \par \uc2 One nation hit by the fallout is Japan, the world's third-largest economy, which has to pay more for essential imports of oil, natural gas and food. These imports are generally denominated in dollars and now are costing more in yen terms.\par \par \uc2 The dollar rose near 146 yen after \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  Gov. \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  hinted Thursday afternoon that interest rates in the country were likely to stay near zero for the next couple of years.\par \par \uc2 Less than an hour after Mr. Kuroda stopped talking, the government stepped in. It said it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to sell dollars and buy yen -- the first such \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 1998.\par \par \uc2 While a strong dollar means Americans pay less for imports -- a plus for the Biden administration amid high inflation -- the reverse is true in the rest of the world. The strong dollar is exacerbating inflationary pressure outside the U.S., adding a risk to global growth.\par \par \uc2 The yen's fall is sharper than most, but other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are headed in the same direction. China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yuan, this month broke 7 yuan to the dollar for the first time in more than two years, while the British pound has hit multidecade lows.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  resulted in an initial success. Within minutes, the yen rose so that it took only around 141 yen to buy a dollar. Still, that is far below a level set earlier this year, when the dollar was trading at around 115 yen. As of Thursday midday Eastern Time, the yen traded at 142.37 yen to the dollar.\par \par \uc2 "Although \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates in principle should be determined in the market, we cannot stand by idly when speculative and excessive moves repeatedly occur," said Finance Minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2 , adding that the government would act again if needed. Prime Minister \uc2 Fumio Kishida\uc2  repeated the same language at a news conference in New York and said the yen's volatility is the worst it has been in three decades.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Suzuki didn't comment on the size of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The vice minister of finance for international affairs, Masato Kanda, said Japan was in close communication with the U.S.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Treasury Department\uc2  said it didn't participate in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We understand \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's action, which it states aims to reduce recent heightened volatility of the yen," a Treasury spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts questioned whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  could sustain \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its own because its foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, while large, aren't unlimited. The Finance Ministry reported $1.17 trillion in such reserves as of the end of August, much of which is believed to be in dollars. That compares with more than $6 trillion of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading a day, according to the \uc2 Bank for International Settlements\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "If it's just the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  on its own, yes they can cap the decline, but they're not going to be able to change the trend," said \uc2 Brad Bechtel\uc2 , global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at \uc2 Jefferies Group\uc2 . "If you really wanted to change the trend, you would get the backing of the big central banks. Then the markets would be convinced."\par \par \uc2 The core cause of the dollar's strength, particularly against the yen, is the interest-rate differential between the U.S. and Japan, traders have said.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the Fed raised interest rates by three quarters of a percentage point and signaled additional large increases to come. A half-day later in Japan on Thursday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided to maintain short-term interest rates at minus 0.1% and its target for the 10-year Japanese government-bond yield at around zero.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kuroda said the bank had no plan to raise interest rates and repeated the bank's guidance that it would maintain easy monetary policy for the time being, which he defined as two to three years. The reason, he said, was that he didn't see Japan's inflation -- which hit 3% in August -- as likely to last.\par \par \uc2 "It is almost certain that the pace of price increases will fall below 2% next fiscal year and beyond," Mr. Kuroda said at a news conference Thursday. The next fiscal year in Japan begins in April 2023. Inflation in Japan is much milder than in the U.S., where prices have been rising at a pace exceeding 8%.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Kuroda said the yen's recent fall is "one-sided and affected by speculative trading" but reiterated that the bank would continue monetary easing because he said the nation's economy was still recovering from the Covid-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 In recent months, traders have generally brushed off statements by Japanese officials expressing concern about the yen's fall, assuming Tokyo wasn't ready to put its money where its mouth was. That could change now, analysts said.\par \par \uc2 Thursday's message from the authorities was: "Don't take us for fools," said Michiyoshi Kato, director of fixed-income sales at \uc2 Mizuho Securities\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Still, said \uc2 Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities\uc2  strategist Naomi Muguruma, "the effects could be temporary as long as fundamental factors behind a weak yen remain, such as Japan's trade deficit and an interest-rate gap between \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and abroad."\par \par \uc2 Countries regularly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, but usually to weaken \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and make their economies more competitive in global trade. Economists often dub competitive devaluations "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars."\par \par \uc2 This year, policy makers have been leaning in the opposite direction: to boost the values of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar, dubbed a "reverse \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  war" by some on Wall Street. Policy makers in Europe and South Korea have attempted to verbally defend their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks, while China has tried to shore up the yuan with policy changes.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  came on the 37th anniversary of the Plaza Accord, a 1985 agreement among the world's largest economies to weaken the value of the U.S. dollar. At that time, the dollar's blistering rally against its major trading partners was causing global dislocations. The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  worked, and the dollar's value plunged.\par \par \uc2 But it also came with unintended consequences. Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yen, rose sharply, hurting the competitiveness of its manufacturers. Exports suffered, followed by a series of policy decisions that fueled a massive asset bubble and ultimately resulted in Japan's "lost decades" of economic stagnation.\par \par \uc2 --Chieko Tsuneoka, Kosaku Narioka and Andrew Duehren contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Write to Megumi Fujikawa at megumi.fujikawa@wsj.com and Chelsey Dulaney at Chelsey.Dulaney@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 This article is being republished as part of our daily reproduction of WSJ.com articles that also appeared in the U.S. print edition of The Wall Street Journal (September 23, 2022).\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 23, 2022 02:32 ET (06:32 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220923ei9n000ce\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
