\par\par \b \uc2 Japanese Yen Could Extend Recent Losses Despite  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,538 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2022\par 13:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1120 GMT - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the yen on Thursday is unlikely to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from extending its recent losses, \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  says. The Bank of Japan earlier Thursday reiterated its commitment to ultra-loose policy after the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  raised interest rates by 75 basis points and signalled further large moves on Wednesday. That means it is unlikely \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to turn USD/JPY lower, but rather slow the pace of gains in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair, \uc2 Rabobank\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist \uc2 Jane Foley\uc2  says in a note. "In view of our bullish USD view, we retain a 3-month target of USD/JPY 147.00." USD/JPY falls 1.1% to 142.495 after earlier hitting a two-week low of 140.735, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1055 GMT - \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  lifted the rate path slightly less-than-expected in the short end, implying a 25bp hike in November, December and 1Q23, while the long end was lowered, says \uc2 SEB\uc2 's chief Norway strategist Erica Dalsto. Though the rate path didn't deviate much from \uc2 SEB\uc2 's forecast, less front-loading of rate hikes makes the overall impression slightly dovish, she says. "\uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  has clearly noted that the rate weapon is starting to work, reducing the need for further large rate hikes." A final rate hike is likely in December this year rather than 1Q23 as suggested by the rate path, implying that the policy rate is likely to peak around 2.75%-3.00%, she adds. (dominic.chopping@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1055 GMT - \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  raised its policy rate to 2.25% from 1.75% - a move that was largely expected but was still relatively tame compared to recent moves by other central banks, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economist \uc2 James Pomeroy\uc2  says in a note. "On the back of more hawkish moves from central banks in recent days and weeks, notably Sweden's 100bp hike on Tuesday, a 50bp move may be seen as a relatively dovish one." The statement and forecasts from \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  showed near-term inflation forecasts revised up materially while growth forecasts were slashed, he says. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  still expects the policy rate to peak at just above 3%, but slightly sooner than previously, with rate cuts coming in late 2023 or early 2024, Pomeroy says. (dominic.chopping@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1050 GMT - The Swiss franc's declines after the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  's policy meeting Thursday are unlikely to last, Monex Europe \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst \uc2 Simon Harvey\uc2  says in a note. The franc fell after the \uc2 SNB\uc2  raised interest rates by 75 basis points, meeting consensus forecasts but a 100bp move was a "credible risk," he says. Moreover, the \uc2 SNB\uc2  maintained its commitment to two-sided \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  instead of explicitly relaying a preference for a stronger franc, he says. However, the decision is "merely a blip in the road for the stronger CHF narrative." The \uc2 SNB\uc2 's inflation forecasts suggest further rate rises in the near-term and it signalled the possibility of increases at unscheduled meetings, Harvey says. EUR/CHF rises 1.8% to 0.9677. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1041 GMT - \uc2 Citi\uc2  's analysts expect a 50 basis point interest rate rise by the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  later on Thursday, with votes split between 25, 50 and 75 basis points, but they reckon few would be surprised by a 75bps move. This is "not least given peer pressure from the size of hikes elsewhere, including the Fed's third 75bp last night," they say. "We think it's not about precise policy calibration at this point, but rather the optics of looking tough on inflation," they say. Economists expect the BOE to raise the bank rate by 50 basis points to 2.25%, according to The Wall Street Journal's poll. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1036 GMT - The key interest rate of the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  is back in positive territory for the first time since January 2015 and is no longer the lowest in the world, ING says in a note. This rate hike is intended to combat inflation in Switzerland, which reached 3.5% in August. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expects inflation to average 3.0% in 2022 and fall to 2.4% in 2023. Given the inflation forecasts, the \uc2 SNB\uc2  could raise its rate by 75 basis points in December, according to ING. "The deteriorating economic outlook and the expected decline in inflation in 2023 should be enough to leave the \uc2 SNB\uc2  comfortable with a rate of 1.25% for the year as a whole," ING says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1029 GMT - Higher interest rates usually make a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  more attractive to foreign investors but this isn't the case for the U.K. currently, Qontigo says. \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  is expected to raise its base rate to similar heights as the U.S. federal funds rate but sterling could extend recent losses against the dollar, Christoph Schon, senior principal of applied research at Qontigo, says in a note. "The U.K.'s large trade deficit is likely to put more downward pressure on the pound, while also fuelling inflation." The \uc2 BOE\uc2  sets out its latest interest rate decision at 1100 GMT. GBP/USD rises 0.3% to 1.1307, having earlier reached its lowest level since March 1985 at 1.1212, according to FactSet.(renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1027 GMT - The \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  is likely to proceed with outright gilt sales in a decision at 1100 GMT, when it is expected to raise its base rate by 50 basis points to 2.25%, although the amount of sales is uncertain, analysts at \uc2 RBC\uc2  say in a note. The government's plans to fund a cap on domestic energy bills from October, due to be announced Friday, leaves the size of gilt sales "open to more question than previously thought." Although the impact on gilt issuance of the fiscal plan makes it possible that the BOE won't opt to commence gilt sales now, this is unlikely "given how much weight they have placed on predictability in terms of their balance sheet reduction strategy." (miriam.mukuru@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1026 GMT - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has boosted the yen, taking the market by surprise, but its effects may not last, says Osamu Takashima, head of G-10 \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  . The attempt to bolster the yen through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears at odds with the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  's monetary easing, Takashima says. Also, since \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  officials generally don't favor the idea of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to undertake a massive yen-buying campaign, he says. USD/JPY is at 142.85, down from the day's high of 145.91. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 1007 GMT - The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  raised its policy rate from minus 0.25% to 0.50% and gave hawkish signals, so \uc2 HSBC\uc2  expects the policy to be lifted by 50 basis points in December. "Thursday's meeting confirms that the \uc2 SNB\uc2  is still worried about the risk of long-term inflation expectations becoming de-anchored," \uc2 HSBC\uc2  's economist Chantana Sam says in a note. Inflation forecasts were again revised up compared to June, with annual inflation now seen at 3.0% in 2022 and 2.4% in 2023, instead of 2.8% and 1.9% previously. The \uc2 SNB\uc2  left the door open to further rate hikes this year in its statement, Sam says. However, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  sees no further changes on the policy rates in 2023, as inflation is likely to start to come down. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1002 GMT - Record inflation in the U.K. and the eurozone is eroding households' real incomes and \uc2 UBS\uc2  says the hit has been unequal across high and low income households. This is because energy and food account for a higher share of consumption in lower income households than in higher ones, economists at \uc2 UBS\uc2  say in a note. In addition, higher income households are more likely to benefit from "excess" savings, a buffer to consumption, accumulated during the Covid-19 pandemic, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. Economists at the Swiss bank argue that fiscal support measures should target lower income households. If this isn't the case, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says tighter monetary policy would be needed to bring inflation back to target.(maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0944 GMT - The series of "aggressive Fed rate hikes" recently may raise the risk of more fund outflows and weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Southeast Asia, \uc2 Fidelity International\uc2  strategist Christopher Wong says in a note. The Fed "still managed to outhawk the markets through the September dot plot and the Summary of Economic Projections" on Wednesday as it raised interest rates by a widely expected 75 basis points, Wong says. Southeast Asian central banks are also likely to tighten monetary policies given inflation levels are above their targets, he adds. But they "will have to strike a delicate balance" or growth could be hurt, Wong says.(clarence.leong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 22, 2022 07:21 ET (11:21 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220922ei9m001am\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
