\par\par \b \uc2 BOE to Raise Rates by 50bps, Proceed With Gilt Sales -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,540 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2022\par 12:27\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1027 GMT - The \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  is likely to proceed with outright gilt sales in a decision at 1100 GMT, when it is expected to raise its base rate by 50 basis points to 2.25%, although the amount of sales is uncertain, analysts at \uc2 RBC\uc2  say in a note. The government's plans to fund a cap on domestic energy bills from October, due to be announced Friday, leaves the size of gilt sales "open to more question than previously thought." Although the impact on gilt issuance of the fiscal plan makes it possible that the BOE won't opt to commence gilt sales now, this is unlikely "given how much weight they have placed on predictability in terms of their balance sheet reduction strategy." (miriam.mukuru@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1026 GMT - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has boosted the yen, taking the market by surprise, but its effects may not last, says Osamu Takashima, head of G-10 \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  . The attempt to bolster the yen through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears at odds with the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  's monetary easing, Takashima says. Also, since \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  officials generally don't favor the idea of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to undertake a massive yen-buying campaign, he says. USD/JPY is at 142.85, down from the day's high of 145.91. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 1007 GMT - The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  raised its policy rate from minus 0.25% to 0.50% and gave hawkish signals, so \uc2 HSBC\uc2  expects the policy to be lifted by 50 basis points in December. "Thursday's meeting confirms that the \uc2 SNB\uc2  is still worried about the risk of long-term inflation expectations becoming de-anchored," \uc2 HSBC\uc2  's economist Chantana Sam says in a note. Inflation forecasts were again revised up compared to June, with annual inflation now seen at 3.0% in 2022 and 2.4% in 2023, instead of 2.8% and 1.9% previously. The \uc2 SNB\uc2  left the door open to further rate hikes this year in its statement, Sam says. However, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  sees no further changes on the policy rates in 2023, as inflation is likely to start to come down. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1002 GMT - Record inflation in the U.K. and the eurozone is eroding households' real incomes and \uc2 UBS\uc2  says the hit has been unequal across high and low income households. This is because energy and food account for a higher share of consumption in lower income households than in higher ones, economists at \uc2 UBS\uc2  say in a note. In addition, higher income households are more likely to benefit from "excess" savings, a buffer to consumption, accumulated during the Covid-19 pandemic, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. Economists at the Swiss bank argue that fiscal support measures should target lower income households. If this isn't the case, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says tighter monetary policy would be needed to bring inflation back to target.(maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0944 GMT - The series of "aggressive Fed rate hikes" recently may raise the risk of more fund outflows and weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Southeast Asia, \uc2 Fidelity International\uc2  strategist Christopher Wong says in a note. The Fed "still managed to outhawk the markets through the September dot plot and the Summary of Economic Projections" on Wednesday as it raised interest rates by a widely expected 75 basis points, Wong says. Southeast Asian central banks are also likely to tighten monetary policies given inflation levels are above their targets, he adds. But they "will have to strike a delicate balance" or growth could be hurt, Wong says.(clarence.leong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0941 GMT - \uc2 Citi\uc2  's rates strategists expect the \uc2 U.K. Debt Management Office\uc2  to increase the gilt remit by around GBP60 billion to GBP191.2 billion in a review on Friday. The increased issuance is aimed at funding the energy bailout for the winter. \uc2 Citi\uc2  expects more issuance in short- and medium-term gilts, but a reduction in issuance in long-dated and inflation-linked gilts. "The next two days, then, will likely underline the misalignment of monetary and fiscal policy," they say in a note, refering to the \uc2 BOE\uc2  's expected interest rate rise and selling of gilts alongside the DMO's issuance of record amounts of gilts at higher yields. \uc2 Citi\uc2  's strategists expect 10-year gilt yields to approach 4%. They last trade more than 3 basis points lower at 3.288%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0940 GMT - \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could leave interest rates unchanged in a policy decision at 1100 GMT after unexpectedly cutting rates in August but this may have little impact on the Turkish lira, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. The lira's modest reaction to August's decision shows the market has "given up hoping for monetary policy to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," \uc2 Commerzbank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst \uc2 Esther Reichelt\uc2  says in a note. "That means today's decision is unlikely to have much of an effect on the TRY exchange rates either." USD/TRY rises 0.1% to 18.3615 after earlier hitting a record high of 18.3835, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  , following the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  's decision to lift rates by 75 basis points and signalled further large increases. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0924 GMT - Comex gold futures ticked 0.2% lower to $1,678.80 a troy ounce on Thursday on the back of a stronger U.S. dollar after the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  raised interest rates and signaled more raises to come. Despite gold being considered one of the best inflation hedges there is, its appeal to investors tends to diminish amid elevated interest rates as it yields no interest. Meanwhile, three-month copper is down 0.8% to $7,659 a metric ton, while aluminum falls 2.3% to $2,203 a ton. Three-month nickel on the \uc2 LME\uc2  is up 0.6% to $25,000 a metric ton. (giulia.petroni@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0916 GMT - Japanese financial authorities likely want to slow the pace of the yen's recent weakening, which has been too fast, says Michiyoshi Kato, director of fixed income sales at \uc2 Mizuho Securities\uc2  . Kato says many traders had been very skeptical the Japanese authorities could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to support the yen, and authorities likely wanted to demonstrate that they can. He says he doesn't believe Japanese authorities are trying to make the yen strong. USD/JPY is now at 142.14, down from the day's high of 145.91. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 0915 GMT - The U.K. is bracing itself for the next stage in an aggressive interest rate cycle, with a likely 50 basis-point rate rise at 1100 GMT, but like the major European economies, it is not as well positioned as the U.S. to weather the economic aftershocks of higher rates, says \uc2 Seema Shah\uc2  , chief global strategist at \uc2 Principal Global Investors\uc2  . "Although banks have yet to see much stress on their loan books and [Prime Minister Liz] Truss's government has acted rapidly to cap energy bills, it is the lag effect on the economy which is the greater challenge," she writes in a note. U.K. households will have fewer and fewer means to manage the rising living costs and declining growth, she says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0906 GMT - The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  is likely to further increase interest rates at its December meeting, although by a smaller 50 basis points, Pantheon Macroeconomics senior Europe economist \uc2 Melanie Debono\uc2  says in a note. That would bring the key policy rate at 1.00% by year-end, and should mark a pause in the bank's interest-rate hiking cycle for 2023, she says. "Given that we don't buy that the bank wants the Swiss franc to be stronger forever, we think the \uc2 SNB\uc2  will go down a hiking path that is markedly below that of the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  ," Debono says. The \uc2 SNB\uc2  expects inflation to fall back to target, which is set at less than 2%, by 1Q 2024 without further interest-rate hikes, she says. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0905 GMT - Sterling gains ahead of the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  's policy decision at 1100 GMT, with another large interest-rate rise expected, most likely of 50 basis points, but it is still at risk of falling in the wake of this decision, ING analyst \uc2 Chris Turner\uc2  says in a note. The \uc2 BOE\uc2  's policy tightening has provided little support to sterling this summer and instead, U.K. fiscal concern is growing. Friday's government mini-budget "could prove the trigger for another round of gilt [\uc2 U.K. government\uc2  bonds] and sterling selling." GBP/USD could fall towards 1.10 and EUR/GBP could rise to around 0.88, Turner says. GBP/USD rises 0.5% to 1.1323, having earlier hit its lowest level since March 1985 at 1.1212, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2  . EUR/GBP falls to a one-week low of 0.8709. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 22, 2022 06:27 ET (10:27 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220922ei9m0011l\par }\page {\par