\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Seen as Unlikely to Support the Yen for Long -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,482 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2022\par 14:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0855 ET - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to change the yen's weakening trend, \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  's \uc2 George Saravelos\uc2  says in a report. "It is simply not credible for a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be debasing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  via extreme amounts of QE while authorities pursue a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the same time," Saravelos says. "For the JPY to start strengthening either the global dollar positive environment needs to turn or the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  needs to abandon" it's cap on yields. Saravelos worries that "\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will just lead to an unnecessary loss of reserves and credibility." The yen rises 2.5% versus the dollar, which is also weakening against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 0853 ET - The FTSE 100 drops 0.3%, or 20 points to 7217 after the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  increased interest rates by 50 basis points to 2.25%--less than the 70bps some had expected. Miners, oil and retail stocks are among the biggest risers while \uc2 JD Sports Fashion\uc2  and property stocks drop, with the London index recovering some of its earlier losses. Still, U.K. stocks could fall further as economic weakness offsets any potential benefit from lower sterling, data and analytics firm Qontigo says. "Although a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been good news for British blue-chips in the past--for example after the Brexit referendum--the U.K.'s large trade deficit is likely to put more downward pressure on the pound, while also fueling inflation," Qontigo's Christoph Schon writes. (philip.waller@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0852 ET - The \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  is becoming increasingly divided in its interest rate decisions and this suggests market expectations are unlikely to be met, ING says. The \uc2 BOE\uc2  raised its key rate 50 basis points to 2.25% with five Monetary Policy Committee members backing the move but three preferring a 75bp rise and one favoring a 25bp move. "For first time since the great financial crisis, we have a three-way split," ING analysts say in a note. For investors, this increasing divide should be seen as a sign that the BOE is unlikely to raise rates in line with market expectations, they say. Swap markets are now pricing a peak rate close to 5% next year, they say. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0851 ET - The key takeaway from the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  's interest rate decision Thursday was the slight tweak in its statement about future policy moves, Ebury says. The \uc2 BOE\uc2  lifted its benchmark rate by 50 basis points to 2.25% and pledged to respond "forcefully, as necessary" should the outlook suggest more persistent inflationary pressures. "The phrase 'as necessary' was not included in the bank's previous communications, and may perhaps indicate that further 50bp hikes are not a given at upcoming meetings," Ebury strategist Matthew Ryan says in a note. However, markets still see additional 50bp moves as highly likely during at least the next couple of meetings, and sterling's reaction has therefore been rather muted, he says. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0850 ET - The one thing that is clear with U.K. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is that "nobody knows the appropriate approach," Jamie Niven, senior fund manager at Candriam says. \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  raised the bank rate by 50 basis ponts to 2.25%, but the Monetary Policy Committee's views were split between 25 and 75 basis points. Candriam expects many central banks to become more aggressive now, allowing them to potentially slow down at coming meetings. For the BOE, the market is still pricing almost 150bps of interest rate rises at the final two MPC meetings of 2022, which "feels a lot to me given the committee, as a whole, weren't comfortable hiking by 75bps today," he says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0843 ET - Sterling looks vulnerable and \uc2 U.K. government\uc2  bonds, or gilts, could rise even after the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  raised interest rates by 50 basis points to 2.25% on Thursday, BlackRock Investment Institute says. Given the enormity of the \uc2 U.K. government\uc2  's forthcoming fiscal package, the U.K.'s credibility will "undergo a trial by markets," \uc2 BlackRock\uc2  chief investment strategist Vivek Paul says in a note. "The U.K. current account deficit and widening budget gap make sterling particularly vulnerable," he says. "Following sizeable yield moves, and given we believe that the BOE will ultimately not follow through with all the hikes priced into markets, we have a modest preference for gilts relative to other developed market government bonds such as U.S. Treasuries." (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0842 ET - Treasury yields extend gains after the Fed projections indicated rates are unlikely to peak until early next year. The two-year trades at 4.105% and the 10-year at 3.564%. The Fed is expected to repeat the 75-basis point hike in November, after Powell made it clear fighting inflation trumps protecting jobs or avoiding a recession. Yields also take a boost from weekly jobless claims, which rose a little less than expected, at 213K, in a sign that monetary tightening is yet to break labor markets and wage inflation. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 0834 ET - The \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  's 50 basis-point increase in the base rate to 2.25% signals its readiness to give a "forceful response" to stubbornly high inflation, Katrin Loehken, U.K. economist at \uc2 DWS\uc2  , writes in a note. "Given the strong wage pressure and a quasi double-digit inflation rate, this also seems necessary," she says. The \uc2 BOE\uc2  is also aware that the economic indicators are pointing south, with an expected recession approaching, she says, adding that this is probably the main reason why it has refrained from raising interest rates even more. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0811 ET - Gilt yields rise sharply after the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  raised the bank rate by 50 basis points and unanimously voted to sell GBP80 billion of its gilts stock over the coming twelve months, comprising both maturing gilts and gilt sales. The \uc2 BOE\uc2  reiterated that there would be "a high bar for amending the planned reduction in the stock of purchased gilts." The Monetary Policy Committee will only review the stock of purchased assets at a scheduled annual meeting. The 10-year gilt yield rises around 8 basis points to 3.427% after the announcement, from around 3.297% beforehand, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (miriam.mukuru@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0804 ET - European banks can now look forward to an additional EUR40 billion boost to their revenue as central banks raise interest rates faster than expected, Bank of America Global Research analysts say in a research note. "Rates have moved upwards more quickly than any bank had in its plan," they say. Banks have so far faced very little pressure to pay more for deposits, which could also help drive margin expansion, they say. \uc2 BofA\uc2  analysts now expect the boost to European banks' revenue from interest rates to be EUR40 billion bigger compared with their expectations at the time of 2Q earnings reporting a couple of months ago. (cristina.roca@wsj.com; @_cristinaroca)\par \par \uc2 0802 ET - The \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  is expected to increase interest rates to a peak of 4.00% amid a tight labor market and sticky inflation expectations, \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  chief U.K. economist \uc2 Paul Dales\uc2  says in a note. Thursday's 50 basis-point interest-rate hike was partly driven by the government's easing of fiscal policy, which the BOE expects will lower short-term inflation but add to price pressures in the medium-term, he says. "The Bank has indicated it will raise rates further to offset some of the boost to demand from the government's fiscal plans," Dales says. "The Bank has a lot more work to do yet and the pivot to lower rates is a story for 2024 rather than 2023," he says. (xavier.fontdegloria@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0754 ET - The Turkish lira extends its losses to reach a record low against the dollar after \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its benchmark interest rate by 100 basis points to 12% despite inflation above 80%. The bank said it "assessed that the updated level of policy rate is adequate under the current outlook." The decision comes amid continued calls from Turkey President \uc2 Recep Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  for lower rates. "With external financing conditions tightening, the risks remain firmly skewed to sharp and disorderly falls in the lira," Capital Economics economist Liam Peach writes. USD/TRY rises to a high of 18.4008 after the decision, from 18.3748 beforehand, according to FactSet. Capital Economics sees USD/TRY rising to 24.0000 by year end. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 22, 2022 08:55 ET (12:55 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220922ei9m001tn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
