20\'22 \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to prop up the yen \u8226\'20\'22 Kanda: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stem weak yen \u8226\'20\'22 That sees USD/JPY slump from 145.90 to 140.31, on the EBS, so far \u8226\'20\'22 It is far from certain such \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  will prove successful medium-term \u8226\'20\'22 It could possibly just enable USD/JPY bulls to enter at better levels \u8226\'20\'22 Short-term, expect volatile USD/JPY and EUR/JPY trading \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/JPY's correlation with USD/JPY is high, 30/60-day logs are above +0.50 GBP/USD jumps through 1.13 as Japan sells dollars for yen \u8226\'20\'22 Cable jumps to 1.1352 as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, selling USD/JPY \u8226\'20\'22 1.1352 = intra-day high \u8226\'20\'22 1.1213 was 37-year low for GBP/USD at 0701 GMT (on hawkish Fed) \u8226\'20\'22 1.1352 approximates to last week's low (Sept 16). 1.1358 was Tuesday's low \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 BoE\uc2  rate decision at 1100 GMT; hike of at least 50 bps expected AUD/USD 0.6670 caps Japan-spurred rally from 28-month low \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD met headwind a fraction shy of 0.6670 after rallying off 0.6574 \u8226\'20\'22 0.6670 is former support point turned resistance level (last week's low) \u8226\'20\'22 0.6574 was 28-month low -- before \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  weighed on USD \u8226\'20\'22 \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  started shortly after 0800 GMT \u8226\'20\'22 Drop to 0.6574 followed Fed's hawkish rate hike on Wednesday The loss of liquidity will impact \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Nearly every \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is now tightening monetary policy, withdrawing liquidity and undermining assets that had previously ballooned due to stimulus stemming from every crisis since 2008. With less liquidity, assets deemed to be risky are likely to fall, as are risky \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  deemed safe should rally. The liquidity of individual \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is key to their potential to move in the months ahead. Those that lack depth - most emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and others grouped with major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like New Zealand's dollar - have the potential to move greater distances in rarefied liquidity. The great depth of market that is afforded the dollar, making it the world's reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , will cast it an increasingly positive light if less liquid \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  plunge. Both the drops seen so far this year for emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the dollar's gains could be dwarfed going forward. The movements so far were mainly fuelled by the U.S. policy tightening policy alone, while future moves will be influenced by the mass tightening by central banks across the globe. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3C1DbW7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\par \par \uc2 Rate hike significant for ZAR but policy outlook more so\par \par \uc2 In a major week for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  interest rate decisions and following Wednesday's 75-basis point U.S. rate hike, today's \uc2 South African Reserve Bank (SARB) \uc2 move holds significance for the rand, and the bank's views on the policy outlook even more so. The SARB is widely expected to tighten policy by 75-basis points today and more importantly maintain the margin with U.S. interest rates. Anything less than 75-basis points will underpin further USD/ZAR gains. The latest South African inflation data hinted at a possible peak in price rises and investors will be watching for indications of monetary policy moderation down the line. However, one-month's price data will not force the SARB to signal any major change in its policy stance. The SARB has a 4.5% mid-point inflation target and until prices get close to that target, tight monetary policy will continue to drag on USD/ZAR gains. USD/ZAR is still set to go higher, however, as South African domestic woes are damaging the rand's recovery prospects. USD/ZAR levels to watch today at 17.8150 topside and 17.5325 on the base. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3BWuxrH"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\par \par \par \par \uc2 \f2 To read our latest \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news and analysis services, click on\f28 \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-25T10:51:56.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220922ei9m01vwh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
