rest rates and international situation \u8226\'20\'22 07:30 SNB monetary policy rate decision; 0.50% f\u8217\'20\'19cast, -0.25% prev \u8226\'20\'22 07:45 German FinMin Lindner discusses current and future challenges for financial sector \u8226\'20\'22 08:10 \uc2 ECB\uc2 's Fernandez-Bollo gives online remarks at event in Brussels \u8226\'20\'22 08:30 \uc2 Norway \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary policy decision, press conference; 2.25% f\u8217\'20\'19cast, 1.75% prev \u8226\'20\'22 10:15 \uc2 ECB\uc2 's Tuominen participates in panel Frankfurt \u8226\'20\'22 11:00 BoE rate decision and minutes of meeting; 2.25% f'cast, 1.75% prev \u8226\'20\'22 15:00 \uc2 BoE\uc2 's Tenreyro speaks on climate change at seminar at \uc2 San Francisco Fed\uc2  \u8226\'20\'22 16:30 Keynote by \uc2 ECB\uc2 's Schnabel at Network Luxemburg \u8226\'20\'22 18:30 BoE's Haskel participates in a panel Commentary and Analysis USD/JPY-In a nervous uptrend post-BOJ; Kuroda presser eyed \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY moves steadily higher, finds footing after sharp post-\uc2 BOJ\uc2  whipsaw \u8226\'20\'22 C.bank maintains short-, long-term rate targets; no tweak to dovish guidance \u8226\'20\'22 Remains global outlier with low rates despite weak yen \u8226\'20\'22 Initial 145.40 high on dovish \uc2 BOJ\uc2  swiftly reversed; drop to 143.50 ensues \u8226\'20\'22 Speculation of \uc2 BOJ\uc2  rate-checks in jittery market but no confirmation \u8226\'20\'22 Dip buyers surface but rebound tops out at 144.95 as traders wary of \uc2 BOJ\uc2  \u8226\'20\'22 Gov Kuroda's presser expected at 0630 GMT; views on weak yen, inflation eyed \u8226\'20\'22 BOJ-Fed policy divergence to underpin USD; \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears may cap gains EUR/USD-Remains heavy in Asia as hawkish Fed resonates \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD opened -1.3% at 0.9838 after hawkish Fed and Ukraine uncertainty undermined \u8226\'20\'22 After trading -9847 the EUR/USD came under pressure as USD firmed in Asia \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD traded to 0.9807 before bids ahead of 0.9800 discouraged sellers \u8226\'20\'22 It bouced to 0.9838 when USD/JPY had a whippy reaction to \uc2 BOJ\uc2  decision \u8226\'20\'22 Rally was brief and EUR/USD is trading around 0.9820 into the afternoon \u8226\'20\'22 USD likely to remain in demand for the short-term at least \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD resistance at former support at 0.9864F with sellers 0.9850 \u8226\'20\'22 More resistance is at 0.9977 where the 10 & 21-day MAs converge \u8226\'20\'22 There isn't any support until a 2002 monthly low at 0.9608 AUD/USD-Extends losses in Asia as risk selloff continues \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD opened -0.85% at 0.6633 after hawkish Fed hike sent USD higher \u8226\'20\'22 Risk selloff in wake of Fed also weighed on AUD/USD sentiment as Asia opened \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD fell sharply below 0.6600 before bids appeared below 0.6590 \u8226\'20\'22 The low was 0.6583 and is trading around 0.6590 into the afternoon \u8226\'20\'22 E-minis are down 0.58% while the AXJ index is down 1.40% \u8226\'20\'22 Dalian iron ore is up 2.88% to offer some support for the sagging AUD \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD poised to move lower fundamentally and technically \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance is at former support at 0.6670/80 and 10-day MA at 0.6728 \u8226\'20\'22 There isn't support of note until the 61.8 of the pandemic move at 0.6463 GBP/USD plumbs the depths, awaiting BOE rate decision \u8226\'20\'22 GBP/USD slumped to new 37-yr low of 1.1221 vs close 1.1270 \u8226\'20\'22 Last at 1.1238, some dip-buying appears before BOE decision \u8226\'20\'22 Spikes toward 1.1392 top of downtrend channel will be sold \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 BOE\uc2  expected to hike 50bps but may sound less hawkish than Fed \u8226\'20\'22 Government's energy price caps should cool inflation \u8226\'20\'22 Unwinding of quantitative easing also expected to be announced U.S. dollar has ample room to roam higher after Fed The U.S. dollar index convincingly broke higher in the wake of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's hawkish 75 basis-point rate hike Wednesday, and there are no obvious fundamental or technical impediments to significant further gains. The Fed hiked rates as the market was pricing, but managed to exceed hawkish expectations through its aggressive "dot-plot" projections. The forecast of another 1.25 percentage points in hikes by the end of 2022 and a 4.6% median range for end-2023 hammers home the Fed's intention of remaining extremely aggressive in the short term and its commitment to "higher for longer" over the next year. The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is likely to conclude that the U.S. dollar can go "higher for longer" and recent predictions that the USD was ready to correct may have to be reconsidered. The Fed probably won't mind a higher USD, as it will cheapen import costs, helping in their resolute efforts to bring inflation down to their 2% target. Meanwhile the euro, the main component of the U.S. dollar index, is being undermined by fears of the impact of the escalating war in Ukraine. Safe-haven flows due to rising geopolitical fears will mainly benefit the U.S. dollar as the Japanese yen holds little appeal while the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  keeps yields floored. The U.S. dollar index is trending higher with the 5, 10 and 21-day moving averages aligned in a bullish formation and tilting higher. Only a break below the 10-DMA at 109.90 would warn that a short-term top is forming. There is no significant resistance of note ahead of the 2001 index high at 121.02, the objective of the current move. AUD/USD poised to fall as reasons to sell pile up The Australian dollar is looking vulnerable to a deeper fall after the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's hawkish 75 basis-point rate hike re-ignited the U.S. dollar uptrend. Risk assets fell in the wake of the Fed decision and aggressive rate hike projections. Rising geopolitical pressures due to the escalation of the Russia-Ukraine conflict portend more market volatility. As a risk-sensitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the AUD underperforms when markets are volatile. The hawkish Fed outlook has also heightened global growth fears, with the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  2s/10s yield curve inverting further, signalling that the bond market is pricing in a recession. Weakening global demand will likely continue to weigh on commodity prices and pressure commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the AUD, NZD and CAD. The AUD/USD has fallen below 0.6600 in Asia Thursday morning, as buyers are hesitant despite recent falls. There is now no technical support ahead of the 61.8 Fibonacci retracement of the pandemic low-high at 0.6463. The AUD/USD is trending lower with the 5, 10 and 21-day moving averages aligned in a bearish formation and pointing lower. Only a break above the 10-DMA at 0.6728 would warn a bottom is forming.\par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-25T04:53:29.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220922ei9m00j7p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
