\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Yen spikes after  \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention \highlight\uc2 , stocks slump\b0\par\par\uc2 1,084 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2022\par 22:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds close of U.S. markets)\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  after dollar/yen breaks 145\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bonanza as UK, Swiss, Norway hike\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Stocks slump on Wall Street, in Europe, Asia\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Bond yields rise after Fed rate hike\par \par \uc2 By Herbert Lash and Marc Jones\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK/LONDON, Sept 22 (Reuters) - The yen spiked higher on Thursday after the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's strong stance on rates the day before roiled the outlook for bonds and stocks while forcing \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time since 1998.\par \par \uc2 The dollar slid after surging to fresh two-decade highs following the Fed's raising of interest rates on Wednesday by a hefty 75 basis points. Its projection of more large increases to come cemented a "higher for longer" view on rates.\par \par \uc2 The bond market responded with the part of the yield curve measuring the gap between two- and 10-year Treasury notes inverting the most since at least 2000. The measure, a signal of a likely recession in a year or two, later eased a bit to stand at -42.0 basis points.\par \par \uc2 Stocks fell further on Wall Street and in Europe, where Russia's threat on Wednesday to use nuclear weapons amplified the existing economic pain and volatility from the Ukraine war. The major British, German and French bourses tumbled more than 1%.\par \par \uc2 But the day's big news was Tokyo swooping in to support the yen soon after Europe opened. While such a move had seemed imminent for weeks - the yen has fallen 20% this year, almost half of that in the last six weeks - it still packed a punch.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  surged almost 4% to 140.31 to the dollar from 145.81 in a little over 40 minutes. The yen was last up 1.17% versus the greenback at 142.36.\par \par \uc2 Central banks hiking rates around the world and Japan fighting back against the weak yen cooled the dollar's latest burst to fresh highs, said Joe Manimbo, U.S. senior market analyst at Convera.\par \par \uc2 "But the Fed's unflinching determination to restore 2% inflation is likely to keep the buck well-supported for the foreseeable future," Manimbo added.\par \par \uc2 With the dollar stalled, the euro edged up 0.01% to $0.9839 and other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  gained as well.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo's move came just hours after the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  maintained super-low rates, fighting the global tide of monetary tightening by the U.S. and other central banks trying to rein in roaring inflation.\par \par \uc2 Volatility and uncertainty have risen as the market comes to grips with a policy regime that is reducing liquidity after a decade of abundance, said \uc2 David Bahnsen\uc2 , chief investment officer at wealth manager The Bahnsen Group in Newport Beach, California.\par \par \uc2 "Excessive quantitative easing over the past decade is going to result in excessive tightening and the market has no way to properly price what this means for valuations," Bahnsen said.\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street, the Dow Jones Industrial Average closed down 0.36%, the S&P 500 dropped 0.85% and the rate-sensitive Nasdaq Composite slid 1.37%.\par \par \uc2 The likelihood of a recession if the Fed maintains its rate-hiking stance suggests earnings will come down 15% next year, said Mike Mullaney, director of global markets at Boston Partners.\par \par \uc2 Excluding the energy sector, the estimated growth rate for the S&P 500 in the third quarter already has declined by 1.7%, according to Refinitiv data.\par \par \uc2 "We're going to revisit the (June) lows," Mullaney said of the S&P 500. "The number being thrown around by the bears is 3200. Under a recessionary scenario that's definitely in play."\par \par \uc2 In Europe, the pan-regional STOXX 600 index lost 1.79% to close below 400 for the first time since January 2021. MSCI's gauge of global stock performance shed 1.04%, breaking below this year's bottom to touch lows last seen in November 2020.\par \par \uc2 MSCI's emerging markets index fell 0.90% and Asian stocks marched overnight to a two-year low after the Fed's rate hike and outlook.\par \par \uc2 The median of Fed officials' own outlook has U.S. rates at 4.4% by year's end - 100 bps higher than their June projection - and even higher, at 4.6%, by the end of 2023.\par \par \uc2 Futures scrambled to catch up. The yield on two-year Treasuries hit a 15-year high of 4.135% in Asia and were last at 4.120%. Ten-year yields set fresh 11-year highs and were last up 19 basis points at 3.702%.\par \par \uc2 In Europe, Germany's rate-sensitive two-year bond yield rose to 1.897%, its highest since May 2011, before easing to 1.833%.\par \par \uc2 FOLLOW THE FED\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  also pulled up its rates by 75 basis points, only the second increase in 15 years. The move ended a 7-1/2-year spell with negative rates.\par \par \uc2 Also in Europe, Norway and Britain raised rates by 50 bps with traders seeing plenty more ahead.\par \par \uc2 The pound rose modestly on the day after hitting a 37-year low of $1.1213 overnight on growing worries about the state of Britain's finances. Sweden's crown had also touched a record low despite the country's steepest rate hike in a generation earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 The global economic outlook is helping drive the dollar higher as U.S. yields look attractive and investors think other economies look too fragile to sustain rates as high as those contemplated by the Fed.\par \par \uc2 Japan and China are outliers and their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are sliding particularly hard.\par \par \uc2 The dollar's rise has also sent emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumbling and punished cryptocurrencies and commodities.\par \par \uc2 Lira traders were left wincing again as Turkey, where inflation is running at around 85%, defied economic orthodoxy and slashed another 100 basis points off its interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Oil rose in volatile trading on concerns an escalation of the war in Ukraine could further hurt supply.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude futures settled up 63 cents at $90.46 a barrel and U.S. crude rose 55 cents to settle at $83.49.\par \par \uc2 U.S. gold futures settled 0.3% higher at $1,681.10 an ounce.\par \par \uc2 Bitcoin rose 4.76% to $19,345.00.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Herbert Lash, Additional reporting by Marc Jones in London, Tom Westbrook in Sydney; Editing by Kirsten Donovan, Nick Zieminski and Richard Chang) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-22T21:29:11.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220922ei9m03vcp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
