\par\par \b \uc2 Treasury Yields Surge as Global Central Banks Scramble to Respond to Fed, Inflation -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sam Goldfarb \par \uc2 1,031 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2022\par 22:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  bond yields surged Thursday, after foreign governments and central banks rushed to raise interest rates or otherwise support local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  pressured by the dollar's strongest rally in a generation.\par \par \uc2 U.S. yields were largely stable overnight after the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  raised short-term rates by 0.75 percentage point for the third consecutive meeting and said more large rate increases are likely as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seeks to quell inflation.\par \par \uc2 But long-term U.S. yields began a sharp ascent shortly before the start of U.S. stock trading Thursday, responding at least in part to several central banks raising rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the world's third-largest economy, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to sell dollars and buy yen for the first since 1998.\par \par \uc2 The moves unnerved investors, who are already contending with the sharp decline in U.S. stock indexes this year and expectations that the Fed's rate-tightening campaign will inevitably lead to a significant economic slowdown.\par \par \uc2 As central banks one-up each other, raising rates and taking other aggressive actions, it is generating a high degree of uncertainty about what they will do next, said \uc2 Jim Caron\uc2 , senior portfolio manager and chief strategist of global fixed income at \uc2 Morgan Stanley Investment Management\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Investors, he said, are thinking "'I thought I knew where they were going, but now the sky's the limit,' and that's where you get into this freefall type of mentality."\par \par \uc2 Selling of \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  bonds sent the yield on the benchmark 10-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  note to 3.705% from 3.511% Wednesday, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2 . That marked its biggest one-day gain since June 13 and highest close since February 2011. Yields rise when prices fall.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields play a critical role in the global economy, setting a floor on borrowing costs for U.S. consumers and business and establishing a benchmark forward-looking return against which other assets are measured.\par \par \uc2 A historically large climb in yields this year -- driven by stubbornly high inflation and escalating expectations for how high the Fed will raise interest rates -- has already sent mortgage rates soaring. It also has punished stocks, causing investors to mark down the value of expected corporate profits because they can now get a substantial return by just buying and holding Treasurys.\par \par \uc2 Thursday's big jump in yields stands to exacerbate those trends, while highlighting the unpredictable fallout of the current inflationary environment.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said they couldn't definitely pin Thursday's move on one particular event. Still, many said it could be broadly explained by how the Fed's aggressive interest-rate increases are strengthening the U.S. dollar by encouraging global investors to pull money out of other markets to invest in higher-yielding U.S. assets.\par \par \uc2 That trend in turn is putting extra pressure on other countries to defend their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as they also fight inflation, and contributing to a race to lift rates around the world.\par \par \uc2 While analysts said they couldn't definitively pin Thursday's move on one particular event, many said it could be broadly explained by how the Fed's aggressive interest-rate increases are strengthening the U.S. dollar and putting pressure on other countries to defend their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as they also contend with inflation.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, central banks from Norway to South Africa raised interest rates by larger-than-expected increments. \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  eschewed a 0.75-percentage-point increase in favor of a more conservative half-percentage point but moved ahead with plans to sell its portfolio of \uc2 U.K. government\uc2  bonds, contributing to a large increase in \uc2 U.K. government\uc2  bond yields.\par \par \uc2 "You can think of it as a vicious cycle," Mr. Caron said.\par \par \uc2 Of particular concern to U.S. investors was the Japanese government's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the yen. That move led to speculation on Wall Street that the government might be selling Treasurys to raise the dollars it needed to buy its home \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some analysts expressed skepticism about that scenario, noting that the Japanese government is believed to have large holdings of U.S. cash and cash-equivalents, reducing the need to sell longer-term Treasurys.\par \par \uc2 "Japan has plenty of cash in its reserves," said \uc2 Brad Setser\uc2 , a senior fellow at \uc2 the Council on Foreign Relations\uc2  and an expert on global capital flows. It is "not at all clear it needs to sell Treasurys."\par \par \uc2 For his part, Japanese Finance Minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2  declined to comment about the specifics of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including how many yen \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought for dollars and what types of dollar-denominated assets it sold.\par \par \uc2 "It's not crazy for people who follow the Treasury market to anticipate in a world where a lot of Asian central banks are \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they're going to be wanting to build up their dollar cash holdings and therefore they're at the margin tending to sell Treasurys," Mr. Setser said.\par \par \uc2 More broadly, some analysts said investors have reason to worry about demand for Treasurys from foreign investors now that U.S. bonds are no longer an outlier in the developed world offering positive returns.\par \par \uc2 "Foreign demand for Treasurys has been light this year, "said Priya Misra, head of global rates strategy at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  in New York. "There's been a global rise in rates so people don't need to look to the Treasury market for yield,"\par \par \uc2 Ms. Misra argued that a big reason why yields jumped globally on Thursday was that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had increased speculation that the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would lift its policy aimed at controlling bond yields, even after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's leader pushed back against that idea on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Such a move, she said, would push up Japanese bond yields. That could then lead Japanese investors to sell foreign bonds, including Treasurys, so they could buy Japanese debt instead.\par \par \uc2 -- Megumi Fujikawa contributed to this article\par \par \uc2 Write to Sam Goldfarb at sam.goldfarb@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 22, 2022 16:48 ET (20:48 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220922ei9m00371\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
