\par\par \b \uc2 Treasury Yields Surge as Global Central Banks Scramble to Respond to Fed, Inflation -- WSJ\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sam Goldfarb \par \uc2 739 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2022\par 20:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  bond yields surged Thursday, after foreign governments and central banks rushed to raise interest rates or otherwise support local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  pressured by the dollar's strongest rally in a generation.\par \par \uc2 U.S. yields were largely stable overnight after the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  raised short-term rates by 0.75 percentage point for the third consecutive meeting and said more large rate increases are likely as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seeks to quell inflation.\par \par \uc2 But long-term U.S. yields began a sharp ascent shortly before the start of U.S. stock trading Thursday, responding at least in part to several central banks raising rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the world's third-largest economy, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to sell dollars and buy yen for the first since 1998.\par \par \uc2 The moves unnerved investors, who are already contending with the sharp decline in U.S. stock indexes this year and expectations that the Fed's rate-tightening campaign will inevitably lead to a significant economic slowdown. Among the sources of concern: signs that longtime buyers of U.S. debt will potentially become sellers in a new global market landscape remade by inflation and rising rates.\par \par \uc2 "It's not crazy for people who follow the Treasury market to anticipate in a world where a lot of Asian central banks are \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , they're going to be wanting to build up their dollar cash holdings and therefore they're at the margin tending to sell Treasurys," said \uc2 Brad Setser\uc2 , a senior fellow at \uc2 the Council on Foreign Relations\uc2  and an expert on global capital flows.\par \par \uc2 In recent trading, the yield on the benchmark 10-year \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  note climbed to 3.689% from 3.511% Wednesday, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2 , on track for its biggest one-day gain since June 13 and highest close since February 2011. Yields rise when prices fall.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields play a critical role in the global economy, setting a floor on borrowing costs for U.S. consumers and business and establishing a benchmark forward-looking return against which other assets are measured. A historically large climb in yields this year -- driven by stubbornly high inflation and escalating expectations for how high the Fed will raise interest rates -- has already sent mortgage rates soaring and delivered a punishing blow to stocks by decreasing the value of companies' expected earnings.\par \par \uc2 Thursday's big jump in yields stands to exacerbate those trends, while highlighting the unpredictable fallout of the current inflationary environment.\par \par \uc2 While analysts said they couldn't definitively pin Thursday's move on one particular event, many said it could be broadly explained by how the Fed's aggressive interest-rate increases are strengthening the U.S. dollar and putting pressure on other countries to defend their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as they also contend with inflation.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, central banks from Norway to South Africa raised interest rates by larger-than-expected increments. \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  eschewed a 0.75-percentage-point increase in favor of a more conservative half-percentage point but moved ahead with plans to sell its portfolio of \uc2 U.K. government\uc2  bonds, contributing to a large increase in \uc2 U.K. government\uc2  bond yields.\par \par \uc2 "You can think of it as a vicious cycle," said \uc2 Jim Caron\uc2 , senior portfolio manager and chief strategist of global fixed income at \uc2 Morgan Stanley Investment Management\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Of particular concern to U.S. investors was Japan's move to support the yen, which led to intense speculation on Wall Street that the government might be accomplishing that at least in part by selling some its \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  holdings.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts expressed skepticism about that scenario, noting that the Japanese government is believed to have large holdings of U.S. cash and cash-equivalents, reducing the need to sell longer-term Treasurys.\par \par \uc2 For his part, Japanese Finance Minister \uc2 Shunichi Suzuki\uc2  declined to comment about the specifics of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including how many yen \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  bought for dollars and what types of dollar-denominated assets it sold.\par \par \uc2 Still, investors and analysts said it was rational for U.S. bond investors to be nervous, as Japan and other Asian countries try to prop up their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 -- Megumi Fujikawa contributed to this article\par \par \uc2 Write to Sam Goldfarb at sam.goldfarb@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 22, 2022 14:58 ET (18:58 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220922ei9m002sa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
