\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 2-Central banks raise rates again as Fed drives global inflation fight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,178 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2022\par 19:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 (Adds Treasury statement, analyst reaction)\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Rates raised from Britain to Indonesia after Fed moved\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Investors price in bigger hikes from \uc2 ECB\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Japan steps in as yen plummets\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  under pressure\par \par \uc2 By Francesco Canepa and Howard Schneider\par \par \uc2 FRANKFURT/WASHINGTON, Sept 22 (Reuters) - Global central banks continued raising interest rates on Thursday, following the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  in a fight against inflation that is sending shockwaves through financial markets and the economy.\par \par \uc2 Japan, the outlier among major developed economies, kept interest rates steady on Thursday only to be punished as traders pushed the yen to a record low against the dollar - prompting the first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since 1998.\par \par \uc2 It was a potential sign of a massive adjustment to come as the world adapts to U.S. interest rates rising to levels not seen since the global financial crisis 15 years ago prompted the Fed to slash its policy rate to zero and unleash massive rounds of bondbuying.\par \par \uc2 That era of cheap liquidity, which lasted through the worst of the coronavirus pandemic and until inflation became a prominent risk, is now ending. U.S. interest rates and the U.S. dollar serve as reference points for borrowing costs around the world, and \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  officials have now flagged not just plans to continue tightening monetary policy, but to keep it tight for years to come in what may for many countries amount to a fresh financial shock - and a broad repricing of bonds, stocks, and other financial instruments.\par \par \uc2 The value of the dollar is soaring, helping ease inflation in the United States even as it raises the costs of many dollar-priced imports for other countries, a factor that may have figured into \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Some analysts feel more is bound to follow.\par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets tends to result in...less optimal economic outcomes than would otherwise result," RSM Chief Economist \uc2 Joe Brusuelas\uc2  wrote after Japan's action. "But the current inflation shock may outweigh this reluctance. We may be entering an era of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets."\par \par \uc2 In the aftermath of the 2007 to 2009 financial crisis, central bankers often accused each other of waging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  wars to cheapen local money to promote exports, an accusation levelled pointedly at the Fed. Inflation may now prompt a similar tension in the other direction.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  officials, who monitor global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies closely for signs countries are \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to gain an advantage, took note of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's move on Thursday as an effort to "reduce recent heightened volatility" in the yen, but stopped short of endorsing it.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , asked in July about the yen's substantial depreciation, said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was warranted only in "rare and exceptional circumstances."\par \par \uc2 Though many countries are battling a common inflation outbreak in the aftermath of the COVID-19 pandemic, the Fed's response has stood out both because of the dollar's global role and the aggressiveness of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Fed Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , asked about the risks of major central banks shifting monetary policy in unison, said that while the Fed tries to estimate the impact of policy "spillovers" between countries, he and his colleagues had to remain focused on local economic conditions.\par \par \uc2 "We are very aware of what's going on in other economies around the world and what that means for us and vice versa," Powell said at his press conference on Wednesday after the Fed approved its third consecutive "unusually large" 75 basis point rate increase. But, he said, U.S. officials "have a domestic mandate, domestic objectives" of stable U.S. inflation and maximum employment.\par \par \uc2 HALF A DOZEN RATE HIKES\par \par \uc2 The actions by the Fed, along with those of other major central banks, have formed the backdrop for early warnings from international officials and analysts that rising rates for \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the dollar and the euro could tighten global financial conditions so much it leads to a global recession.\par \par \uc2 Along with the Fed's action on Wednesday, its fifth interest rate increase since March, a half dozen central banks from Indonesia to Norway followed suit with their own rate increases and often with guidance that more would follow.\par \par \uc2 They are fighting inflation rates ranging from Switzerland's 3.5% to nearly 10% in Britain - the result of a rebound in demand since the pandemic subsided accompanied by sluggish supply, especially from China, and rising prices for fuel and other commodities in the wake of Russia's invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Central bankers were adamant that curbing runaway price growth was their main task at present. But they were bracing for their actions to take a toll, as rising borrowing costs typically dampen investment, hiring and consumption.\par \par \uc2 "We have got to get inflation behind us," Powell told reporters after Fed policymakers unanimously agreed to raise the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's benchmark overnight interest rate to a range of 3.00%-3.25%. "I wish there were a painless way to do that. There isn't."\par \par \uc2 The Fed said it expected the economy to slow to a crawl and unemployment to rise to a degree historically associated with a recession - a prospect looming ever larger in the euro zone too and seen as highly likely in Britain.\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  raised rates and said it would continue to "respond forcefully, as necessary" to inflation, despite the economy entering recession.\par \par \uc2 "For borrowers, this will mean significantly higher costs yet again and yet still no real control on the soaring cost of living," Emma-Lou Montgomery, an associate director at \uc2 Fidelity International\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 World stocks fell close to a two-year low and emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  plummeted as investors prepared for a world where growth is scarce and credit harder to get.\par \par \uc2 Market participants have also pushed up their rate expectations for the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is all but certain to hike again on Oct. 23. It is now seen taking its own interest rate to almost 3% next year from 0.75% now.\par \par \uc2 Japan opted to hold its rates near zero to support the country's fragile economic recovery, but many analysts believe its position to be increasingly untenable given the global shift to higher borrowing costs.\par \par \uc2 "There's absolutely no change to our stance of maintaining easy monetary policy for the time being. We won't be raising interest rates for some time," \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  said after the policy decision.\par \par \uc2 But the yen plummeted against the dollar following the decision, forcing Japanese authorities to step in and buy the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a bid to stem the slide.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued with its unorthodox policy on Thursday by delivering another surprise interest rate cut despite inflation running at more than 80%, sending the lira to an all-time low against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Francesco Canepa and Howard Schneider; Editing by Hugh Lawson and Andrea Ricci) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-22T18:45:35.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220922ei9m02a3n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
