\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange \highlight\uc2  markets to halt yen's slide\b0\par\par\uc2 724 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has decided to act this Thursday to try to stop the yen's plunge. The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has announced an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market unprecedented in more than two decades with the aim of supporting the value of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which on Thursday, before the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , exceeded the red line of 145 dollars per yen, its lowest point in 24 years. "We have taken decisive action," confirmed Masato Kanda, the Asian country's deputy finance minister for international affairs. Kanda answered positively when asked if this was in fact an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to Reuters. The Japanese government has not clarified the extent of the yen purchase.\par \par \uc2 The decision was taken after the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  opted, also on Thursday, to keep interest rates in negative territory. While the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  are betting on tackling galloping inflation by raising rates, Tokyo is keeping its finger on the pulse and holding out as the last of the developed economies to fly the flag of an ultra-loose monetary policy, with the aim of providing cover for the country's still fragile recovery after the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 And no monetary script flip is expected. "There is absolutely no change in our stance of maintaining a loose monetary policy for the time being," \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  stressed in an appearance on Thursday. "We will not raise interest rates for some time."\par \par \uc2 The fall of the yen to the precipice of 145 dollars, the barrier that has triggered the alarms, was almost an act of reflex to the new U.S. turn of the screw, which chose on Wednesday to raise the official price of money for the fifth time this year, raising rates to over 3% for the first time since 2008.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has managed, for the time being, to halt the yen's slump, which has regained some of the ground it has been losing, partly pushed by the rigorous wave of central banks around the world. Initial gains were around 2%. Tokyo's move sends a signal to the markets that it is ready to show where its limits lie, according to an analyst quoted by the \uc2 Financial Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has lost around 20% of its value against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, pushing up import costs and potentially weighing on consumption, while the country has been trying to contain the inflationary clawback since the end of the pandemic. Price rises are lower than in other corners of the globe, but Japan is no stranger to turbulence. Core inflation, excluding the more volatile food prices, reached 2.8% in August, exceeding the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's expectations for the fifth consecutive month.\par \par \uc2 The country has struggled to climb out of the coronavirus hole, dragging its feet behind other slowly recovering powers. It has been reviving the machinery throughout 2022 as covid-related restrictions were lifted. Its GDP grew by 3.5% in the second quarter, above forecasts.\par \par \uc2 The country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  nevertheless expects sustained improvement. It believes it is "likely" that the economy will continue on this growth path as the impact of covid fades and supply chains are greased, it said in an official statement issued after Thursday's meeting.\par \par \uc2 Although it expects the world's third-largest economic power to remain "under pressure" from rising prices stemming from the war in Ukraine, it believes the economy will continue to "grow at a pace above the potential growth rate" as "the virtuous cycle from income to spending" gradually intensifies. He is also confident of a slowdown in prices from 2023 onwards.\par \par \uc2 Yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a rare move. The last time \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  took action to support its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in 1998, during the Asian financial crisis. Thursday's action has been taking shape for months. In June, the yen appeared to go into a tailspin during a week of successive exchange rate falls. Its exchange rate against the dollar then reached its lowest level in 24 years, and since the great Asian crisis of the late 1990s. The Japanese authorities said at the end of the summer: "We are ready to respond in any way necessary".\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220922ei9m007s2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
