\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen surges after  \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes \highlight\uc2  with first support since 1998\b0\par\par\uc2 1,148 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2022\par 17:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par \par         * \par       "We have taken decisive action," says Japan's Kanda \par     \par \par         * \par       Dollar/yen has widest spread between daily high, low since 2016 \par     \par \par         * \par       Sterling, Swiss franc, Norway's crown slide after rate hikes \par     \par \par         * \par       Dollar hits new multi-year high vs sterling, euro\par     \par \par   \par  (Adds analyst comment, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  table, NEW YORK dateline, byline, updates prices)\par     By Alun John, Gertrude Chavez-Dreyfuss and Harry  Robertson\par        LONDON/NEW YORK, Sept 22 (Reuters) - The Japanese yen jumped across the board on Thursday after\par monetary authorities \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to boost the battered \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for the first\par time since 1998, although analysts said Japan may struggle to keep the yen strong for long.\par     The dollar was last down 1.5% at 141.91 yen. It hit a low of 140.31 after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ,\par having earlier reached a fresh 24-year peak of 145.9 yen. The spread between the day's high and low for the\par pair was the widest since June 2016. \par     The euro, Australian dollar and pound also plunged against the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , before regaining a\par little ground. \par     "We have taken decisive action," Japan's Vice Finance Minister for international affairs Masato Kanda\par told reporters, responding in the affirmative when asked if that meant \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     Confirmation of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  came just hours after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOJ\f28 \uc2 \f2  decided to maintain low interest rates to\par support the country's fragile economic recovery.\par     \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOJ\f28 \uc2 \f2  Governor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Haruhiko Kuroda\f28 \uc2 \f2  told reporters the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could hold off on hiking rates or changing\par its dovish policy guidance for years.\par     In contrast, central banks around the world, most notably the \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 , are raising rates\par aggressively and the policy divergence has weighed on the yen. \par     However, analysts said Japan can't keep propping up the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on a sustained basis.\par     "Over the next three to six months or possibly even longer, as long as those diverging paths of monetary\par policy are still in place and those differences persist, you'll continue to see a weaker yen," said Brendan\par McKenna, international economist and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wells Fargo Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2 . \par     "Yields in the U.S. today are up almost six basis points or so and yields in Japan are down. So I think\par if anything the spread should continue to favor the U.S. dollar as we go forward, and that's something that\par will continue to result in a weaker yen through the end of this year and probably into early 2023," he\par added.\par     The U.S. 10-year yield hit 3.68%, the highest since February 2011. It was last up 16 basis points at\par 3.674.\par     Even after Thursday's moves, the dollar is still up 23.2% against the yen this year. \par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  BONANZA \par     In a very busy day for markets, the pound pared the small advance it had made in London trading after\par the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of England\f28 \uc2 \f2  raised interest rates by 50 basis points. \par     The hike was in line with expectations but markets had been pricing in a small chance of a larger 75 bp\par move. \par     Sterling was last 0.2% higher against the dollar at $1.1295, not too far from a fresh 37-year\par low of $1.1213, hit in Asia trading. \par     "I'm quite surprised that the bank didn't take this opportunity to go 75 bps, particularly given the\par cover from some of the other global central banks. The pound looks particularly vulnerable here if the bank\par stays behind the curve," said Hugh Gimber, global markets strategist at JP Morgan Asset Management.\par     The euro was little changed at $0.9838, recovering from a new 20-year trough hit earlier in\par the global session.\par     The dollar index - which measures the greenback against a basket of six other major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  -\par was down 0.3% at 111.17, sliding from a 20-year high of 111.81 hit early in the day following the conclusion\par of the Fed's policy meeting on Wednesday. \par      The U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  issued new projections showing rates peaking at 4.6% next year with no cuts until\par 2024. It raised its target interest rate range by another 75 basis points (bps) overnight to 3%-3.25%, as\par was widely expected. \par     The dollar was already supported by demand for safe-haven assets after Russian President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Vladimir Putin\f28 \uc2 \f2 \par announced he would call up reservists to fight in Ukraine.\par     Separately, the Swiss franc tumbled after Switzerland's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  raised rates by 75 bps, when some\par had talked up the possibility of a full percentage point move. \par     The dollar and euro both climbed roughly 1.5% against the franc, with the dollar last at 0.9808 francs\par  and the euro at 0.9647 francs. \par     The Norwegian crown eased against the euro after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  there hiked interest rates by an\par expected 50 bps, and said there may be a more gradual approach to tightening ahead. \par     The euro was last up 0.5% at 10.2205 crowns.\par     ========================================================\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bid prices at 10:43AM (1443 GMT)\par  Description      RIC         Last           U.S. Close  Pct Change     YTD Pct       High Bid    Low Bid\par                                               Previous                   Change                   \par                                               Session                                             \par  Dollar index                 111.2700       111.4700    -0.16%         16.315%       +111.8100   +110.4600\par  Euro/Dollar                  $0.9825        $0.9838     -0.14%         -13.58%       +$0.9908    +$0.9807\par  Dollar/Yen                   141.8850       144.1050    -1.53%         +23.26%       +145.8900   +140.3100\par  Euro/Yen                     139.41         141.73      -1.64%         +6.98%        +143.7000   +138.7200\par  Dollar/Swiss                 0.9806         0.9663      +1.45%         +7.47%        +0.9849     +0.9622\par  Sterling/Dollar              $1.1276        $1.1270     +0.06%         -16.62%       +$1.1363    +$1.1213\par  Dollar/Canadian              1.3488         1.3466      +0.15%         +6.67%        +1.3544     +1.3410\par  Aussie/Dollar                $0.6633        $0.6633     +0.00%         -8.75%        +$0.6670    +$0.6574\par  Euro/Swiss                   0.9634         0.9507    