\par\par \b \uc2 Global Commodities Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,388 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2022\par 15:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering Commodities. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 0855 ET - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to change the yen's weakening trend, \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 's \uc2 George Saravelos\uc2  says in a report. "It is simply not credible for a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be debasing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  via extreme amounts of QE while authorities pursue a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the same time," Saravelos says. "For the JPY to start strengthening either the global dollar positive environment needs to turn or the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  needs to abandon" it's cap on yields. Saravelos worries that "\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will just lead to an unnecessary loss of reserves and credibility." The yen rises 2.5% versus the dollar, which is also weakening against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 0840 ET - Natural gas prices fall toward a six-week-low, down 1.7% to $7.646/mmBtu as investors await a potentially-bearish weekly EIA storage report at 10:30am ET. A WSJ survey is predicting a 95B cubic feet injection that would be well above the five-year average rise of 81. Some traders are even forecasting a result in the triple digits, which if confirmed could induce more selling of the commodity. Weaker demand for LNG in Asia is driving some of the bearish sentiment as countries such as Japan and South Korea indicate LNG prices have become too expensive, and they would rather go back to coal than pay the premium for cleaner-burning fuel. (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0828 ET - The US benchmark oil price rises 1.9% to $84.51 a barrel as the market returns toward the middle section of recently tight ranges following yesterday's moderate sell-off due to the Fed's rate hike. WTI crude has closed the week at $85 or $86 for the past three weeks straight, and looks like it may do so again tomorrow, or come close. Investors have allowed prices to consolidate slightly above eight-month-lows as a struggling global economy and slack demand keeps the buyers at bay, while the threat of production cuts by \uc2 OPEC\uc2  and a slowdown in US oil production growth have investors assuming prices won't fall much further from current levels. (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0756 ET - \uc2 Darden Restaurants\uc2  shares fall 2.2% in pre-market trading after the Olive Garden owner reports fiscal 1Q same-store sales rose 4.2%, while analysts polled by \uc2 FactSet\uc2  expected a 5.1% increase. Net income for the quarter ended Aug. 28 fell to $193M, from $230.9M in the year-ago period. Adjusted earnings per share of $1.56 met expectations. Sales were $2.45B, near analysts' expectations. Operating costs and expenses grew during the period from last year. (heather.haddon@wsj.com; @heatherhaddon)\par \par \uc2 0629 ET - Palm oil futures ended lower, following the U.S. Fed's move to raise interest rates to multi-year highs, while indicating further increases in the coming months. Higher interest rates typically attract investor funding away from the commodities market. While downward pressure is likely to persist, \uc2 Citi\uc2  analysts reckon palm oil is unlikely to suffer significant pull-backs and prices should be well supported into the year-end, given potential supply disruptions amid unfavorable weather conditions in Malaysia and Indonesia's producing regions. The benchmark Bursa Malaysia Derivatives contract for December delivery lost MYR58 to MYR3,829.00 a ton. (yifan.wang@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0524 ET - Comex gold futures ticked 0.2% lower to $1,678.80 a troy ounce on Thursday on the back of a stronger U.S. dollar after the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  raised interest rates and signaled more raises to come. Despite gold being considered one of the best inflation hedges there is, its appeal to investors tends to diminish amid elevated interest rates as it yields no interest. Meanwhile, three-month copper is down 0.8% to $7,659 a metric ton, while aluminum falls 2.3% to $2,203 a ton. Three-month nickel on the \uc2 LME\uc2  is up 0.6% to $25,000 a metric ton. (giulia.petroni@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0521 ET - Sentiment for copper in Europe is weakening much faster than key macroeconomic indicators amid power supply issues and potential shutdowns, says Graeme Train, head of metals research at commodity trading firm \uc2 Trafigura Group\uc2 . "What we're seeing at the moment is sentiment towards the macro environment decelerating much faster than hard data," Train says during the Fastmarkets Copper Conference in Barcelona. The pace of destocking is accelerating alongside a much faster pullback in purchasing activity, which is likely driving down investor activity, he says. Meanwhile, aluminum's current worries remain a red flag for copper, Train adds. (yusuf.khan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0437 ET - Higher recycling rates of copper scrap and secondary material are going to be needed to fulfill demand, according to Inka Guixa, chief executive of Spanish copper company La Farga. "The challenge for the copper sector is that the amount of copper is increasing and there will be a lot of demand," Guixa said during the Fastmarkets' Copper Conference in Barcelona. "We need to fulfill that demand, so we have increased capacity. Increasing recovery from end of life will be beneficial for the whole sector, [the question] is just how we do this across the value chain." Guixa called for further technological developments in recovery rates from copper products at the end of their lives so that more secondary material reenters the market. (yusuf.khan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0427 ET - Oil prices rebounded on Thursday after sliding in the previous session on the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 's third consecutive interest-rate rise of 0.75 percentage points. The Fed's move to tamp down inflation is expected to result in a sharper economic downturn that could weaken demand for oil and also make oil imports more expensive due to a stronger U.S. dollar. Yet, prices remain under pressure on fears of even tighter supply ahead of the winter after Russia's President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  ordered a partial mobilization of reservists and raised the threat of a nuclear response. Brent crude trades 1.2% higher at $90.90 a barrel and WTI futures are up 1.3% at $84.02 a barrel. (giulia.petroni@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2340 ET - Copper prices are slightly higher in early Asia trade, regaining some ground after overnight losses following the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's decision to raise interest rates to multiyear highs while signaling further increases by year-end. Galaxy Futures analysts say copper prices may begin to face some pressure after a broad recovery trend in recent weeks, as China's supply tightness is likely easing. They say this comes as improved weather conditions have helped ports to resume \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while domestic production is also picking up. Three-month \uc2 LME\uc2  contract rises 0.1% to $7,695 a ton. (yifan.wang@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2308 ET - Iron ore prices are higher in early Asian trade, extending a broad upturn in recent sessions driven by supply tightness and higher demand from peak steel-making seasonality. But Galaxy Futures analysts advise investors to remain cautious in the near term, as overall commodities trading sentiment is expected to remain muted and a risk-averse approach is likely to prevail after the Fed raised rates and signalled further increases by year-end. The analysts also point to longer-term demand weakness amid China's real estate liquidity crisis. The most-traded January iron-ore contract on the \uc2 Dalian Commodity Exchange\uc2  gains 3.0% to CNY716.5 a ton. (yifan.wang@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2052 ET - Gold is lower in the early Asian session amid a stronger USD and mostly higher Treasury yields following the Fed's 75bp rate increase and its projections for further increases in the coming months. However, gold's decline may be limited on safe-haven demand spurred by an escalation in the Russia-Ukraine war after Russian President Putin ordered reservists to mobilize and made remarks perceived as a threat to use nuclear weapons, analysts say. Spot gold is down 0.8% at $1,660.70/oz. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 22, 2022 09:15 ET (13:15 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220922ei9m0020j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
