\par\par \b \uc2 Global  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income Roundup: Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,514 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2022\par 12:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 The latest Market Talks covering \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Fixed Income. Published exclusively on Dow Jones Newswires throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 1041 GMT - \uc2 Citi\uc2 's analysts expect a 50 basis point interest rate rise by the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  later on Thursday, with votes split between 25, 50 and 75 basis points, but they reckon few would be surprised by a 75bps move. This is "not least given peer pressure from the size of hikes elsewhere, including the Fed's third 75bp last night," they say. "We think it's not about precise policy calibration at this point, but rather the optics of looking tough on inflation," they say. Economists expect the BOE to raise the bank rate by 50 basis points to 2.25%, according to The Wall Street Journal's poll. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1036 GMT - The key interest rate of the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  is back in positive territory for the first time since January 2015 and is no longer the lowest in the world, ING says in a note. This rate hike is intended to combat inflation in Switzerland, which reached 3.5% in August. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expects inflation to average 3.0% in 2022 and fall to 2.4% in 2023. Given the inflation forecasts, the SNB could raise its rate by 75 basis points in December, according to ING. "The deteriorating economic outlook and the expected decline in inflation in 2023 should be enough to leave the SNB comfortable with a rate of 1.25% for the year as a whole," ING says. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1029 GMT - Higher interest rates usually make a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  more attractive to foreign investors but this isn't the case for the U.K. currently, Qontigo says. \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  is expected to raise its base rate to similar heights as the U.S. federal funds rate but sterling could extend recent losses against the dollar, Christoph Schon, senior principal of applied research at Qontigo, says in a note. "The U.K.'s large trade deficit is likely to put more downward pressure on the pound, while also fuelling inflation." The \uc2 BOE\uc2  sets out its latest interest rate decision at 1100 GMT. GBP/USD rises 0.3% to 1.1307, having earlier reached its lowest level since March 1985 at 1.1212, according to FactSet.(renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1027 GMT - The \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  is likely to proceed with outright gilt sales in a decision at 1100 GMT, when it is expected to raise its base rate by 50 basis points to 2.25%, although the amount of sales is uncertain, analysts at \uc2 RBC\uc2  say in a note. The government's plans to fund a cap on domestic energy bills from October, due to be announced Friday, leaves the size of gilt sales "open to more question than previously thought." Although the impact on gilt issuance of the fiscal plan makes it possible that the BOE won't opt to commence gilt sales now, this is unlikely "given how much weight they have placed on predictability in terms of their balance sheet reduction strategy." (miriam.mukuru@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1026 GMT - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has boosted the yen, taking the market by surprise, but its effects may not last, says Osamu Takashima, head of G-10 \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  at \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 . The attempt to bolster the yen through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears at odds with the \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary easing, Takashima says. Also, since \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  officials generally don't favor the idea of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely to undertake a massive yen-buying campaign, he says. USD/JPY is at 142.85, down from the day's high of 145.91. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 1007 GMT - The \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  raised its policy rate from minus 0.25% to 0.50% and gave hawkish signals, so \uc2 HSBC\uc2  expects the policy to be lifted by 50 basis points in December. "Thursday's meeting confirms that the SNB is still worried about the risk of long-term inflation expectations becoming de-anchored," \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's economist Chantana Sam says in a note. Inflation forecasts were again revised up compared to June, with annual inflation now seen at 3.0% in 2022 and 2.4% in 2023, instead of 2.8% and 1.9% previously. The SNB left the door open to further rate hikes this year in its statement, Sam says. However, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  sees no further changes on the policy rates in 2023, as inflation is likely to start to come down. (maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1002 GMT - Record inflation in the U.K. and the eurozone is eroding households' real incomes and \uc2 UBS\uc2  says the hit has been unequal across high and low income households. This is because energy and food account for a higher share of consumption in lower income households than in higher ones, economists at \uc2 UBS\uc2  say in a note. In addition, higher income households are more likely to benefit from "excess" savings, a buffer to consumption, accumulated during the Covid-19 pandemic, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says. Economists at the Swiss bank argue that fiscal support measures should target lower income households. If this isn't the case, \uc2 UBS\uc2  says tighter monetary policy would be needed to bring inflation back to target.(maria.martinez@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0944 GMT - The series of "aggressive Fed rate hikes" recently may raise the risk of more fund outflows and weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Southeast Asia, \uc2 Fidelity International\uc2  strategist Christopher Wong says in a note. The Fed "still managed to outhawk the markets through the September dot plot and the Summary of Economic Projections" on Wednesday as it raised interest rates by a widely expected 75 basis points, Wong says. Southeast Asian central banks are also likely to tighten monetary policies given inflation levels are above their targets, he adds. But they "will have to strike a delicate balance" or growth could be hurt, Wong says.(clarence.leong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0941 GMT - \uc2 Citi\uc2 's rates strategists expect the \uc2 U.K. Debt Management Office\uc2  to increase the gilt remit by around GBP60 billion to GBP191.2 billion in a review on Friday. The increased issuance is aimed at funding the energy bailout for the winter. \uc2 Citi\uc2  expects more issuance in short- and medium-term gilts, but a reduction in issuance in long-dated and inflation-linked gilts. "The next two days, then, will likely underline the misalignment of monetary and fiscal policy," they say in a note, refering to the \uc2 BOE\uc2 's expected interest rate rise and selling of gilts alongside the DMO's issuance of record amounts of gilts at higher yields. \uc2 Citi\uc2 's strategists expect 10-year gilt yields to approach 4%. They last trade more than 3 basis points lower at 3.288%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2 . (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0940 GMT - \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could leave interest rates unchanged in a policy decision at 1100 GMT after unexpectedly cutting rates in August but this may have little impact on the Turkish lira, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. The lira's modest reaction to August's decision shows the market has "given up hoping for monetary policy to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," \uc2 Commerzbank\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst \uc2 Esther Reichelt\uc2  says in a note. "That means today's decision is unlikely to have much of an effect on the TRY exchange rates either." USD/TRY rises 0.1% to 18.3615 after earlier hitting a record high of 18.3835, according to \uc2 FactSet\uc2 , following the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's decision to lift rates by 75 basis points and signalled further large increases. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0924 GMT - Comex gold futures ticked 0.2% lower to $1,678.80 a troy ounce on Thursday on the back of a stronger U.S. dollar after the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  raised interest rates and signaled more raises to come. Despite gold being considered one of the best inflation hedges there is, its appeal to investors tends to diminish amid elevated interest rates as it yields no interest. Meanwhile, three-month copper is down 0.8% to $7,659 a metric ton, while aluminum falls 2.3% to $2,203 a ton. Three-month nickel on the \uc2 LME\uc2  is up 0.6% to $25,000 a metric ton. (giulia.petroni@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0916 GMT - Japanese financial authorities likely want to slow the pace of the yen's recent weakening, which has been too fast, says Michiyoshi Kato, director of fixed income sales at \uc2 Mizuho Securities\uc2 . Kato says many traders had been very skeptical the Japanese authorities could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to support the yen, and authorities likely wanted to demonstrate that they can. He says he doesn't believe Japanese authorities are trying to make the yen strong. USD/JPY is now at 142.14, down from the day's high of 145.91. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 22, 2022 06:41 ET (10:41 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220922ei9m000zn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pich