\par\par \b \uc2 POLL- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  unlikely to  \highlight22\uc2 intervene \highlight\uc2  to stem weak yen, half of economists say\b0\par\par\uc2 599 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2022\par 06:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 * Open reuters://realtime/verb=Open/url=cpurl://apps.cp./Apps/econ-polls?RIC=JPGDPAP GDP poll data\par \par \uc2 * Open reuters://realtime/verb=Open/url=cpurl://apps.cp./Apps/econ-polls?RIC=JPCPIAP core CPI poll data\par \par \uc2 By Kantaro Komiya\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 21 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's decline, said just over half of economists polled by Reuters, though a fifth said weakening beyond 150 per U.S. dollar could trigger action.\par \par \uc2 With a widening gap between the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's (\uc2 BOJ\uc2 ) ultra-loose policy and rapid tightening of its global peers, the yen has lost nearly 20% against the greenback this year, hitting a 24-year low of 144.99 and prompting policymakers this month to flag a readiness to act in the face of volatile \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement.\par \par \uc2 The market is bracing for further volatility and possible government action ahead of an upcoming series of major \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meetings, including the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 , U.S. Federal Reserve and Bank of England. The yen traded at around 143 on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Still, a slim majority of economists thought any direct action was a long shot. Twelve of 23 respondents, or 52%, said the government would not buy yen to stop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from further weakening, the Sept. 8-19 poll showed.\par \par \uc2 Any internationally coordinated action also is unlikely as the United States favours a strong dollar to curb inflation, said Akiyoshi Takumori, chief economist at Sumitomo Mitsui DS Asset Management.\par \par \uc2 "The 'rate check' surprised everyone, but \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 -signalling would be the last thing they could do," Takumori said, referring to the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  last week asking \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders for current rates - queries widely seen as a prelude to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Five respondents said 150 yen per dollar would prompt \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Hiroshi Watanabe, senior economist at Sony Financial Group, said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "might be possible if the yen falls beyond the 150 line at very high speed, but in reality, it's extremely unlikely given the ineffectiveness of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ".\par \par \uc2 Another three chose "155 yen per dollar" when asked for a trigger. Two selected 160 and one picked "weaker than 165 yen per dollar".\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last carried out yen-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1998, when the Asian financial crisis triggered a yen sell-off and rapid capital outflow.\par \par \uc2 WEAKER GROWTH, FASTER INFLATION\par \par \uc2 Economists in the survey downgraded their growth outlook for Japan due to accelerating inflation, coronavirus resurgence and a global economic slowdown.\par \par \uc2 The economy will likely expand an annualised 1.4% in July-September, less than the 2.0% forecast in an August poll, showed the median estimate of 35 respondents. The projection for October-December was 1.9%, versus the previous poll's 2.2%.\par \par \uc2 In the second quarter this year, the world's third-largest economy grew 3.5%, helped by robust consumer and corporate spending.\par \par \uc2 The core consumer price index (CPI), which excludes volatile fresh food items, will likely rise 2.8% in the last three months of this year, the poll showed, more than the previously estimated 2.5%.\par \par \uc2 Economists expected core CPI to rise to 2.4% this fiscal year, before slowing to 1.2% in fiscal 2023, the poll showed.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in the poll, \uc2 BOJ\uc2  Deputy Governor \uc2 Masayoshi Amamiya\uc2  was economists' top pick for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next chief to succeed incumbent \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2  in the spring.\par \par \uc2 (For other stories from the Reuters global economic poll: )\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Kantaro Komiya; Polling by Arsh Mogre and Devayani Sathyan; Editing by Jonathan Cable and Christopher Cushing) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-21T05:05:01.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220921ei9l00fw9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
