\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECTED- \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -U.S. dollar sails higher as markets price in hefty Fed rate hike\b0\par\par\uc2 1,006 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 September 2022\par 22:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  (In 6th paragraph, corrects to show the dollar is on track in 2022 to post its largest yearly percentage\par gain in 38 years, not 18)\par     * Markets in consolidation mode ahead of Fed policy decision\par     * Fed funds futures price in 19% chance of 100-basis-point hike\par     * China's yuan hits 26-month low against dollar\par     * New Zealand dollar falls to lowest since May 2020 vs U.S. dollar\par \par     By Gertrude Chavez-Dreyfuss\par     NEW YORK, Sept 19 (Reuters) - The dollar rose against major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Monday, trading within narrow\par ranges, ahead of a slew of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  meetings this week led by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve\f28 \uc2 \f2 , which is likely to\par raise interest rates by another 75 basis points (bps).\par     Volume was light overall, with markets in London and Tokyo closed for public holidays.\par     World stock markets remained on edge, however, and the dollar maintained its firm tone, given\par expectations that the Fed would maintain its aggressive tightening path until next year to contain\par stubbornly high inflation.\par     Fed funds futures have priced in an 81% chance of a 75 bps rate hike this week and a 19% probability of\par a 100-increase at the conclusion of the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's two-day policy meeting, according to Refinitiv\par data\par     The dollar index, which measures the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against six counterparts, was up 0.1% at 109.62,\par not far from 20-year high of 110.79 hit on Sept. 7.\par     Since the beginning of the year, the dollar index has surged 14.7%, on track for its best yearly\par percentage gain in 38 years.\par     "Generally, the trend is your friend until the bend in the end. The dollar is going to follow that\par pretty well," said Amo Sahota, executive director, at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  consulting firm Klarity \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in San Francisco.\par     "Will there be more dollar strength before the FOMC (Federal Open Market Committee)? I think the market\par is going to pull a little bit here. It will go into a holding pattern and some consolidation," he added.\par     This week is also smattered with holidays that could thin liquidity and result in sharper price moves,\par with Japan and Britain off on Monday, Australia on Thursday, and Japan again on Friday, among others.\par     In other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the euro was little changed against the dollar at $1.0021, sterling\par slipped 0.1% to $1.1424 and within sight of Friday's 37-year low of $1.13510, while the New Zealand\par fell 0.6% to US$0.5958.\par     Earlier in the session, the New Zealand unit fell to its lowest since May 2020 of US$0.5933\par     The Canadian dollar fell to its lowest in almost two years to C$1.3354 per U.S. dollar. The\par U.S. dollar last changed hands at $1.3258, flat on the day.\par     Against the yen, the dollar rose 0.2% to $143.18, hovering beneath a strong resistance level\par at 145 that has been reinforced by Japanese policymakers' toughened talk of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par     The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is widely expected to stick with massive stimulus at its meeting on Wednesday and\par Thursday, keeping its ultra-loose policy in place. But a turning point in Japanese monetary policy may come\par sooner than has been thought, with the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  recently dropping the word "temporary" for its\par description of elevated inflation.\par     China's yuan ended at a fresh 26-month low on Monday and traded below the psychologically critical\par 7-per-dollar level. In offshore trade, the yuan was 0.35 weaker.\par     Bitcoin, the biggest cryptocurrency by market value, fell to a three-month low below $19,000,\par as unease over rising interest rates globally knocked risk assets. It was last down 0.2% at\par $19,381.\par     Ether, the cryptocurrency used in the Ethereum blockchain, rallied from a two-month low against the\par dollar and was last 1.4% higher at $1,358.60. \par     Ethereum went through a major software upgrade last week that alters the way ether tokens are created,\par drastically reducing its energy usage.\par     \par     ========================================================\par     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bid prices at 3:19PM (1919 GMT)\par  Description      RIC         Last           U.S. Close  Pct Change     YTD Pct       High Bid    Low Bid\par                                               Previous                   Change                   \par                                               Session                                             \par  Dollar index                 109.6000       109.5500    +0.06%         14.569%       +110.1800   +109.4800\par  Euro/Dollar                  $1.0022        $1.0015     +0.07%         -11.84%       +$1.0029    +$0.9966\par  Dollar/Yen                   143.2100       142.9400    +0.19%         +24.40%       +143.6400   +142.6500\par  Euro/Yen                     143.51         143.13      +0.27%         +10.12%       +143.5500   +142.8100\par  Dollar/Swiss                 0.9645         0.9644      +0.17%         +5.90%        +0.9695     +0.9