\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Government  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  in exchange rates:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  as a case study\b0\par\par\uc2 1,517 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 September 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The yen's recent depreciation to a twenty-four year low against the dollar has raised hackles due to the yen's traditional safe haven role in times of turmoil, such as the war in Ukraine. The yen's slide had already begun as major central banks signaled a tightening of monetary policy to fight inflation, while the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  redoubled its loose monetary policy and its zero target for 10-year bond yields. The depreciation accelerated further as oil and gas prices soared, weakening Japan's terms of trade.\par \par \uc2 Read also: Despite our own inflation, dollar dominance drives down the yen and euroRead also: What caused Japan's postwar economic miracle?Leading analysts were cautious about the falling yen and its negative impact on import prices and consumption, but recommended that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  continue its ultra-loose monetary policy to reflate the economy and support growth. What these analysts fail to understand is that, in a long-term perspective, the value of the yen relative to other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is anchored in economic fundamentals driven by monetary developments and its purchasing power.\par \par \uc2 Since the yen's sharp appreciation following the Plaza Accord in 1985, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has retained its relative strength against the dollar despite Japan's ultra-loose fiscal and monetary policies. This is because money creation has proceeded at a slower pace in Japan than in the US, as the Plaza Accord marked the beginning of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's lost decades, illustrating well how disruptive government \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets can be.\par \par \uc2 The Plaza Accord\par \par \uc2 For more than three decades, following its sudden appreciation in the wake of the 1985 Plaza Accord (Figure 1), the yen has followed a stable trajectory against the dollar. The Plaza Accord among the G-5 countries - the United States, West Germany, France, Japan and the United Kingdom - was the first major experiment in international monetary cooperation to revalue the exchange rate system.\par \par \uc2 At the urging of the United States, which wanted to devalue the dollar and reduce the trade deficit, the five central banks agreed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to rebalance international trade and growth. The alleged overvaluation of the US dollar was the result of relatively high interest rates promoted by the Fed during 1980-82 to quell inflation, combined with Ronald Reagan's expansionary fiscal policy during 1981-84, which led to capital inflows and dollar appreciation.\par \par \uc2 High budget deficits coupled with buoyant domestic demand swelled the trade deficit, producing the famous "twin deficits" of the 1980s. In reality, it was not the exchange rate that was the problem, but US fiscal profligacy and the excessive expansion of the money supply, which exceeded 12% in 1983. Instead of fixing domestic policies, the \uc2 US government\uc2  convinced Japan and Germany to manipulate their exchange rates and increase domestic demand. As Ludwig von Mises so aptly put it, "What governments call international monetary cooperation is concerted action for the sake of credit expansion."\par \par \uc2 Chart 1: Spot exchange rate between the yen and the U.S. dollar\par \par \uc2 Following concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the yen appreciated from about 240 units to the dollar in September 1985 to 153 units in 1986. By 1988, the yen had nearly doubled in value to an exchange rate of 120 units to the dollar. Many analyses, including reports by the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2 , agree that the significant government-driven appreciation of the yen sowed the seeds of Japan's subsequent and lasting economic debacle.\par \par \uc2 In the first half of 1986, as Japan's exports plunged following the yen's appreciation, the economy went into recession. The sale of dollars by the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  also reduced the money supply and domestic demand. The authorities overreacted and introduced considerable macroeconomic stimulus, cutting interest rates five times, by a total of 3 percentage points, by 1989. A large fiscal package was introduced in 1987, even though the economy was already recovering. Credit growth then soared, fueling a stock market and real estate boom that exploded in the early 1990s and triggered nearly three decades of dismal economic performance.\par \par \uc2 Monetary developments maintained yen strengthInstead of allowing a curative recession to liquidate the malinvestments of the late 1980s boom, Japan redoubled growth stimulus, which eroded the capital stock, labor productivity and real wages and brought lasting economic stagnation. Based on Japan's ultra-loose monetary and fiscal policies, a dramatic weakening of the yen was to be expected.\par \par \uc2 However, growth stimuli kept alive zombie companies and banks riddled with bad assets, reducing credit growth. In addition, private capital outflows soared due to elevated economic uncertainty fueled by the government's anti-market policies, denting further money creation. Although the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's balance sheet increased several times more than that of its peers due to aggressive asset purchases (Chart 2), the overall increase in broad money supply (M3) remained very moderate relative to the US and the euro area (Chart 3). Therefore, the slower money supply growth in Japan explains both the relative strength of the yen after its sharp appreciation episode and the weak inflation in Japan. From a longer-term perspective, the mainstream's sudden concern about a dramatic weakening of the yen seems misplaced, particularly when combined with the incoherent recommendation that the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  maintain its loose monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 Chart 2: Balance sheet size: \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  versus other central banks.\par \par \uc2 Source: Borrallo Egea and del R\u237\'00\'EDo L\u243\'00\'F3pez, \uc2 Bank of Spain.Chart\uc2  3: Money supply growth (M3) in Japan vs. the US and the euro area\par \par \uc2 Note: In order to better reflect changes in purchasing power parities, which are the main driver of the main driver of exchange rate parities, M3 growth exchange rate parities, M3 growth has been deflated by real GDP growth.The Plaza Accord and the U.S. trade deficit.To what extent have Plaza's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  solved the US trade deficit? The US trade deficit continued to grow in 1986 and 1987 and was not reduced until later, fueling skepticism about the positive impact of the agreement. In "Plaza Accord, 30 Years Later," \uc2 Jeffrey Frankel\uc2  admits that governments are not capable of altering the exchange rate over a long period without a corresponding change in fundamentals, but argues that exchange rate \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  can be effective in the short run, particularly if they shape market expectations and are coordinated among major central banks, as was the case in 1985.\par \par \uc2 Thus, Frankel believes that the Plaza Accord \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  proved useful in reducing both the apparently overvalued US dollar and the trade deficit, but only with a two-year lag. However, this explanation ignores the fact that the US economy slowed considerably after the October 1987 stock market crash and eventually went into recession in 1990.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, the budget deficit also narrowed after 1987, pointing to a slowdown in money creation. In fact, the annual growth rate of the monetary aggregate M3 slowed down considerably, from a peak of 12.2% in 1983 to 8.9% in 1985 and 4.1% in 1989 (Figure 4). This nicely illustrates Mises and Murray Rothbard's idea that the balance of payments is driven by intentional changes in people's cash balances and is not "unfavorable" if there is a gold drain, which is a mercantilist fallacy.\par \par \uc2 A trade deficit reflects a voluntary reduction in the amount of money held by the public in favor of purchasing goods and services or reflects a domestic inflationary policy that increases the nominal income of citizens. In the case of the US, the dollar's status as the world's major reserve \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  allows fiscal and monetary expansion to be used to finance trade deficits with relative ease, the dollar being less prone to depreciation than other fiat \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . An acceleration of money creation can increase trade deficits and vice versa, which does not imply that the dollar is overvalued by the markets and that governments should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to adjust the exchange rate parity.\par \par \uc2 Figure 4: Growth rate of broad money (M3) in the US.\par \par \uc2 ConclusionThe equilibrium exchange rate between two \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is given by their purchasing power parity - that is, the ratio of the purchasing power of each in terms of other economic goods. Any attempt to divert the exchange rate from its market value through government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to be undone by market arbitrage, which restores the exchange rate to its purchasing power parity. A lasting change in the value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can take place when governments use monetary policy levers to adjust interest rates and the money supply. In this case, the change in exchange rates reflects the movement of domestic prices and alters the balance of trade, as explained by Mises. However, monetary policy \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  introduce other distortions into the economy that can have serious economic consequences, as illustrated by Japan's lost decades after the Plaza Accord.\par \par \uc2 This article was originally published on Mises.orgMihai\par \par \uc2 Macovei\par \par \uc2 ([email protected]) is a research associate at the Ludwig von Mises Institute in Romania. Tags: dollarinterventionismJapanMonetary policyYen\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220918ei9i0040c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
