\par\par \b \uc2 Indian Morning Briefing: Asian Markets Broadly Higher; \uc2 PBOC\uc2  Keeps Key Policy Rate Unchanged\b0\par\par\uc2 1,232 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2022\par 05:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL MARKETS \par DJIA         31135.09    30.12   0.10% \par Nasdaq       11719.68    86.10   0.74% \par S&P 500       3946.01    13.32   0.34% \par FTSE 100      7277.30  -108.56  -1.47% \par Nikkei Stock 27907.08    88.46   0.32% \par Hang Seng    18982.27   135.17   0.72% \par Kospi         2419.71     8.29   0.34% \par SGX Nifty*   18045.50    55.5    0.31% \par *Sept contract \par  \par USD/JPY  143.29-30  +0.09% \par Range    143.45   142.80 \par EUR/USD  0.9974-77  -0.05% \par Range    0.9987   0.9970 \par  \par CBOT Wheat Sept $8.536 per bushel \par Spot Gold   $1,697.10/oz Unch \par Nymex Crude (NY) $88.68   $1.37 \par  \par  \par U.S. STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks inched higher Wednesday, recovering some losses from the previous day's wild session that was spurred by a hotter-than-expected inflation report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 added 13.32 points, or 0.3%, to 3946.01, a day after the benchmark index plummeted 4.3% in its worst selloff since June 2020. The Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 30.12 points, or 0.1%, to 31135.09. The tech-focused Nasdaq Composite gained 86.10 points, or 0.7%, to 11719.68. The indexes wobbled between small gains and losses throughout the session. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese stocks were slightly higher, led by railway and airline stocks, as hopes continue that the government will ease Covid-19-related border rules to attract foreign visitors. \f28 \uc2 \f2 East Japan Railway\f28 \uc2 \f2  was up 2.6% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 ANA Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  was 1.6% higher. Investors were closely watching Japanese government officials' comments on the yen as uncertainty continues over the Fed's pace of tightening. USD/JPY was at 143.10, compared with 143.85 as of Wednesday's Tokyo stock market close. The Nikkei Stock Average was 0.1% higher at 27855.65.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's benchmark Kospi was flat at 2410.66 after opening higher amid choppy trade. Korean won movements would likely be closely watched after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hit a 13-year low versus the U.S. dollar, amid concerns that the U.S. Fed will further raise interest rates following a hotter-than-expected inflation data. Electronics stocks were mixed, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 SK-Electronics\f28 \uc2 \f2  falling 0.5% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Samsung Electronics\f28 \uc2 \f2  up 0.4%. Amid tech stocks, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kakao Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2  . was 0.6% higher and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Naver Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2  . was down 0.2%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong shares opened lower but quickly turned positive, rebounding from yesterday's six-month closing low. This followed Beijing's move to extend tax deferral for smaller manufacturers in a bid to support economic growth. Property developers led gains, with \f28 \uc2 \f2 Country Garden Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  jumping 8.3%, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Longfor Group\f28 \uc2 \f2  up 6.0% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 China Resources Land\f28 \uc2 \f2  adding 3.2%. Among tech shares, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tencent Holdings\f28 \uc2 \f2  was up 0.6% after subsidiary \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tencent Music Entertainment\f28 \uc2 \f2  filed to list in Hong Kong by way of introduction. Among decliners, \f28 \uc2 \f2 NetEase\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Baidu\f28 \uc2 \f2  were each down more than 1%. The benchmark Hang Seng Index was up 0.4% at 18927.08.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese shares were largely higher in early trade. The benchmark Shanghai Composite Index rose 0.5% to 3253.36 and the Shenzhen Composite Index gained 0.2% to 2105.23, while the ChiNext Price Index was flat at 2503.70 after opening higher. "The absence of any clear resolution in China's Covid-19 policy and uncertainty on further moderation in economic conditions ahead remain a weighing block for risk sentiments," IG market strategist Yeap Jun Rong says in a note. Among lenders, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Agricultural Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  is flat and Bank of China rises 0.3%. \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were consolidating against USD after sharp movements during the past two days caused by hotter-than-expected U.S. inflation and media reports of \f28 \uc2 \f2 BOJ\f28 \uc2 \f2  rate check in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market. Factors including better economic fundamentals in Asia were helping to smooth volatility in the region's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets for now, said ING economists in a morning commentary. USD/KRW rose 0.2% to 1,394.79 and USD/SGD was little changed at 1.4059.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Goldman Sachs\f28 \uc2 \f2  sees further scope for JPY weakness in the near term, given its economists' latest upward revisions to the Fed's rate-increase path and its rates strategists foreseeing upside risks to \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasury\f28 \uc2 \f2  yields, said analyst Karen Reichgott Fishman in a research report. \f28 \uc2 \f2 GS\f28 \uc2 \f2  's standard \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  model suggests that if the 10-year Treasury yield rises to 4.5%, USD/JPY could rally by another 7% to nearly 155, all else being equal, the analyst says. While likelihood of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  looks low, escalation in verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  together with reported "rate checks" should raise the odds priced into markets, the analyst added. \f28 \uc2 \f2 GS\f28 \uc2 \f2  remains comfortable with its 3-month USD/JPY forecast of 145.00. USD/JPY was up 0.1% at 143.32. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par METALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold prices were little changed in Asian trade, after declining overnight as investors continued to digest Tuesday's surprisingly strong U.S. August inflation data. "Gold's fate will be determined next week when policy makers decide if the Fed needs to be even more aggressive with fighting inflation," Oanda senior market analyst \f28 \uc2 \f2 Edward Moya\f28 \uc2 \f2  said in a note. "Gold could be vulnerable to a tumble towards $1,650 and possibly much lower if the Fed signals more aggressive rate hikes remain on the table," he added. Spot gold was flat at $1,697.10/oz. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par OIL SUMMARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil prices were higher in early Asia trade, extending overnight gains amid speculation that the Biden administration may consider refilling the U.S. oil reserve at $80 a barrel. Sentiment is also partly supported by news that China's city of Chengdu would gradually ease its lockdown measures. "With Iranian oil exports unlikely to rebound any time soon given setbacks to the revival of the 2015 nuclear accord, we continue to believe that oil markets will tighten by the end of the year, supporting our call for Brent futures to lift to $US100/bbl in Q4," \f28 \uc2 \f2 CBA\f28 \uc2 \f2  strategist Vivek Dhar said in a note. Front-month WTI futures rose 0.6% to $88.99/bbl and Brent gained 0.4% to $94.51/bbl. \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES \par  \par China Announces $28.73 Billion Relending Quota for Manufacturers, Service Providers \par China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Keeps Key Policy Rate Unchanged \par Supplier Inflation Stayed Elevated in August \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Amtrak\f28 \uc2 \f2   to Suspend Long-Distance Services as U.S. Railroad Strike Looms \par Japan Exports Grow on Robust Demand \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 RBA\f28 \uc2 \f2   Board Members May Face Calls for Added Transparency \par Australian Unemployment Edges Higher, Job Market Stays Drum Tight \par New Zealand's Economy Rebounds as Covid Lockdowns End \par EU Seeks to Raise $140 Billion Clawing Back Energy Profits \par Global Oil Demand Undermined by China Lockdowns, \f28 \uc2 \f2 IEA\f28 \uc2 \f2   Says \par TikTok Exec Says Agreement With \f28 \uc2 \f2 U.S. Government\f28 \uc2 \f2   to Address User-Data Concerns \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Disney\f28 \uc2 \f2  's Chapek Hints at All-in-One Streaming App \par Tencent Music Seeks Hong Kong Listing Via Introduction \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Google\f28 \uc2 \f2   Makes Cuts to In-House Startup Incubator \par U.S. to Withhold $130 Million in Security Aid to Egypt Over Human Rights Concerns \par U.S. Considers Sanctions on Iran-Linked Entities After Salman Rushdie Attack \par Far-Right Party in Sweden Drives Opposition Bloc to Election Win \par Justice Department Charges Three Iranians With Ransomware Attacks \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 14, 2022 23:00 ET (03:00 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220915ei9f0001b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
