\par\par \b \uc2 Fitch\uc2  Cuts China GDP Growth Forecast -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,493 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2022\par 09:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0713 GMT - \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  lowers its forecast for China's GDP growth this year to 2.8% from 3.7%, citing weaker-than-expected 2Q economic data, a deeper slump in the country's property market and the deteriorating global outlook. For 2023, the ratings company now expects the Chinese economy to expand 5.3%, compared with its previous forecast of 4.5% growth. Beijing set an official growth target of around 5.5% for 2022 earlier this year but stopped mentioning it since the middle of the year. (singaporeeditors@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0701 GMT - USD/JPY may eventually rise to 147 or higher, although this might take longer than expected owing to potential rate checks and \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , say \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  analysts in a research report. Some significant factors may result in further JPY weakness, such as a widening policy rate gap between Japan and other countries, and Japanese households beginning to accumulate foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposits, the analysts say. Rate checks and actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are unlikely to alter the path of JPY depreciation as current JPY weakness is in line with fundamentals, the analysts add, noting data today showing Japan posted Y2.8 trillion trade deficit in August, thelargest-ever deficit for a single month. USD/JPY is up 0.4% at 143.69. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0642 GMT - ABN Amro expects German 10-year Bund yields to decline from the fourth quarter onward, reflecting recession risks the eurozone economy is facing, says rates strategist Sonia Renoult in a note. ABN Amro forecasts the 10-year Bund yield to decline to 1.55% in the fourth quarter from 1.76% in 3Q 2022, to 1.25% in 1Q 2023 and to 1.10% in 2Q 2023 before a mild increase again. "Once the economy shows signs of stabilisation with improving economic and market conditions, we expect the market to shift to a risk-on mode again moving Bund yields higher in H2 2023," Renoult says. Eurozone 10-year government bond yields rise across the board with the German Bund yield trading 3.5 basis points higher at 1.737%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0634 GMT - Japanese stocks ended higher, led by gains in railway and airline stocks, on hopes of easing pandemic-related border restrictions. \uc2 East Japan Railway\uc2  and \uc2 Japan Airlines\uc2  climbed 1.9% each. The Nikkei Stock Average rose 0.2% to 27875.91. USD/JPY was at 143.64, compared with 143.17 as of Wednesday 5 p.m. ET. U.S. economic data are in focus, including retail sales and weekly jobless claims due later in the day. The yield on the 10-year Japanese government bond was flat at 0.250%. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 0628 GMT - China's economic activity likely showed signs of improvement in August with faster growth in industrial output and retail sales, according to 15 economists polled by The Wall Street Journal. Industrial output likely rose 4% on year in August, up slightly from the 3.8% increase in July. Retail sales likely increased 4% from a year earlier in August, up from 2.7% in July. Growth in fixed-asset investment may have slowed slightly to 5.5% on year in the January-August period, compared with a 5.7% increase in the first seven months. The National Bureau of Statistics is due to release the data on Friday. (singaporeeditors@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0625 GMT - German Bunds are expected to remain better supported after 10-year yields have repeatedly run into resistance near 1.8%, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  's rates strategist Rainer Guntermann and head of interest rates strategy Michael Leister write in a note. "Bunds remain vulnerable as terminal rate expectations are edging higher and risk sentiment stabilises, but 10y yields should continue to find support at the year-to-date highs near 1.8%," the strategists say. Markets' expectations of \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  terminal rates have risen to above 2.5% after last week's monetary policy meeting and they look to stabilize around this level, according to Refinitiv data. The 10-year Bund yield is trading almost 4 basis points higher at 1.739%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0614 GMT - Greece lines up for scheduled rating reviews by \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Investors Service and DBRS on Friday, and the former might upgrade Greece, say \uc2 Citi\uc2  's rates strategists in a note. "Tomorrow's rating reviews of Greece by \uc2 Moody's\uc2  and DBRS could be significant for GGBs [Greek government bonds]," \uc2 Citi\uc2  's strategists say. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  rating of Greece at 'Ba3' with stable outlook is below that of \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  , S&P Global Ratings and DBRS, they say. "Net, we see scope for a catchup by \uc2 Moody's\uc2  via a one-notch upgrade to Ba2," \uc2 Citi\uc2  's strategists say. Meanwhile, they expect no change by DBRS which upgraded Greece to 'BB' (high) in March, the highest assessment among the four main agencies, the strategists say. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0551 GMT - USD/SGD is steady in the afternoon Asian session, supported by prospects of a large Fed rate hike later this month. Fed fund futures still suggest a 26% probability of a 100bp increase, say Maybank analysts in a research report. The market focus remains on USD Index's upside, buoyed by the possibility of a large rate increase and by lingering fragile sentiment, the analysts say. On daily chart, USD/SGD's bullish momentum has largely moderated, while its relative strength index isn't showing a clear bias, the analysts add, pegging resistance at 1.4100 and support at 1.3930. USD/SGD is little changed at 1.4064. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0532 GMT - The Japanese government may be able to use its version of U.S. Homeland Investment Act, which provided a tax holiday for the repatriation of foreign earnings, in a bid to bolster the yen, Daisuke Karakama, chief market economist at \uc2 Mizuho Bank\uc2  , says in a research note. Japanese companies accumulated about Y37.6 trillions of retained earnings at their overseas subsidiaries, according to a government survey. A policy of encouraging Japanese companies repatriate earnings to aid domestic demand shouldn't invite criticism from other countries, whereas a market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the government could cause political issues, Karakama says. USD/JPY is at 143.57, near its 24-year highs. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 0259 GMT - Malaysia has to undertake aggressive fiscal consolidation if the government intends to reduce its fiscal deficit to 3.5% of GDP in 2025 from 6.0% projected in 2022, ANZ Research economists Debalika Sarkar and Sanjay Mathur say in a note. Tax reforms neglected during the pandemic should have a renewed focus in the 2023 budget to boost the government's revenue, they say. It is crucial that the government implement its medium-term revenue strategy as soon as possible to support its fiscal-consolidation goals, especially since Malaysia's revenue as a share of GDP has been declining, they note. Subsidy rationalization works better but it might push inflation higher, they add. ANZ maintains its full-year fiscal deficit forecast for Malaysia at 6.2% of GDP. (yingxian.wong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0228 GMT - \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  sees further scope for JPY weakness in the near term, given its economists' latest upward revisions to the Fed's rate-increase path and its rates strategists foreseeing upside risks to \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields, says analyst Karen Reichgott Fishman in a research report. \uc2 GS\uc2  's standard \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  model suggests that if the 10-year Treasury yield rises to 4.5%, USD/JPY could rally by another 7% to nearly 155, all else being equal, the analyst says. While likelihood of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks low, escalation in verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  together with reported "rate checks" should raise the odds priced into markets, the analyst adds. \uc2 GS\uc2  remains comfortable with its 3-month USD/JPY forecast of 145.00. USD/JPY is up 0.1% at 143.32. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0139 GMT - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are consolidating against USD after sharp movements during the past two days caused by hotter-than-expected U.S. inflation and media reports of \uc2 BOJ\uc2  rate check in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Factors including better economic fundamentals in Asia are helping to smooth volatility in the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for now, say ING economists in a morning commentary. While reports of a \uc2 BOJ\uc2  rate check has resulted in JPY making some decent gains, few expect this JPY strength to persist unless there's some real change in \uc2 BOJ\uc2  's underlying policy stance, the economists add. USD/JPY edges 0.1% lower to 143.01 while USD/KRW rises 0.2% to 1,394.79 and USD/SGD is little changed at 1.4059. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 15, 2022 03:13 ET (07:13 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220915ei9f000jy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
