\par\par \b \uc2 Nikkei Rises 0.2%, Led by Railway, Airline Stocks -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,412 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2022\par 08:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0634 GMT - Japanese stocks ended higher, led by gains in railway and airline stocks, on hopes of easing pandemic-related border restrictions. \uc2 East Japan Railway\uc2  and \uc2 Japan Airlines\uc2  climbed 1.9% each. The Nikkei Stock Average rose 0.2% to 27875.91. USD/JPY was at 143.64, compared with 143.17 as of Wednesday 5 p.m. ET. U.S. economic data are in focus, including retail sales and weekly jobless claims due later in the day. The yield on the 10-year Japanese government bond was flat at 0.250%. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 0628 GMT - China's economic activity likely showed signs of improvement in August with faster growth in industrial output and retail sales, according to 15 economists polled by The Wall Street Journal. Industrial output likely rose 4% on year in August, up slightly from the 3.8% increase in July. Retail sales likely increased 4% from a year earlier in August, up from 2.7% in July. Growth in fixed-asset investment may have slowed slightly to 5.5% on year in the January-August period, compared with a 5.7% increase in the first seven months. The National Bureau of Statistics is due to release the data on Friday. (singaporeeditors@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0625 GMT - German Bunds are expected to remain better supported after 10-year yields have repeatedly run into resistance near 1.8%, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  's rates strategist Rainer Guntermann and head of interest rates strategy Michael Leister write in a note. "Bunds remain vulnerable as terminal rate expectations are edging higher and risk sentiment stabilises, but 10y yields should continue to find support at the year-to-date highs near 1.8%," the strategists say. Markets' expectations of \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  terminal rates have risen to above 2.5% after last week's monetary policy meeting and they look to stabilize around this level, according to Refinitiv data. The 10-year Bund yield is trading almost 4 basis points higher at 1.739%, according to \uc2 Tradeweb\uc2  . (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0614 GMT - Greece lines up for scheduled rating reviews by \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Investors Service and DBRS on Friday, and the former might upgrade Greece, say \uc2 Citi\uc2  's rates strategists in a note. "Tomorrow's rating reviews of Greece by \uc2 Moody's\uc2  and DBRS could be significant for GGBs [Greek government bonds]," \uc2 Citi\uc2  's strategists say. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  rating of Greece at 'Ba3' with stable outlook is below that of \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  , S&P Global Ratings and DBRS, they say. "Net, we see scope for a catchup by \uc2 Moody's\uc2  via a one-notch upgrade to Ba2," \uc2 Citi\uc2  's strategists say. Meanwhile, they expect no change by DBRS which upgraded Greece to 'BB' (high) in March, the highest assessment among the four main agencies, the strategists say. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0551 GMT - USD/SGD is steady in the afternoon Asian session, supported by prospects of a large Fed rate hike later this month. Fed fund futures still suggest a 26% probability of a 100bp increase, say Maybank analysts in a research report. The market focus remains on USD Index's upside, buoyed by the possibility of a large rate increase and by lingering fragile sentiment, the analysts say. On daily chart, USD/SGD's bullish momentum has largely moderated, while its relative strength index isn't showing a clear bias, the analysts add, pegging resistance at 1.4100 and support at 1.3930. USD/SGD is little changed at 1.4064. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0532 GMT - The Japanese government may be able to use its version of U.S. Homeland Investment Act, which provided a tax holiday for the repatriation of foreign earnings, in a bid to bolster the yen, Daisuke Karakama, chief market economist at \uc2 Mizuho Bank\uc2  , says in a research note. Japanese companies accumulated about Y37.6 trillions of retained earnings at their overseas subsidiaries, according to a government survey. A policy of encouraging Japanese companies repatriate earnings to aid domestic demand shouldn't invite criticism from other countries, whereas a market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the government could cause political issues, Karakama says. USD/JPY is at 143.57, near its 24-year highs. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com; @kosakunarioka)\par \par \uc2 0259 GMT - Malaysia has to undertake aggressive fiscal consolidation if the government intends to reduce its fiscal deficit to 3.5% of GDP in 2025 from 6.0% projected in 2022, ANZ Research economists Debalika Sarkar and Sanjay Mathur say in a note. Tax reforms neglected during the pandemic should have a renewed focus in the 2023 budget to boost the government's revenue, they say. It is crucial that the government implement its medium-term revenue strategy as soon as possible to support its fiscal-consolidation goals, especially since Malaysia's revenue as a share of GDP has been declining, they note. Subsidy rationalization works better but it might push inflation higher, they add. ANZ maintains its full-year fiscal deficit forecast for Malaysia at 6.2% of GDP. (yingxian.wong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0228 GMT - \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  sees further scope for JPY weakness in the near term, given its economists' latest upward revisions to the Fed's rate-increase path and its rates strategists foreseeing upside risks to \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields, says analyst Karen Reichgott Fishman in a research report. \uc2 GS\uc2  's standard \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  model suggests that if the 10-year Treasury yield rises to 4.5%, USD/JPY could rally by another 7% to nearly 155, all else being equal, the analyst says. While likelihood of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks low, escalation in verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  together with reported "rate checks" should raise the odds priced into markets, the analyst adds. \uc2 GS\uc2  remains comfortable with its 3-month USD/JPY forecast of 145.00. USD/JPY is up 0.1% at 143.32. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0139 GMT - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are consolidating against USD after sharp movements during the past two days caused by hotter-than-expected U.S. inflation and media reports of \uc2 BOJ\uc2  rate check in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Factors including better economic fundamentals in Asia are helping to smooth volatility in the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for now, say ING economists in a morning commentary. While reports of a \uc2 BOJ\uc2  rate check has resulted in JPY making some decent gains, few expect this JPY strength to persist unless there's some real change in \uc2 BOJ\uc2  's underlying policy stance, the economists add. USD/JPY edges 0.1% lower to 143.01 while USD/KRW rises 0.2% to 1,394.79 and USD/SGD is little changed at 1.4059. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0033 GMT - South Korea's benchmark Kospi is flat at 2410.66 after opening higher amid choppy trade. Korean won movements will likely be closely watched after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a 13-year low versus the U.S. dollar, amid concerns that the U.S. Fed will further raise interest rates following a hotter-than-expected inflation data. Electronics stocks are mixed, with \uc2 SK-Electronics\uc2  falling 0.5% and \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  up 0.4%. Amid tech stocks, \uc2 Kakao Corp\uc2  . is 0.6% higher and \uc2 Naver Corp\uc2  . is down 0.2%. (justina.lee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2344 GMT - Japanese stocks may be rangebound as uncertainty persists over the Fed's pace of tightening. Nikkei futures open up 60 points at 27720 on the SGX. USD/JPY is at 142.84, compared with 143.85 as of Wednesday's Tokyo stock market close. Investors are closely watching economic data and Japanese government officials' comments on the yen. The Nikkei Stock Average fell 2.8% to 27818.62 on Wednesday. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2040 GMT - The IPC stock index closes down 0.6% at 46745 points, with miner Grupo Mexico shares down 5.2% and retailer Walmex off 1.4%. Real estate investment trust Fibra Mty, which focuses on industrial property, raises 3.45B pesos ($173M) including overallotments in its fourth follow-on issuance, pricing the shares at MXN12.20. The shares edge up 0.3%. \uc2 Coca-Cola Femsa\uc2  shares rise 0.1%. The bottler accepted for repurchase around $300M in bonds due 2030 and 2043. The peso firms to 19.97 against the US dollar from 20.08 yesterday. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 15, 2022 02:34 ET (06:34 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220915ei9f000dg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
