\par\par \b \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  Expects Further Scope for Near-Term JPY Weakness -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,405 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2022\par 04:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2022, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 0228 GMT - \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  sees further scope for JPY weakness in the near term, given its economists' latest upward revisions to the Fed's rate-increase path and its rates strategists foreseeing upside risks to \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  yields, says analyst Karen Reichgott Fishman in a research report. \uc2 GS\uc2  's standard \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  model suggests that if the 10-year Treasury yield rises to 4.5%, USD/JPY could rally by another 7% to nearly 155, all else being equal, the analyst says. While likelihood of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  looks low, escalation in verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  together with reported "rate checks" should raise the odds priced into markets, the analyst adds. \uc2 GS\uc2  remains comfortable with its 3-month USD/JPY forecast of 145.00. USD/JPY is up 0.1% at 143.32. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0139 GMT - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are consolidating against USD after sharp movements during the past two days caused by hotter-than-expected U.S. inflation and media reports of \uc2 BOJ\uc2  rate check in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Factors including better economic fundamentals in Asia are helping to smooth volatility in the region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for now, say ING economists in a morning commentary. While reports of a \uc2 BOJ\uc2  rate check has resulted in JPY making some decent gains, few expect this JPY strength to persist unless there's some real change in \uc2 BOJ\uc2  's underlying policy stance, the economists add. USD/JPY edges 0.1% lower to 143.01 while USD/KRW rises 0.2% to 1,394.79 and USD/SGD is little changed at 1.4059. (ronnie.harui@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 0033 GMT - South Korea's benchmark Kospi is flat at 2410.66 after opening higher amid choppy trade. Korean won movements will likely be closely watched after the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a 13-year low versus the U.S. dollar, amid concerns that the U.S. Fed will further raise interest rates following a hotter-than-expected inflation data. Electronics stocks are mixed, with \uc2 SK-Electronics\uc2  falling 0.5% and \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  up 0.4%. Amid tech stocks, \uc2 Kakao Corp\uc2  . is 0.6% higher and \uc2 Naver Corp\uc2  . is down 0.2%. (justina.lee@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2344 GMT - Japanese stocks may be rangebound as uncertainty persists over the Fed's pace of tightening. Nikkei futures open up 60 points at 27720 on the SGX. USD/JPY is at 142.84, compared with 143.85 as of Wednesday's Tokyo stock market close. Investors are closely watching economic data and Japanese government officials' comments on the yen. The Nikkei Stock Average fell 2.8% to 27818.62 on Wednesday. (kosaku.narioka@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2040 GMT - The IPC stock index closes down 0.6% at 46745 points, with miner Grupo Mexico shares down 5.2% and retailer Walmex off 1.4%. Real estate investment trust Fibra Mty, which focuses on industrial property, raises 3.45B pesos ($173M) including overallotments in its fourth follow-on issuance, pricing the shares at MXN12.20. The shares edge up 0.3%. \uc2 Coca-Cola Femsa\uc2  shares rise 0.1%. The bottler accepted for repurchase around $300M in bonds due 2030 and 2043. The peso firms to 19.97 against the US dollar from 20.08 yesterday. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2031 GMT - The \uc2 Financial Accounting Standards Board\uc2  votes to propose a rule requiring businesses to start reporting on the assets they bring into certain joint ventures. US generally accepted accounting principles don't currently address how parties to a joint venture should recognize the contribution of some assets, such as buildings and equipment. Under the proposal, joint ventures would have to disclose their formation date, why they're being formed and their fair value at formation, which involves measuring liabilities and assets at their current value. They would also have to share the size of certain assets and liabilities that will be recognized by the joint venture. As proposed, the disclosures will apply to a small cohort of joint ventures that result in a stand-alone entity and will only affect new joint ventures. (jennifer.williams-alvarez@wsj.com; @jenkayw)\par \par \uc2 1956 GMT - The two-year Treasury yield rises 0.028 percentage point to 3.782% and the 10-year falls 0.011 pp to 3.411%, as markets brace for even tighter monetary policy that may drive the US economy into recession. After yesterday's surprisingly hot CPI numbers, today's August PPI reading was more in line with expectations. Weekly jobless claims are expected to accelerate to 225K tomorrow, from 222K in the previous week. Chatter about a possible 100-basis point hike next week remained strong today, but markets are still betting more heavily on a 75bp rate increase. (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 1805 GMT - The Fed is likely to make it clear that monetary policy will be restrictive for a prolonged period of time, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  economists say in a report ahead of next week's rate decision and quarterly outlook publication. They expect a 75 basis point increase, even after Tuesday's hot CPI data triggered some bets for a 100bps increase. \uc2 BofA\uc2  says the window for a soft landing is narrowing and the committee is likely to cut growth forecasts. "Participants are likely to mark down their expectations for growth this year, and carry forward some softness into 2023 on account of weaker momentum and expectations for a more restrictive monetary policy stance." (paulo.trevisani@wsj.com; @ptrevisani)\par \par \uc2 1756 GMT - \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2  President \uc2 Glen Hauenstein\uc2  says the company has yet to see "any cracks in our demand" despite looking for any signs of softness amid global economic jitters. At the Morgan Stanley Laguna Conference, Hauenstein says the company has seen a pickup in demand for business travel since Labor Day Weekend. He adds that leisure travel demand has remained strong as well even as the company enters what's historically been an off-peak season. He says Americans are still travelling to Europe, perhaps to take advantage of the relatively strong US dollar. (will.feuer@wsj.com; @WillFOIA) Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 This item was corrected at 3:07 p.m. ET to reflect that \uc2 Delta Air Lines\uc2  President \uc2 Glen Hauenstein\uc2  made comments at the Morgan Stanley Laguna Conference. An earlier version missattributed the statements to CEO \uc2 Ed Bastian\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 1637 GMT - Incoming data suggests the Fed's preferred inflation gauge, the personal consumption expenditure price index, will rise further on a year-over-year basis when it's next reported, says Omair Sharif, of Inflation Insights, in a research note. He says the core August PCE price index is on track to rise by 4.8% from the same month in 2021, up from the 4.6% year-over-year rise seen in July. Sharif adds, "it wouldn't be surprising," to see the year-over-year core PCE price index move up again to 5% when the September reading comes out. (michael.derby@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1636 GMT - If \uc2 Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to hit its 2% inflation target, it will likely have to tip the country into a recession, Desjardins economist \uc2 Royce Mendes\uc2  says in a research note. He adds that a monetary-policy induced recession became "our base-case forecast in early summer," and that recent data releases, and the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  's reaction to them, "have reinforced that view. "While some shops are still debating whether or not a recession is on the horizon, we're now wondering if it will only be a mild downturn." (glenn.johnson@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1631 GMT - \uc2 BRP Inc\uc2  ., maker of snowmobiles, ATVs and personal watercraft, is well-positioned to weather short-term volatility caused by macroeconomic uncertainty, \uc2 National Bank of Canada\uc2  's \uc2 Cameron Doerksen\uc2  says in a research note. The analyst thinks retail demand still looks solid, with 3Q retail sales up 20% year-over-year as supply chain pressures begin to slowly ease, allowing the company to boost output accordingly. "The risk of an economic slowdown may weigh on sentiment around \uc2 BRP\uc2  shares in the near-term," the analyst says, adding "[we] view the stock's current valuation as already reflecting the likelihood of a recession." Shares are up 10% at C$98.61. (adriano.marchese@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 14, 2022 22:28 ET (02:28 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020220915ei9f00041\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
