 suffered a setback Wed on fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  \u8226\'20\'22 That gave \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders an opportunity to buy USD/JPY at a cheaper level \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY bulls will likely charge at 145 again: chart bullish \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/JPY's correlation with USD/JPY high, 30/60-day logs are above +0.50 \u8226\'20\'22 LDP's Katayama: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't be that effective \u8226\'20\'22 LDP's Hagiuda urges $209 bln stimulus to combat infl, weak yen GBP/USD eyes 1.15 as shorts hope for big bonus from Fed \u8226\'20\'22 Cable drop towards 1.1500 influenced by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  steer from senior LDP official \u8226\'20\'22 Katayama: "Solo \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't be that effective" \u8226\'20\'22 BoJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk aided Wednesday's GBP/USD rise to 1.1589 \u8226\'20\'22 Dollar may add to recent gains if Fed raises rates by 100 bps next week \u8226\'20\'22 100 bps hike might depress cable through 1.1407 (last week's 37-year low) \u8226\'20\'22 UK's Kwarteng considering end to cap on banker bonuses AUD/USD pivots 0.6750 ahead of U.S. retail sales data \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD has traded 31 pip range thus far Thursday; 0.6740-0.6771 \u8226\'20\'22 0.6771 is also the high water-mark since Wednesday's one-week low of 0.6705 \u8226\'20\'22 Drop to 0.6705 followed Tuesday's hotter than expected U.S. inflation data \u8226\'20\'22 Raft of U.S. data due at 1230 GMT, including Aug retail sales; 0.0% MM f/c \u8226\'20\'22 Australian employment rose by 33,500 in August, in line with expectations \u8226\'20\'22 \uc2 RBA\uc2  under the microscope in policy review Fed, BoJ, SNB and \uc2 BoE\uc2  may generate \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  whirlwind \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates could be shaken and stirred by monetary policy decisions from a quartet of major central banks in less than 24 hours next week, starting with the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  on Wednesday at 1800 GMT. Thursday will then see announcements from the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 , \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  (0730 GMT) and \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  (1100 GMT). The dollar could add to its recent gains if the Fed raises rates by 100 basis points -- the perceived probability of which is currently 27%. The yen should benefit if the BoJ unexpectedly ditches its commitment to capping the 10-year Japanese government bond yield at 0.25%, with scope for the franc to strengthen if the SNB lifts rates by 100 bps. Money markets currently suggest there is a 51% chance of the SNB hiking by a full percentage point. The \uc2 BoE\uc2  will impact the pound by deciding whether to raise rates by 75 bps, the perceived probability of which is currently 56%. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3RNTU4C"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  Euro could fall another 5% before the year is out At its lowest point this year the euro had fallen over 20% from its 1.2349 January 2021 high against the dollar and despite good bidding interest in and around parity, there is risk of another 5% decline, taking EUR/USD below 0.9500 before year-end. There is a danger the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is placing too much emphasis on the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hawkish stance and not enough attention to the economic outlook for the euro zone. \uc2 ECB\uc2  rate hikes are not a safety net but more a slowing mechanism for EUR/USD depreciation. The euro zone economy remains at risk to high energy prices and despite a moderation in gas and energy costs the damage to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  bloc's manufacturing-industrial sector could be long lasting and possibly game changing. There are signs that inflation might peak in the next month or two and this plus less pessimism over growth could put the euro in a better light. However, the energy crisis is not over and further pain for Europe is likely. This and the dollar's safe-haven role and a more resilient U.S. economy will weigh on EUR/USD. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tmsnrt.rs/3dbM2Lf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-18T11:17:45.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220915ei9f01yoh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
