EZ Jul Trade Balance E30.8 bln deficit prev, NSA E24.6 bln deficit prev \u8226\'20\'22 09:00 EZ Q2 Labour Costs YY 3.2% prev; Wages 2.7% prev Looking Ahead - Events, Auctions, Other Releases (GMT) \u8226\'20\'22 09:15 ECB De Guindos speaks at Lisbon conference/12:00 Portugal CB Centeno speaks \u8226\'20\'22 09:30 ECB McCaul at Frankfurt OMFIF-SPI symposium Commentary and Analysis USD/JPY showing some buoyancy, JPY crosses steady to up \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY sees small bounce in Asia after being hit hard yesterday \u8226\'20\'22 Tokyo players taking BoJ rate checks yesterday in stride \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY 142.80 to 143.49 EBS in Asia, tracks away from 142.56 low o/n \u8226\'20\'22 Market on quiet side however, flows fewer, caution still prevails \u8226\'20\'22 Japanese importers good buyers today, take advantage of fall yesterday \u8226\'20\'22 Some spec buying noted too, exporters awaiting higher levels \u8226\'20\'22 No large option expiries nearby today, tomorrow schedule light too \u8226\'20\'22 US yields firm, supportive, Treasury 2s @3.801%, 10s @3.423% \u8226\'20\'22 Asia more risk on, Nikkei bounces, +0.5% @27,946, E-Minis +0.2% @3972 \u8226\'20\'22 JPY cross steady to up, AUD/JPY up leg, 96.38 to 97.00 \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/JPY 142.60-143.00, steadies after fall, GBP/JPY 164.73-165.43 \u8226\'20\'22 CAD/JPY 108.47-98, MXN/JPY 7.1498-7.1805, still best of lot EUR/USD-Consolidates in narrow range in Asia as markets settle \u8226\'20\'22 EUR/USD opened +0.10% at 0.9978 after it was sidelined by USD/JPY action \u8226\'20\'22 It edged up to 0.9984 in early Asia as most risk assets moved higher \u8226\'20\'22 USD/JPY reversed higher late morning and EUR/USD drifted down to 0.9968 \u8226\'20\'22 Heading into the afternoon EUR/USD was trading around 0.9970 \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance is at the 10-day MA at 0.9988 and 21-day MA at 0.9992 \u8226\'20\'22 A clear break above 1.0000 targets the 55-day MA at 1.0118 \u8226\'20\'22 Support is at the Sep 8 low at 0.9931 and trend low at 0.9864 \u8226\'20\'22 US data later today may cause some volatility if US yields react \u8226\'20\'22 US retail sales, IP, Philly Fed, import and export prices and jobless claims on tap AUD/USD-Attains bid tone as risk assets settle and edge higher \u8226\'20\'22 AUD/USD opened +0.21% at 0.6747 after holding above 0.6700 \u8226\'20\'22 After dipping to 0.6740 AUD/USD tracked higher as markets settled \u8226\'20\'22 It traded up to 0.6771 at one stage when Asian equities edged higher \u8226\'20\'22 Aus jobs data came in close to expectations and had minimal impact \u8226\'20\'22 Heading into the afternoon AUD/USD settled around 0.6755/60 \u8226\'20\'22 Resistance is at the 10-day MA at 0.6783 and 21-day MA at 0.6837 \u8226\'20\'22 Bids are tpped ahead of 0.6700 with support at 0.6680/0.6700 \u8226\'20\'22 Bias is for lower while it holds below the 21-day MA GBP/USD-Tuesday's topside failure leaves 1.1400 vulnerable \u8226\'20\'22 -0.15% in a 1.1525-1.1549 range, quiet with a flurry late morning on D3 \u8226\'20\'22 UK fin min Kwarteng seeks to scrap curbs on banker bonuses - FT \u8226\'20\'22 New UK government appears to have abandoned the 'levelling up' strategy \u8226\'20\'22 Techs: daily momentum studies conflict, 21 day Bollinger bands contract \u8226\'20\'22 10 & 21 day moving averages slip, bullish 5 & 10 DMA cross - no strong bias \u8226\'20\'22 Tuesday's failure at the 21 day moving average, now at 1.1651 was bearish \u8226\'20\'22 Targets a test of key 1.1407 support, being the September and 2022 low \u8226\'20\'22 Close above 1.1651 falling 21 day moving average would be a bullish signal \uc2 BOJ\uc2  unmoving on monetary policy despite CPI, JPY \uc2 The Bank of Japan\uc2 's Sept 21-22 policy meeting is being touted by some players as potentially 'live', based on inflation and recent USD/JPY moves. Whatever the arguments for tweaks to monetary policy, the consensus in Tokyo remains for no major changes before Governor \uc2 Haruhiko Kuroda\uc2 's term expires in April 2023. Inflation has risen in Japan, even topping the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's 2% target. That said, the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  view is that inflation will fall back, especially with key energy prices retreating after recent surges. Inflation will undoubtedly plateau at a higher level however, which the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  hopes will be around 2%. As to USD/JPY, Kuroda is still seen as nonchalant over JPY weakness. It aids in the \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's effort to finally put deflationary pressures to rest and push inflation to target. The moves also reflect 'fundamentals' though the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  would never admit this publicly. A stronger U.S. economy, hawkish \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  and higher Treasury yields are powerful fundamental drivers of USD/JPY strength, and most believe the USD will remain broadly strong going forward. The \uc2 BOJ\uc2 's 0.25% cap on 10-year JGB yields is also likely to remain in place. Renewed upward pressure on Japanese yields has emerged with U.S. and major economy yields recently much higher. While the cap will likely remain in place for now, upward adjustments, especially into 2023, should not be ruled out. As monetary policy kicks in, China pulls other levers China may pull more levers to get the economy firing on all cylinders again, as recent monetary easing begins to take effect, and yuan depreciation pressure prevents further rate cuts. One big trigger lies in the property sector. \uc2 The People's Bank of China\uc2  kept its medium-term lending facility (MLF) rate unchanged Thursday, as widely expected, while partially rolling over maturing loans. The effect of August's easing is slowly filtering into the economy, and state banks separately announced deposit rate cuts. Considering officials' firm stance on being prudent and avoiding "flood-like" economic stimulus, further broad interest rate cuts appear less likely. But more targeted measures, such as Wednesday's extension of tax relief for small firms, may be ahead. One powerful tool Beijing has been reluctant to deploy is the relaxing of property market curbs. Having staved off contagion effects from struggling debt-ridden behemoths, the market remains depressed, but help may be at hand. China's new home prices slipped 0.9% annually in July; data for August is due Friday. Reports of easing downpayment and price rules in various cities across China have boosted real estate indices. More property stimulus measures may be in the pipeline, and the yuan, currently propped up by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is not pricing that in.\par \par \uc2 To read our latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and analysis services, click on\par \par \uc2 Released: 2022-9-18T05:27:12.000Z \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020220915ei9f00om5\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 (foreign exchange or fx or forex or currenc*) and (interven* or operation*) and (japan near10 interven*) and (rst=trtw or rst=tprw or rst=tdjw) and (central bank or ministry of finance or treasury ministry or monetary authority)\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9072\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Datum\cell